      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>14,i-</head>
        <p>14,i-</p>
        <p>GET two words out of "enougli " meaning" not enough." Answer: One hug. MONEY is not essential to happiness, but happy peopre usually have enough. " WILT you love me when I'm old?" she asked. "Certainly," he replied promptly, "if you will love me when I am bald." SOME men are só fond of work that they will neglect their business for hours to watch a gang of men paving the street. "Miss (Imo-L=ON is pretty plain, isn't she? " "Yes, she is pretty plain, but her younger sister is pretty pretty.' "How are you getting on with your motor- ear?" "I can start it all righe but, I can't stop unless I run into something." No STINT.—The Talkative Man: "I always weigh my words." The Caustic Maid: "Do you? You seem to give pretty good measure." i " THE man n the moon must be awfully old," raid little Johnny. " Why ?" queried his father. "Because he has a birthday every month." CRISISONBEAK : "Longfellow said that in this world a man must either be anvil or hammer." Mrs. Crimsonbeak : "Oh, I don't know I , How about the bellows?" INFORMED.—" Father, what is a lineal descen- dant?" "A lineal descendant is a person who has to fall back on some praiseworthy ancestor for his own importance." "So you have named your girl twin Henrietta ?" "Yes, but I changed it a little. It was such a long name that I cut it in two, and called the boy Henry and the girl Etta." MRS. LIGHTLY: "Why, Harry, that great big horse has been drinking at that trough for five minutes." Mr. Lightly: "Well, don't you see he is a draught. horse ? " SUDGES : "Now that you are living in the country, I suppose you have fresh milk every day?" Dashaway : "We didn't this morning.' Sububs : "Why not?" Dashaway "The train from town was two hours late." MismEss : "Bridget, I told you I wanted all the eggs for breakfast soft, and several were quite hard.' Bridget: "They were all in to boil the same length of time, only some of them felt the heat more than the others.' FRIEND: "What! A learned man like you with a dictionary on your desk ? " Pompous: "Oh, I never look at it. It's for the benefit of my friends who want to talk to me. Have a look at it." " FOOTPAD: "Your money or your life ! " Vi3tim : "Here is my money; but I wish you would give me a recipe, so the next highwayman I meet won't half kill me for refusing to "hand over'?!" FRED: "What do you mean by the entho rrass- anent of riches?" Cosbwigger : "Well, my WI!, I once heard of a woman who had a Ll,OOO set of dishes, and she was so afraid of the servants breaking them that she washed them herself." MISTRS: "Did you tell the lady I was out?" Servant Girl: "Yes, ma'am." Mistress: "Did she seem to have any doubts about it?" Ser- vant Girl: "No, ma'am; she said she knew you wasn't !" FROTECrED.—Tenor (in Germany): "When I practice I sing only songs composed by the Emperor." Bass "Ah ?" Tenor: " Yes, and then the neighbours may not kill me without being guilty of lese majeste." FIRST Hs: "What a ridiculously giddy ores., ture that young Miss Dorking is ! " Second Hen: "Oh, she's young yet. Wait till she has known the sorrow of shall for three weeks on a china egg and two door 'knobs. She'll sober down then."</p>
        <p>LIT'rLE JOHNNY: " W'en Miss Nexdor got married her mother threw an old slipper after her. What was that for?" Little Ethel: "Oh, they always do that. That means that her mamma • isn't never goin' to spank her any more." "I DoN'T like your heart action," the doctor said, applying•thestethosc?pe again. "You have had some trouble with angrnapectoris." " Yoa're partly right, doctor," sheepishly answered the young man. " Ouly-that•sin't,ber-name."</p>
        <p>Taman is an incessant war -of fun between Chicago and New York. Chicago, girls are sup- posed to have big feet, as witness the following joke from a New York paper: Gerald: "You'll feel a pain in your foot in about -half-an-hour.' Geraldine: "R%sa...do you mean.?" Gerald: "I just stepped on your-toe." His ertnor Wonar.—" Did the investigation annoy you?" "Not much," answered the secretary of the company, who had just been in the witness box. "But it puzzled me a little now and then to decide whether I should say decline to answer or 'I don't remember.'" WHEN.' Dom Pedro ass shown one of our greatest mechanical inventions, his attention was particu- larly directed to a fiy.wheel. He was told that it performed 5,000 revolutions per minute. " Ah," he replied, "I admit that beats our South Ameri- can Republics." "Or course, you saw all the interesting places of the French capital, as all tourists do. But did you mingle with the people there, and get really acquainted with the city? Did you see Paris proper" "No. To tell the truth, I didn't think Paris ever was that way." "I GOT my start in life by picking up a pin. I had been refused employment by a merchant, and on my way out I saw a pin, and " "I know; I've heard of that boy so often ! The merchant was impressed with your carefulness, and called you back and made you head of the firm !" I saw the pin and picked it up, and sold it for a hundred pounds. It was a diamond pin." QUITE A NOTHERTHING.---.' The country," affirmed a merchant the other day, "is going to the dogs. I'm as certain of it as lam of anything." "By the way," suggested a friend, "what'll you sell the site at the corner of the street for?" "That lot," said the merchant quickly, "is not for sale, I shall retain it. In less than ten years it will bring more than double what it would sell for to- day." WHO is thef blonde lady?" asked the timid young man. "Widow," was the answer. "Whore is she from?" "St. Petersburg." "And that young woman who is just coming off the balcony, with the red shawl over her shoulders ? " "She's from South Africa—Johannesburg." "And that lady with the classic profile and the stately car- riage—where is she from?" "I don't know. From her demeanour to me I should say loeberg." "IN the court of a certain Scottish burgh, a man was charged with the theft of a pig. The worthy Bailie, in sentencing the prisoner, remarked that pig-stealing in the burgh had lately been too rife, and finished his peroration thus :—"And unless I make an example of you it's very certain none of us will be safe." THE bright boy of fiction is playing with his Noah's ark. "What are these two extra chips of wood?" asked the bright boy's father. It is necessary for the bright boy of fiction to have a father, you know; there has to be somebody to draw him out. "Them," replied the bright boy, without hesitation, "is the microbes ! " Of course, if we think a minute, we perceive that there must have been a pair of microbes in the ark. HE had just reached the peroration of his tribute to the modern athletic girl when the little fellow in the corner interrupted him. "Pardon me," said the little fellow, " but may I ask if you married one of these modern girls ? " "Why no, "I thought not," interrupted the little fellow, as he resumed his seat, and those sitting near him were quick to note that he carried one arm in a sling, and had a large bump on the top of his head. CLERGYMAN (to youthful rustic at last examina.- tion before confirmatiou) "My boy, what is the meaning of the word repentant ? " Boy: "Please, sir, I don't know." Clergyman: "If I stole a loaf of bread, what should I be ? " Boy: "Please, sir, locked up." Clergyman "Well, but if I was, shouldn't Ibe sorry afterwards ?" Boy: "Yes, sir." Clergyman: "Ah, now you see my meaning. Why should I feel sorry ? " Boy "Please, sir, 'cos you was ketched." " .NELLIE," spoke the youth, with an air of confidence, "only say the word and you and I will sail together on the rough sea of life, bravely buffeting the waves, trimming our sails to meet the adverse winds that— "Go, Geoffrey, go," interrupted the maiden. "The man who takes me on that journey must offer me a first-elass passage on a steamer." Whereupon Geoffrey sailed out of her presence with a decided list to port.</p>
        <p>Warrington P S.A Society. The weekly meeting of tt.is eociety in the Co- operative Ball. Sankes-streer, cn Sunday was largely attended. The chairimn was Mr Edward Taylor. Mr Crreenhough gave two solos "The Moly City "and "'1 he Sailor'a Gra”e," for which he was accorded abr arty reception. The lesson was read 1)3 Mr W Barnes. Mr James Owen gave an instructive address on "3 he growth of the P.S.A roughout the country, and tte work that is being done at therneetings."</p>
