        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>At last we can joyfully.. clothe ourselves in the dainty muslins, silks and laces which are our right at this time of the year; the long continuance of cold and wet weather till far on in June having rendered, such "airy-fairy" garments quite out of the question. The fact of this delay in the eagerly desired summer days of sunshine and cloudless skies makes us appreciate them when they do come more thoroughly, and the light blouses and dress and gay floral head gear appear more fascinating than ever to our eyes. Our laundry bills how- ever, have {a rather unpleasant way of expanding to unpleasant proportions, as muslins and all such diaphonous 'materials must be spotlessly clean and fresh looking to achieve success. Nothing is more unsightly than a soiled, crushed and crumpled muslin, which seems to appeal in a depressed manner to all beholders as though to beg them to remember that it is not to blame for willingly ruining the appearance of the wearer. The prettiest girl would lose all her charm in a draggled muslin. Still, much can be done to make a delicate dress wear longer if a little extra trouble is not grudged. A muslin dress should be always carefully folded and will generally be much improved if the creases (which are unavoidable however careful one may be) are ironed out 'before putting the gown away and any necessary stitches put in.</p>
        <p>, A MUSLIN TOILETTE. A torn lace frill or edging gives a very untidy look to a toilette. The pretty gown sketched is made of white spotted muslin, very simple in style with a ribbon sash finishing the waist. The old-fashioned sash has again made its ap- pearance and does much to take away from the unfinished look a plain skirt sometimes presents behind. It is worn in a number of ways and looks very picturesque and becoming on a slender figure. A pretty manner of</p>
        <p>arranging it is to do away- witi . the bunchy /bow and have the ends come from below the broad band, which should come higher at the back of the figure than the front, where it must come to a narrow point in the centre. If there is a bow at all it must be more of a knot than anything else. Another manner of wearing the sash is to fasten it at the left side in loops and a knot with long ends depending down the skirt. The old style of a twisted girdle in gold or silver is most effective and looks specially well with the Empire or Princesse dress. The lace sash has also many admirers, but is more difficult to arrange than that of ribbon. Of course all those who are inclined towards enbonpoint should avoid all sash effects and wear only the simplest and plainest band round the waist, or otherwise the dimensions will apparently be increased.</p>
        <p>The charming hat of picturesque mode sketched was designed for wear at a very smart function. It is made of fine cream chip lined with drawn cream chiffon. The trimming consists of a few twists of chiffon and some lovely ostrich plumes which shade off at the tips to an exquisite pink. It was to accompany a lace dress made over a pale pink underdress, the whole toilette being completed by a cream satin sunshade lined with drawn pink chiffon. Ostrich feathers are much in vogue this season as a trimming on all sorts of millinery and though</p>
        <p>:4 II"‘ . 44 \•,..,,r, ..,.....-,1 ..- ir 1 4 : ..-...,• .:....,., :., •1 I/ ;,./ • - . , . •: i . • i 1 &amp;Ado 44-16 46...... .• t ....,..„.... ..,.. ~, -- --</p>
