      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LOVELY HAT.</head>
        <p>A LOVELY HAT.</p>
        <p>the expense incurred in the purchasing of them may seem at first rather great to the economi- cally minded, they will Le found cheaper than flowers or ribbon in the long run if care is taken of them. It costs very little to them cleaned or re-curled and after this process they emerge as good as new. Panama hats are now made in different colours, such as blue or mauve, and are trimmed with scarves of white silk beauti- fully embroidered in cream, or in bunches or sprays of natural coloured flowers to match the colour of the straw. Coarse traw hats look picturesque when simply trimmed by a swathing, and loose tulle or chiffon strings tied in large bows either under. the chin or more generally at the side, with wide doting ends. Perhaps the most popular head gear of the moment is the Marquise, of a 'elightly. larger build than formerly. It is an eminently con- venient hat being at once useful yet•ereart, and s&amp;ted to everyday wear. The fascinati- 7, evening blouse depicted is at once useful ma fashionable. It is made of black spotted net over black satin and is trimmed with some germ lace. The sleeves are of the wide Turkish style, transparent of course, finishing at the wrists in lace bands. A whole toilette Iterial and style would</p>
        <p>in thiz mat : 7;.544 lIIIIK a•io ' '...','''''' ''• • 1 i •• . . ,f . r•I .-I I ti •), ISsi• if ' -4.- .. • IVA , ~...,,.,, ''':- (;1 • -:: -_l'l.:''',.;:'/ . , . • I . . 7.%</p>
