      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>AN EVENING BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>AN EVENING BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>look very smart and prove very useful as black can be worn on any occasion. Indeed every girl should possess a black voile or cashmere gown for afternoon wear, and a black net or lace one the for evening. A black skirt without a bodice is also very useful as one can wear a variety of blouses with it. The blouse during summer is always at its prime and is highly esteemed for there is a comfortable easy feeling about it when in wear which is most alluring et a time when comfort is the first consideration. ITho biome differs very little now-a-days from the. bodice when it appears in its most elaborate foam, though 029 gamin, blouge,..is kept Ili,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>No colour is perhaps more becoming to the Queen than heliotrope, and this was the use selected for the Gimple and elegant toilette in which Her Majesty ar- peered at the Imperial Coronation Bazaar, otganiEed on reboil of the Hospital for Sick Children, in Great Ormonde-street. The big shower bouquet of heliotrope orchids presented to the Queen, matched perfectly, and among the many fair women gathered together en thin necavion, surely none looked fairer or sweeter than our Queen. Not one of the thirty-ex stalls was overlooked. by Her Majesty, who bought largely. There seemed to be but one crumpled roseleaf in connection with this , very briliiant bazaar, and ttat was, the decision at the ast moment that no raffles were to be allowed.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Manchester is one of thoee people—of whnm there are a good many—who do not approve of raffles at bazaars, but it is much to be feared that the substitute which his lordship eugeests will not have a very enduring popularity. The bishop's idea is, that twenty people should combine to purchase au article,and that they should decide by votes who should become the possessor of it. In some cases that would mean that the men who had subeeribed would make a col;ective preeent to some woman who bad been working hard for the bazaar, That would be a very good, and nice thing to do, but people would not go on subscribing in suce circumstances to an indefinite extent. In other cases, the plan might work very vell, at first, because there are plenty of people who imagine that they are popular The process of undeceiving them would not be,however, a pleasant one, and when it had been completed they might not be desirous of repeating the experiment. It is uafortunately impossible to disassociate sight- seeing from accidents, and a very pathetic one has hap- pened in the West End of London just before the Queen passed on her way to open the Coronation Bazaar. Many people bad gathered along the route to see Her Majesty drive by, when a heavy flag rope dislodged some coping stones on a church portico, which fell on the sightseers underneath. Several were seriously injured and Miss Straitly, a Canadian, who had come over with some friends for the Coronation, was instantly killed. She was the only one of her party who was not at the Bazaar, and her friends did not know of her death until later in the day. The circumstances recall those of the only serious accident which was said to have had any association with the Coronation of Queen Victoria. In honour of that event, a Captein Currie, accompanied by a Mrs Graham, made a balloon ascent from Hyde Park but as 1 there was no wind, they remairred floating over Lone err. In descending, the balloon struck the coping stones of a high building, and the falling meterial caused fatal in- juries to a pedestrian who happened to be underneath. v The season since Lent, has not been remarkable for fashionable weddings, but two or three (bat will attract a good deal cf notice are taking place this month. There is theemarriage of Earl Beauchamp with Lady Lettice Grosvenor, the youngest sister of the Duke of West- minster, the wedding being celebrated at Eaton, and on the Stle of this month, Miss Gully, the Speaker's daughter, is to be married. Mr Gully has a host of Parliamentary friends, and his daughter's wedding will be very numerously attended. Tee Dowager Countess De La Warr was to have been marrie3 early this month. She put off her wedding with the Chaplain of the Savoy until after the I Coronation, so that she could attend as a Peeress,—a right she forfeits by marriage with a Commoner—and consequently she found herself in something more than a dilemma, when the Coronation was definitely post- poned. The modern honeymoon scarcely lasts as many days salt used to weeks. Time was whee the newly married couple disappeared for a long interval. Nowadays no one ever thinks of doing any such thing as often as not, before all the papers which describe the ceremony, are out, one reads that the newly wedded pair were at the theatre, or some other social entertainment. A very neceeeary item in a woman's bed or dressing room is a full length swinging mirror in which ills possible to obtain a complete view of the toilette. In thr se days the best wardrobes boast little in the way of a really useful mirror, and the narrow slips of glass in- serted into the cheaper varieties are quite useless. Such an addition as suggested would do away with , many of the exaggerations and absurdities one meets with daily. The woman with the encr- mous hat would perchance discover that something smaller and neater in the way of headgear would prove more becoming and more in proportion with her height and build ; that less exaggerated ruffle&amp; or gowns, would be preferable'; that something or other either in style, or as regards colour or fabrics was want- ing to blend her into " one harmonious whole." We are often accused of a too great love for the mirror, but, as a matter of fact the majority of us would do well, to more frequently seek its advice and help. A full length mirror, in which ills possible to see ourselves as others see us. is en absolute necessity to the woman who would be artistically and well dressed. The craze for jewelled lace appears to be diminishing. Women possessed of valuable family laces, have appar- ently begun to recognise that the process in no way adds to the beauty of the fabric, that the said fabric is fre- quently ruined by being sewn over with gems, and moreover that the effect is frequently vulgar •to a degree. The most worn lace at the monsene is Alencon. In imitation, this is preferable to many other laces, and is being worn by quite dressy women. Many women who possess throats, not of the milky- white variety, or those on which time has alas left its mark, discard their pearls as being impossible wear. And rightly BO if they contemplate placing the pearls next the skin. But another course is possible to all but the hopelessly scraggy woman, namely that of arrang- ing a slightly boned collar of old lace, about the throat, upon which the pearls can rest. The fashion of wear- ing quantities of jewellery at all hours of the day con- tinues, and the fashitnable woman still crowds on as many rings as her fingers can possibly hold. A dust cloak is a very desirable addition to the ward- robe of every woman, and especially to those women who have little to spend upon dress, for dust ruins clothes. Tossore silk is unquentionatly the best material for the purpose, and with the help of a good pattern and machine, a dainty, eimple dust cloak can soon be run up: Lengths of tussore silk of the desired quantity (about 15 to 17 oards) are frequently to be had at sales, at exceedingly low figures and the only ornamentation these cloaks require is a trimming of coarse lace, threaded through with black silk baby ribbon.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp'a Mercantile Gazette," the number of New Bankruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending July 12th, was 79. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 69, elacwing an increase of 10, being a net decrease in 1902, to date, of 73.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WI L MAY</head>
        <p>WI L MAY</p>
        <p>LALL RIGHTS RESIERVISIX]</p>
        <p>BARRY PAIN.</p>
        <p>PART m. CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Enterland desired me to say, sir, that she would be down directly," said Carter, as he opened the drawing-room door. Carter and Mrs. Blayd had both been taken into my sister's service—Carter' of course as butler, and Mrs. Blayd as maid to Wilmay, housekeeper, occasional secretary, and several other things to --ny sister. She had no fixed position, and my sister said that she was a treasure.</p>
        <p>I waited a few minutes in the drawing-room. It was the usual fashionable mixture of things ancient and modern, and just as much too full and too feminine as every other drawing.room in the dis- trict. Then Bertha entered looking mysterious and pleased, which she was, and much younger than thirty-five, which she was not. She shook me warmly by the hand, kissed me, and exclaimed, "Edward, we've got a surprise for you." " Yes ? " I said. "Then, of course, you needn't trouble to apologise." "Why, I've hardly kept you waiting a second. The fact is that it didn't come until the last moment—oh, I've nearly let the secret out." "Let it quite out. You won't be happy until you've done it. Besides, I hate surprises. They're all alike—something goes pop and hurts you, and then everybody else laugl*. I think they're shock- ingly vulgar myself." "But this surprise isn't at all like that. It's beautiful."</p>
        <p>"Well, how's Wihnay ? And where's Wil- may ? " " Wilmay's very well indeed, and quite charmed with your present, and you won't see her just yet —that's part of the secret." " You've hidden her behind a curtain or a screen or somewhere. And she'll jump out and scream, and the ghastly part of it is that you'll both of you think it funny. When is it going to happen ?" Carter announced that dinner was served. "It's not going to happen at all, and Wilmays much too old to care about such stupid childish games. Take me into the dining-room, and you shall behold her."</p>
        <p>In the dining-room Wilmay was standing at one end of the table. She was a transformed Wilmay. Hitherto, she had worn her hair down; now her hair was up and done in the latest and correctest fashion. Hitherto, on the occasions when she had dined downstairs with Bertha and myself, she had worn something which I called a " kid's compro- mise," but I believe is known as a demi-toilette. Now she was in a regular dinner-dress, white, low, apd long. Round her neck was the string of pearls that I had given her. She looked very shy and rather pleased; she smiled and blushed. I made a low obeisance, kissed her hand, and begged to be allowed the honour of congratulating Miss Amory on her birthday. . . . .</p>
        <p>Wilmay was-protesting, and thanking me for the pearls as we sat down to dinner. Wilmay, as my sister had arranged, took the place of the hostess, and Bertha and myself sat on either side of her. " Well ? " said Bertha, "and what might my lord the guardian happen to be thinking of us?" "At the first shock—l mean, at the first en- chantment—l can think only of myself. Wilmay, tell the truth. How old are you?" " I'm sixteen, please, Edward. No, that's not right, I'm in my seventeenth year." "And I'm really in my thirty-ninth year, and I'm considered to be young for my age; have always felt young until now. Now I feel that I am one hundred and four. I see the babe for which I bought chocolates—" "But, Edward," interrupted Wilmay, "1 waf not a babe; I was ten years old." "A very young ten, and wore on the occasion when I first met you a somewhat unconventional costume."</p>
        <p>Wilmay laughed and blushed. It was as though someone had given her the power of blushing for a birthday present. She had never been in the least self-conscious before. • . . ...</p>
        <p>"And now," I said, "what a change is here!" "Not really changed," Wilmay pleaded, "My hair's done differently, and I've got a new dress, but I'm still just the same—the same, but in dis- guise." But she was not just the same—and she was never just the same again. The twilight of child- hood was passing, and the dawn was coming--the dawn, the day, and the night. The dinner was amusing. enough. Bertha was always more or less, a bright woman. Wilmay laughed, more quietly it seemed to me, than usual, and talked less. Some of the things she said were serious to the verge of sentimentality, and, of course' we laughed at her for them mercilessly. She had been reading stories and seeing plays, and she said that it seemed as if life were very sad, but that the sadness was in some ways rather nice. And then she was compelled to laugh at herself.</p>
        <p>Bertha said that I was not to be long over my cigarette, because I was going to play to them. "No, I think not," I said. "Your piano's never in tune. And you cover it with a sort of brocade tablecloth, pot palms, photographs, majolica, and mess generally, because you think it looks pretty." "Speak to him, Wilmay!" said Bertha. "Ex- plain to him!" "The piano's perfectly all right, Edward," said Wilmay, laughing, "and you may have the things taken off it, if you like. But while you're in my house, you're expected—to—to behave as such. "I accept the .ebuke in the spirit in which it is offered, and in five minutes I will do myself the honour to wait upon her—as Inv tailor says." But when I got into the drawing-room, somehow or other we all three began to talk, and we went on talking until I rose to go. I had promised to meet some men at the club. Then Wilmay said that I must stop a little longer, and play, because it was her birthday. So °I stopped and played them Chopin waltzes and nocturnes, and they went through the usual raptures. It was late rather when at last I stood ir the hall putting on my coat. Wilmay followed me from the drawing-room into the hall.</p>
        <p>"Has Mr. Derrimer's cab got a lamp inside it ? " she asked Carter. Carter believed so. "Then," she said, giving me a little note, "you may have this to read as you go home. Good-night, again." The note ran as follows : "Dear Edward,—l am writing this in my bedroom beforel dinner. I know I shan't be able to thank you half enough for those lovely pearls, and I want to chank you again and again. You are always so kind to me. "Ever your loving, WILMAY." I put the note in my pocket. For a few minutes I mused absent-mindedly, and then I noticed that the cab had nearly reached the club. I made the man turn round and drive me home instead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>Bertha did not bring Wilmay out, definitely and formally, until she was seventeen. During the year before I had got into the habit of going rather often to Bertha's house. Bertha loved good music for ten minutes. It was essential that the music should be good, and that it should not last longer than ten minutes. So she used to send me with Wibnay to St. James's Hall, while she enjoyed at home what she called "the rare luxury of not hearing a concert." Sometimes I rode in the Park with Wilmay. In one way and another I saw a good deal of her. , Bertha was widening the scope of her ambition for Wilmay. "You may not know it," she said to me, "but Wilmay is probably at this moment the most beau- tiful girl in the world." "I don't know all the others," I said. "But Wilmay looks all right." "Looks all right! I've been through ten London seasons,without seeing anything like her."</p>
        <p>"Ten London sea-sonsv, or-even more--we're getting very old, Bertha, very, very old." " You can be old, if you like," replied Bertha. "Age is no crime in a man. But I am not old, and I won't be. Ah !" she sighed, "If I could only be Wilmay for one season ! I wonder if she knows what a perfectly heavenly time she is going to have." Bertha had some grounds for her extravagant praise. Wilmay's beauty was unusual and re- markable. In the street, as she went unconsciously past, people turned to look at her. In the theatre or at concerts, though she dressed quietly and her own attention was always wholly given to the play or the music, she made something like a sensation. That eccentric but charming old lady, Lady Harston, met Wilmay by chance at Bertha's house, and was much impressed. When she left she shook Wilmay warmly by the hand, and said, "Good-bye, my dear, and thank you." "But why do you thank me ?" Wilmay asked. "Because I have seen the face of an angel." Wilrnav asked me afterwards what she had</p>
        <p>meant. I told her that I dmt not know, an that Lady Harston was more or less mad. But I might just as well have told her the truth, for it was im- possible to spoil her. I think she knew that she was very beautiful, and I suppose she was glad of it. But compliments on her beauty, however delicate and indirect, bored her when they came from women and frightened or displeased her when they came from men. Bertha gave a dance soon after Wilmay came out. Certainly, that night Wilmay hardly looked liked a flesh-and-blood mortal, an ordinary thing that danced and could be taken down to supper. Her beauty was not of this world. It was not only that hcr features were perfect in shape and that the colouring of her eyes and hair and skin was so lovely ; she had a rare sweetness of expreision, an expression that is only found with an absolutely unselfish and noble nature. I saw Stenling, the portrait-painter, looking at her with undisguised and reverent admiration. Later in the evening Bertha touched me on the arm. "Isn't she splendid?" she said,</p>
        <p>" Who ? " - "Wilmay, of course," Bertha went on, speaking rapidly in a low tone. "Everybody's talking of her. Stenling's in a great state of excitement. He goes about saying, Either I paint Miss Amory or I die.' But I musn't wait here," and before I could reply she had passed on. Wilmay gave me two dances. I'danced the first with her, and we sat out the second. "It's good of you, Wilmay," I said, "to take pity on the aged and infirm like this." "Do you know, Edward," she said, "that you've always been the same age ever since I've known you. You've not changed in the least. You'll never be old."</p>
        <p>"It's my fixed intention to be forty next year." "But you've not changed, and I have. And women get old so quickly—it's very sad. Yes, of course I am young enough now. But"—she stretched out her hands—" I feel the nasty little slippery moments running through my fingers and laughing, and I can't hold them." " Wilinay," I said, "this will not do at all. Your time is now. The night is yours—a night of triumph. You must be very happy." She smiled. "I am not really very miserable. But I'm not very happy either.' "Why not ? " " I'm—l don't know—restless. Did you ever want something and yet not know what it was ? " I reflected, and said that I knew what she meant. .</p>
        <p>There was a moment's pause, and then she said :</p>
        <p>"You must not tell Bertha that I am not quite absolutely happy, because she would not. understand as you do, and it would distress her. I should not like that—l'd do anything for Bertha, you know, and anything wouldn't be half enough." Yes, that was precisely what I was afraid of— that Wilmay would do anything for Bertha, even to the imperilling of her own happiness. "Besides," Wilmay went on, "it is really noth- ing—a fit of sentimentality. Why don't you laugh at me, Edward ? " "To-night," I said, "you are too beautiful to be laughed at ? " She looked at me with wide-open, troubled</p>
        <p>eyes. "Or else," I said, "perhaps I've grown too senti. mental myself to do any laughing." lett the dance early, and we went home. flub it was long after that before I went to bed. I had to make up my mind one way or the other, and I found it difficult. The decision came at last, and "it only remained for me to act upon it, and as far as possible to avoid the weakness of pitying myself. When my man called me in the morning, he was a little surprised to be told that we should leave for Paris that night. In the afternoon I went round to Bertha's house. Wilmay was out. Lady Harston was taking her to some picture-gallery. "Which," as Bertha re- marked, is all very well when you're very young, but, personally, I don't go to picture-galleries the afternoon after a dance." Bertha looked at me up and down. "What's the matter with you? What have you been doing with yourself ? " "Nothing. Never been fitter." "Well, you don't look it. Go home, and then go to bed.' "No," I said, "I shall go home, and then go to Paris." Bei tha got up, walked to the window, and looked out. "Going to be away long?" "Oh, some little time, I believe." Bertha sighed. "You won't think me unsisterly, but I'm not altogether sorry to hear it." " Unsisterly? Why, only a near blood relation could have said that. And why are you glad?" "I think you know." "And I'm positive I don't." She crossed the room, and sat down again. "It's not on my account, of course," she said. "I shall miss you very much. You've been most useful and obliging whenever I've given a dinner or anything, and I'm not ungrateful. I like nothing better than to have you here. I'm sure Wilmay will miss you, too. But—well—" "Ah ! We come to it at last." "Well, don't you think that you've been too much with Wilmay of late? You go everywhere with her. It might look—can't you see for yourself ? " "It might look as if I were her guardian—l I am." 'And it may look something quite different. It may make people think that you yourself are engaged to her or about to be engaged to her— prevent a suitable man from coming forward—do IVilmay a lot of harm, while it can do you no good. Where is the sense of • it? Wilmay is fond of you very much as a child is fond of her father. What your feelings towards her are, I don't pretend to know. But if you carry on anything approaching a flirtation with her, you behave very meanly and badly, and of course you cannot possibly marry her." "You put ideas into one's head. Let me think It over. By what law of this land, or any other land, am I forbidden to marry Wilmay, if we both decide on that ? " "You are forbidden by your own feelings." "I have none." "Please be serious. You are twenty-two years older than Wilmay—much too old. You are also much too poor • you have about a quarter of the money that Wilmay hat. With her wonderful beauty she ought to make a brilliant marriage. Youth, wealth, high rank—she ought to have all of them' and you know it, and you have none of them. Besides, what would the world say? That you had taken advantage of your position as her guardian to serve your own interests rather than hers. And the world would be right." "And yet you have told me to marry money." " No—l did mention a lady—two ladies—who might have made you happy, would certainly have accepted you, and happened to have money. That is a very different thing. Ah ! If you could only have taken my advice then !" • "I couldn't have married both, unless I'd arranged for the sentences to run consecutively— that's the right phrase, I believe—and I didn't care to make invidious distinctions. And now, my dear Bertha, you can curb your feVerish imagination. I am not going to marry Wilmay. lam going to Paris to-night, which, as you will see in your calmer moments, is not the same thing." " Can you take nothing seriously? Of course, I 1 did not really suppose that you wanted to marry Wilmay, but her future's a very grave question, and I don't see why I should be laughed at because I am so anxious to do the best I possibly can for her."</p>
        <p>"Well, then, I will speak seriously. You have your own idea of a brilliant marriage, and I do not think your idea is Wilmay's, and Pm absolutely certain that it's not mine. Remind yourself that there are such things as unhappy duchesses." "My dear Edward, I'm not a snob. I only say that one can't give up common-sense altogether." "At any rate, I am not going to have Wilmay forced into any marriage. She must be left free. For goodness' sake don't make plots and schemes for her." "You're really rather crude, Edward. One does not force people now-a-days. I might, perhaps, advise, if I felt quite sure I was right. There could be no harm in that." "True, you've advised me to marry your own selections, and there's been no harm in it. Because, you see, I have not married them. But with Wilmay it is different. She knows nothing of men or of marriage; she is as innocent as a baby, and she is devoted to you. She might let herself be persuaded—in fact, I should think your advice would be much more dangerous than if you point- blank commanded her to marry the nearest Duke. However, when the time comes I shall have some- thing to say, you will remember. And in the meantime I'm not here to quarrel with you, but to say good-bye." "I never quarrel," said Bertha, with her most charming smile. "Good-bye, Edward. Come to London occasionally, you know. You ought to do thg, ius_t_tcLaro_that km.not standinaover Wilmay</p>
        <p>with a horse-whip and making If&amp; marry tre wrong people. I will say good-bye to her for you, and explain, when she comes back."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>For nearly two years after this I never saw Wilmay. Of course I did not spend the whole of this time at Paris. • I did occasionally come to London, and it was more by chance than from intention that I did not meet Wilmay there. For some few months I travelled in Italy. During this period I composed an opera, and it was produced, and had a certain measure of success—that is to say, it was not nearly as good as I had meant it to be, but it benefited me financially. It was the first money of importance that I had ever earned, and I endeavoured to feel that it degraded me, but could not manage it. However, I am quite con- scious that I am the fool of this story and not the hero, and that it is better to write of other people. Let me first of all do justice to Bertha. A very great person indeed, who was sixty-five years old, but looked more, was good enough to approve of Wilmay. He even decided to share his greatness with her, and—with the mad impetuosity so common at that age—sent his sister to talk it over with Bertha. Bertha expressed herself as being much complimented, but was afraid that she was unable to give the great person any encouragement. The great person thought it over, and the mature wisdom of his sixty-five years spared him the trouble of being refused by Wilmay. Bertha felt that she had done well, and sighed. Wilmay laughed. Bertha occasionally sent me a selection, and told me that she had found the very man for Wilmay. At first, this used to make me rather nervous. But I soon discovered that Wilmay never allowed any of these very men to get as far as a proposal. I grew more easy in my mind ; it seemed as if Bertha would be compelled to let Wilmay choose her own husband.</p>
        <p>Only twice did Wilmay in person actually receive a proposal. The first proposal was from a young novelist, a man of good family but no means. He seems to have been really very much in love with Wilmay. He compelled her to hear him, even though he told her at the, time that he knew there was no hope for him. Wilmay refused him defi- nitely and finally, but she did not laugh this time. On the contrary, as she told me in her letter about it, she cried. "He turned white, and fidgetted with his hands, and began sentences which he couldn't finish. And then he was so humble, and didn't seem to think anything about himself, and his eyes were so sad. I couldn't help crying, and I do hope I'll never have anything of the kind happen to me again." Bertha was exceedingly angry with this young man. After his refusal, he went very much to the bad, which, without doubt, was quite romantic of him, though it would probably have mid him better to have written a novel.</p>
        <p>The second proposal was tram a young baronet, hr Vincent Carrone' and this proposal Wilmay .ccepted. As I have done justice to Bertha let me deo do justice to Sir Vincent. He was a man of ,wenty-eight, good-looking, and sufficiently vealthy. He was honestly in love with Wilmay, 'lid in most respects he was a fine fellow. He was lot clever, but neither was he appallingly stupid. lie was a straight man, with a good reputation and t liking for country life and sport. Before the pro. 059 took place I had a letter from Bertha about lira. He had said nothing to her, but he was saying very marked attention to Wilmay, and . Bertha felt sure that he would ask Wilmay to marry him. I replied that if Wilmay was really !and of the man, and accepted him, nothing could ac more satisfactory, but that in the meantime Bertha had better not interfere. How far she did nterfere, if at all, I have never learned since, nor wished to learn.</p>
        <p>Bertha announced to me the engagement in a fetter of six rhapsodic pages. She was quite certain that Wilmay would be very happy. Sir Vincent was so good, and so kind, and—but there was no :Ind to her praise of him. lam quite sure that at his time, as at all other times, Bertha only desired Wilmay's happiness. If she Influenced Wilmay at ill, it was only with the view of securing that sappiness. She concluded by saying that Wilmay ilso would write to me, but that she was very shy in speaking or writing of the engagement. j The letter from Wilmay was much shorter, and J I did not quite like it. There was an almost pathetic reiteration that she hoped, or she believed, that she was going to be happy. But there was no statement that she actually was happy then. she, too, praised Vincent, but almost in the same terms that Bertha used, as if she were repeating a lesson. She did not describe, and, of course, I had not expected her to describe, the circumstances of the proposal; but I was surprised that the greater part of the letter was about Bertha, and not about her- self or her lover. I was not quite satisfied, and yet I seemed to have no definite ground for interfe- rence. I did my best to eliminate any personal feelings of my own, and look at the thing fairly, and I found nothing to support my objection but some infinitesimal things that Bertha would have laughed at. Indeed, Bertha herself had partly explained the tone of Wilmay's letter. A week later the engagement was publicly annoumetl ; the marriage was to take place six months afterwards. Wilmay had written to me frequently since I had left England, often upbraid- in,g me for stopping away so long, asking me why I (lid it, and telling me to come back. Now, is three months, Iliad only two short notes from her. - They were written far less freely and familiarly than her old letters, and though there was ar assumption of lightheartedness all through them. they did not give me the impression that she was happy. As the six months drew to an end, I heard very little of Wilmay. The trousseau was being purchased, preparations were being made for tht marriage, wedding presents were pouring in Wilmay had no time to write, and Bertha only sent short scrappy noteS written in the greatest haste. It was about three weeks before the wedding that I returned to England to complete some necessary business arrangements. I arrived in London in the morning. I had not told Bertha what day I should come, but in thf afternoon I started out to call on her. It was bright, sunny afternoon, and I found the wall through the park pleasant after the hour that I hac just spent in a dry solicitor's dusty office. As : walked along Bertha's victoria met me. Berths and Wilmay were both in it, and neither of then saw inc. Wilmay was laughing. That laugh con soled me. Everything was all right then, and : had been worrying myself for nothing. As Bertha and Wilmay were both out, it waf obviously of no good to go on to Bertha's house To sit in her mistaken drawing-room and wait foi her would have been merely tiresome. So I went back home, meaning to call on Bertha again late] in the afternoon. My flat is on the first floor, an as I walked up the steps to it I passed a handsom( middle-aged gentleman, who was peering about hint uncertainly. He caught me up again on the land- ing, just as I drew my latchkey from my pocket, stared at me _hard, and then raised his hat.</p>
        <p>"I think," he said, "that I must be speaking tc Mr. Derrimer--Mr. Edward Derrimer." "Yes," I said, "that is my name." He was carefully dressed, and rather gave me the idea that all his clothes were absolutely new. His hair was dark brown, and beginning to grow grey ; he wore a short waxed moustache, and touched the ends of it nervously from time to time. His deep-set blue eyes watched me narrowly. He began to pull a card-case from his pocket, and then said:</p>
        <p>"But I think you must know me, Mr. Per. rimer."</p>
        <p>"It's unpardonable, but my memory—" "Perhaps I should have said that you must have heard of me. Wilmay would not recall me, but Philip Amory, who was very, very kind to me —though in some respects he sadly misunderstood me—must have mentioned my name." In a second the scene flashed back on my memory. I saw myself sitting in the library at Sinden, with the table drawn up near to the window, and the lighted lamp on it, reading that letter of Philip's to me. The phrase "adopt a quite different course, came back to me.</p>
        <p>I held out my hand. "You are Wilmay': uncle." He bowed and smiled. "I am. I have been particularly anxious to see you. I thought it best to see you first." "Won't you come in 7" I said. And we parsed together into the room I used as my study.</p>
      </div>
