      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>DANGERS OF A SILK MILL, Girl's Narrow Escape.</head>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF A SILK MILL, Girl's Narrow Escape.</p>
        <p>Is spite of Factory Aots—a—nd precautions to protect workers from machinery, accidents occur. A girl named Emily Sugden, of Wakefield-road, Brighouse, had a narrow escape in a silk spinning mill, when she fainted, falling near a piece of moving machinery. Her workmates promptly rescueg her, or she would have been seriously injured. The "'Bradford Daily ',Argus " interviewed the girl. "I must tell you," said Miss Sugden, " how it was I came to faint in the mill. A year or two ago I went out for a drive and got wet through. As a result I caught a severe cold, which I could not get rid of, until recently Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people entirely cured me. When first taken ill at the mill with dizziness and fainting I was put to bed at home, where I remained for a month, with what proved to be a severe attack of influenza. After this I was allowed to get up, but I was so weak and dizzy that my mother would not trust me in the street for fear I should be run over. Change of air did no good. I begged of my mother to let me try if I could not do my work again." "Did you go back to the silk mill?"</p>
        <p>Yes:, buCI had only been there.a fortnight when I bad the narrow escape you beard of. The feinting fits came on again, and I suddenly fell down against a machine. end the wonder is that I did not get my head taken off. I was again put to bed. The doctor (a very clever one) said the influenza had left me suffering from *rumba. I was a fearful colour—a sort of pale green— and helpless as a baby. 'A friend told me of a young woman who had suffered just like myself, and had been cured by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people. My mother sent for a box. Immediately I begsn them I felt better. I got on rapidly after the first doses, and in less than a week I was able to go out. The dizziness disappeared, and I began to eat better than for a long time. My natural colour returned, and when I had taken three boxes of the pills I was completely cured. When I started with the pills I stopped the medicine sent by the doctor ; it was solely Dr Williams' pink pills that cured me. My mother thought I ought to tell the doctor what I bad done, and so I went to see him. He was glad of my recovery, and said he knew Dr Williams' pink pills, and that there were excellent properties in them."</p>
        <p>The candid opinion of the medical man (whose skill Miss Sugden commends)con fi,ins what all say who have Personally investigated the cures effected by Dr Williams' pink pills in 'mewls, bile, consumr- tion, decline, eczema, fits, gout, headaches, heart disease, influenza, neuralgia, paralysis, rickets, sciatica, and all the ailments that especially affect the male and female system. Dr Williams' medicine com- pany, bolborn-viaduct, London, send the pills post free for two shillings and ninepecoe ; but they can be ob- tained wherever medicine is sold, and the genuineness is assured by Dr Williams' name on the wrapper. Sub- stitutes lacking this name ate to be avoided ; they never cured anyone.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO HOTEL ALTEdATIONS. Action by a Warrington Hairdresser. Alleged Obstruction of Light and Air. At the Liverpool Chancery Court, held in St. George's Hall, oa Tussday, before Vice-Chancellor Hall, the case of James McClew, hairdresser, Sec.. Horssmarket-street, v. George Shaw and Co., Ltd.. of Leigh Brewery,Leigh, was heard. Mr. Maberly and Mr F M Preston (instructed by Messre Jenkins :and Co., Warrington) appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr A. Grant (instructed by Messrs Marsh, Son and Calvert, of Leigh), for the defendant company. The plaintiff (Mr Mo. Clew) claimed an injunc- tion "to restrain the defendant company, its contrac- tors, agents, and workmen, from erecting upon the defendant company's premises in Horsemarket-street any house or building of a greater height than the building which formerly stood upon the premises, and which bad recently been pulled down, or in any such manner, as to darken, injure, or obstruct the access of light or air, to such of tho plaintiff's windows, or lights in his house, number 47, Horsemarketistreet, afcresaid, as are ancient lights." Damages were claimed for the obstruction. In opening the case Mr Maberly said the plaintiff was the owner of a shop and dwelling-house in Horsemarket- street, Warriogton, and the action was brought to re. strain the defendant company from building a wall which obetruoted certain lights on the plaintiff's premises. The Hop Pole Inn, was on the right hand of the defendant's property, which was separated from the Hop Pole by a passaee, the back part of which was only 61t. wide. The plaintiff occupied his premises on the ground floor, having a shop lacing Horsemarket-street, In which he sold tobacco and so on. There was a Risall raised partition and a doo•way into the bairdressiree room at the back. This hairdressing room was lighta by a window, and that was the only light it had. It was, of lltcouree, very valuable for the purposes of the plaintiff's business. There was a living loom beyond that and that was lighted by a window looking into the yard. There was also a wash-house and a scullery also lighted by Window. Until the defendant company began the alterations to the Hap Pole there was a wall eight feet in height, and behind that a small sloping roof. There was a considerable view of the sky from the window which lighted the hair-dressing room. The only projection at all was the corner of the old building ; practically then neither of the walls was an obstruction to the hair- dresser's window. The defendants tad, however, raised that which was before Sit in parts to a height of 18ft 6 inches. The result was that the hairdresser's window was thrown into obscurity. The Vice-Chancellor : Do you complain of any win- dows beside those which have been named ? Mr Maberly : Those on the first floor are obstruated. There is a room on the first floor. One room is used by Miss McClew, who is a milliner, and does a great deal of sewing there. The Vice-Chancellor : *hat is the defence assuming there is an interference ? The building is finished. Mr Grant : Yes, long ago. Mr Maberly ; We gave the defendant company notice on the 20th January, and the writ was issued on the 25th February. The defendants then went on rapidly with the work. Mr Grant: It is a very small thing, as your Honour sees. The Vice-Chancellor : Yes, but the plaintiff has only a small light. If a man is drowning, and is only an Inch above the water,and you push him under,he drowns The question is whether the plaintiff is entitled to relief at all. What is the defence ? Mr Maberley : I say that these are ancient windows. We Were in occupation before the year 1874. We bought In 1874 and were in occupation before that. The Vice-Chancellor : Thera is a defence raised that you are only the lessee. Mr Maberly : I don't think the defendant company seriously suggest that. Mr Grant : We de. Mr Maberly : The defendants say that because we are !vines holding from a predecessor that we cannot prescribe against the lessor, but that is utterly un- founded in law. Mr Grant : The principle upon which my defence is founded is that a man cannot prescribe against bin land- lord. My friend will have to prove that at the date of the lease the house was there-60 years ago. My point is that the house was not there, and that the plaintiff could not require tigt.ts of light against his landlord' I admit that we had no right to build this wall if the house was there.</p>
        <p>After a long conference between counsel and the Solicitors and others engaged in the case, during which the Vice-Chancellor was absent from the court, an order was made on the defen.:ant company to pay plaintiff £1,990, to cover his interest in the premises,tbiS amount also to cover Mr McClew's damages and costs. CADBURY'S COCOA is a pure, relined beverage nutritious stimulating, and digestible. The " Lancet " says it repre lents the standard of highest purity." Entirely free from admixtures, such as kola, malt, hops, alkali, &amp;C. Insist upon having CADBURY'S, as other Cocoas are sometimes sulibeLtAitsultiec sdcorocicliios, eatraproKfist.zE CAPS, PacketsAPsec.—For it —anodr Tins Varicoseon Veins ly. (2 and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Legs, Sprains &amp;O.; they are porous. Debt In texture. and Inexpensive snl are drawn on like an ordinary stocking. —Prices 4s 6d. 7s. Bd and 10s 6d each. Postage free.—WHITE AND WRIGHT. Snr- gD .nstrurnent and Truss Makers. 98, Renshaw-street Ltop</p>
