      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DR. BARNARDO'S HOMES Musical Boys Visit Warrington,</head>
        <p>DR. BARNARDO'S HOMES Musical Boys Visit War- rington,</p>
        <p>A noble work, and one which is deserving of ungrudging support is carried en by the National Incorporated Wails' and Strays' Society—better known as Dr Bareardo's Homes. For thirty fiveyears these homes have been labouring in t e cause of destitute, orphan and waif and stray children. To-day over 5.400 boys and girls are under the care of the institutions and fresh admissions are taking place at a rate of sixty every week. In all over 43,000 young people,rescoed from the most pitiable conditions, have passed through the homes. Over 13,000 of these have been emigrated to Canada and the Colonies, where 98 per cent have done full credit to their training. The work is truly national in principle and extent. Children are admitted freely and unconditionally, on proof of destitution from all parts of the empire. With the object of enlisting sympathy on behalf of the institutions, a meeting was held in the Assembly Room at the Conservative Club on Tuesday evening, when an interesting entertainment was presented by the "musical boys" from one of the homes. The attendance was not nearly so large as the object deserved, but the beautiful weather was no doubt responsible for this. The meeting was under the direction of Mr J B Wookey,tbe deputation secretary It was evi- dent that the boys bad been exceedingly well trained, their entertainment affording the utmost pleasure. The programme included selections on the sweet-toned handbells, the xylophone, dulcimer, cornet, ocarina, and bag-pipes and the buys proved themselves to be skilled performers on them all. Two little fellows gave a vocal duet in a very creditable manner, and were heartily applauded. Mr B Wadswerth kindly gave his services as accompanist. During the interval an interesting address was delivered by Mr. Wookey. Be said the homes had been in existence for 36 years and had been instrumental in permanently rescuing and saying nearly 50,000 bort and girls. Be claimed that the homes were absolutely • unive in character. There were other homes loing -equally cool work, but on different lines. Dr. Barnacle's Homes were unique at least in two respects. Firstly, they never refuvd immediate admissi9n to any ute boys or girls. They might be of any age—they bad • received them as young as four days old—of any creel, or of no cre•cl at all, and of any nationality. They might be blind, deaf, dumb, paralysed. or suffering from some loathsome disease. Only one thing was necoasary, and that was that they should be absolutely destitute. That being so they were received without money, without voting. without patronage, and without waiting. The homes were also unique in a more serious sense than this, be- cause in nearly all the great centres ef population, from Newcastle-on-Tyne te Plymouth, they had workers en- gaged night by night in seeking in the haunts of vice and crime, children who needed their assistance. There were now in the homes dose up3n 6,000 children, and at least 300 of them were babies in arm.. There were no fewer than 700 Children sufficiently afflicted as to eon- eantly need medicsl or nursing skill. The Society had 97 separate institutions, including village homea for girls, labour houses, and homes in which the children &amp;mated the whole of their time to education. Nearly 14,000 boys and girls had been sent to Canada, and it had been proved by the Government statistics that ever 98 per cent of these were leading honest. sober, and industrious lives. Mr Wookey proceeded Co give some interesting details as to the working of the bounce, mentioning that no less than £2OO per day was spent in bread and butter alone. The nE work was entirely dependent upon the freewill offer- ings of kenevolent people, and it was most wonderful 4 where the money came from. The first year's income 0 of the homes, when they were started 36 years ago, was C £214. The income last year was £146,000. In appeal-' lug for assistance, Mr Woolley said they had received a number of children from Warrington who had been sent to them by Christian workers, and if thee had not been received into the homes, they would have become chargeable to the rates either as paupers or criminals, and thus cost far more tekeep or punish than it cost to save them. He asked for assistance out of sheer pity and compassion for the children, and commended the work to his hearers as a great Christian work. (Ap- plause.) It may be mentioned that the Chairman of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee (Alderman Bolton) kindly gave free admission for the boys to the baths. Homes were found for the boys by Mr H. Taylor, and thanks are due to the following for acelioods.ting them : Messrs Taker, R Woods, W Roberts. H Taylor, and H Thornton, and Miss Agnes Pierpoint.</p>
        <p>BOWLING. SARACEN'S READ vWARRJNGTOS CONSERVA- TIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The above clubs met on Saturday on the Sankey-street !rent the match resulting in a victory for the home team by 89 points. Saore : CONSERV.LTIvES. CILRACEN'S HEAD. S. D. 3 P Taylor (oapt) wT BRoawt be rb so n( : a p. .. .. 6 ..... G Warburton ... 8 r ... 8. D. 1 o 11 1.3 P Turner A Broady ............... 10 B Woods ll 1 2 Rst) ... ............... 3113 91 17 y Broadhurst ll 1 W Mitlington 9 f ' W Daniell la Rotherham J H Bitter's mD B watson ?} 7 D Rawlings J Gender. lO t 3 w 3 Willaock J Leierh 3 W Jones 6 W Timmins ............ 11 A Bit:Meal H Booth B Bailey ................ .. 9 } /3 6 9J13 It Bradburn 11}10 P Remiss ............... 6 1 3 Holland 3 13111113ga J Thornton ll J RedseCock ............ 31 11113 J Unsworth 4 wt 3 wileon 119 73 188 93 ..,</p>
