        <p>Majority for Conservative Club B9</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE v WARWICKSHIRE AND WORCESTERSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Teams of twenty-seven a side representing these coun- ties met at Ashton on Saturday afternoon. After an exciting contest, in which fine play was shown on both sides, the home counties won by a single wood. In a S cries of eleven games previously played between the counties Warwickshire and Worceitershire had only won twice. The green was in excellent condition, and the weather favourable, Scores :—Warwickshire and Wor- cest?rshire, 468 ; Lancashire and Cheshire, 467.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>I.</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS,</p>
        <p>In THE BLAZED TRAIL (Constable and Co. p p 416, 65.) Mr Stewart Edward White, like so many American authors, shows a keen eye for the possi- bilities of romance in the world of commerce and for the dramatic interest of a strong man's struggles in the market-place. But his hero has to fight not only with the forces representing civilisation but with the elements of nature in their most pitiless form. The pine forests of North west America provide the stage for this double contest. The " Landloeker " and " Log- roller" are sometimes able to win their victories in the Stock Exchange by harnessing to their own service the forces of Nature. But Nature must be obeyed before she is tamed,and the successful wood merchant must read her face as easily as he reads the faces of men or the lists of the Stock Exchange. And while with one eye he watches for the " freshet " which is to take his logs to market,with the other he must watch the machinations of his rivals, for the same firma who do open battle with him in Chicago will use more questionable modes of warfare in the primitive and lawless backwoods. And,above all,the semi-barbaric employes have to be ruled with a rod of iron. Such are the struggles which mould our hero's character till be is ready for the last great victory—over himself. By an inex- plicable yet eminently human impulse he sacrifices the fruits of all his lab3urs to save an employes life, and in so doing discovers that love, which he had offered up on the altar of success, proves victorious in the end. The author's style is rugged, but ceases to repel after a time because it is adapted to the matter. But his pen seems sometimes to outrun his thoughts. The book abounds in long descriptive passages and technical explanations, full of interest in themselves but a drag on the story. Their aid ought not to be nestled to create the heal atmos- phere. The "get up" of the book is udmirable. But let anyone who would avoid disillusionment forbear to look at the illustrations, . _ .</p>
        <p>Mr F Marion Crawford's powerful novel "to the Palace of the King " has been added to the three- and-sixpenny library of Macmillan. Many people will recollect the first publication of this love story ,-)f old Madrid a year or two ago, and recalling the flattering opinions expressed in regard to it.be glad of the opportunity now afforded of purchasing the work at such a reasonable figure and in such a handy and attractive form. "In the Palace of the King " is certainly one of the best of Mr Crawford's later novels which have not all been up to the earlier standard. No story could possibly compress more thrilling incident into a shorter space )1 time for almost every page brings a new I sensation and the whole action of the tale takes place in about 24 hours. Yet the reader feels to have lived through almost a lifetime at the Spanish Court by the time the last chapter is reached. The novel is a wonder- fully clever piece of dramatic writing, and in point of interest and sensation Comes second to none of Mr Crawford's stories. We recommend it to those who like good fiction, They could read " In the Palace of the King" on the hottest of July days without soffering one minute's boredom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A..Society.—A Unique</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A..Society.—A Unique</p>
        <p>Meeting:</p>
        <p>The cpen meeting ef the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday last was a unique one, inasmuch as the con- duct of it was strietly confined to ladies. The chair was occupied by a lady, the devotional exorcises were condueted by ladle% the speaker and the soloist were also ladies and in justice to those who took part in it, it must be admitted that they acquitted themselves admirably. There was a large gathering, the church being well filled in every part. Mrs. Moores presided and spoke el ,quently upon t' God's claim to our hearts and service." Mrs. Elarnpeon delivered the address and based her remarks upon the incident of the Woman at the well." E'er various thoughts upon Christ's dealings with the woman made a good impression upon the meeting, especially when speaking upon God's understanding of men and their motives, which was so contrary to man's understanding of men. Miss Whitfield read the lesson and Mies Green led the prayer. Two solos were eentributed by Miss Bridge in a manner which delghted th 3 mebting,,. A.t the close, Mr B Fa rrar, the hon secretary complimented the ladies who had taken part in the service for the able and interesting manner they had conducted it, and ex- pressed the hope that the experiment would be repeated at an early date. A collectien was taken on behalf of the funds of the society and realiasi a satisfactory sum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Baptist Ch apel,:Latchford.</head>
        <p>Baptist Ch apel,:Latchford.</p>
        <p>The annual sermons were preached in this church en Sunday by the Rev J VT Varley, of St Annes-Pa-the Sea. In the morning Mr Vsrley addressed a largely attended young people's service, and there was a good congrega- tion in the afternoon. In the °tempi pg the church was crowded to its utmost capacity and was an excellent tribute to Mr Varley's popularity in the districtland abilities as a preacher. The collections realised about £l5.</p>
      </div>
