      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>NARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY'S ANNUAL MEETING. The Financial Position.</head>
        <p>NARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY'S ANNUAL MEETING. The Financial Position.</p>
        <p>A fine summer's evening is not conducive to a large indoor gathering to receive reports and balance sheets and elect officers of even a society such as the Warring- ton Musical Slciety, and it was not surpriiiog to find a small attendance on Tuesday evening at the annual general meetin: of the S3ciety. The meeting was held in Trinity Schoolroom, and was presided over by the Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr). Amongst those present were :—Messrs Charles White, J Newcombe, 11 Rand, W II Payton, MilS Bic., WLI Wil iegon, F Elickman. P. C Price, lir Johnson, New.li, Flipkiga. with F Crossley (conducter), W E Alrin and F J L )rd Oion. secretaries) and L W Reynolds (hon. treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>REPORr OF' THE RON. ge,- OREFARIES.</head>
        <p>REPORr OF' THE RON. ge,- OREFARIES.</p>
        <p>The report of the hen. secretaries (dessrs W E Atkin and F J Lord) was as follows •—', Die ti-et rehearsal was held on September 17th, 1901 ; the final concert was given on March 11th, 1902 Hence the season's work covered a period of about six months. There were in all 29 rehearsals in Trinity &amp;hoof Room, exclusive of one or two extra special practices before concerts else- where. The attendance was on the whole good, but again a falling off in numbers towards the end of the season, after Christmas, has to be noted with regret The work oE the prospectus issued in Oetober last was carried out. Three subscription concerts were given, viz. :—on November 12th, 1901, December 23rd, 1901, and March 11 h, 1902. The Messiah ' was this season included in the series of three subscription concerts. The total orchestra was 33, or one Irss than during the preyinus season, the total • chorus 154, or 16 legs than during the 1900-1 season. The number at subscribers to the concerts (137 names on the books) has not decreased, but we fear the sale of tickets to non-subscribers fell off considerably,:and your committee feel the difficulty of securing a general support from the public to be very great indeed,and the I popularizing of the concerts without deteriorating the Istindard and class of progr imme is each year a problem 'which becomes harder and harder to solve. The con- ductor is to be congratulated on having attended at the rehearsals but one, from wnich, unfortunately, illness prevented his attendance. A successful dance in aid of the funds, resulting in a net profit of just over £5O, was given on Tuesday, February 11th, and the heartiest thanks of the society are due to the Ladies' Corninittes and all numerous friends for their help on this occasion.' The committee has reeeivei with great regret and most uhwillingly felt bound to accept the resignation by Mr G F F White, cf his post as hon. librarian. The sincerest thanks of the society are due t o him for his invaluable services in this connection during several years," . _</p>
        <p>Mr ATKIN added that the committe had considered the advisability of shortening the season, and finishing before Lent. He alluded to the successful dance in aid of the funds of the society, and-thanked all the ladies and gentleman who rendered assistance oo that occasion; in fact their thanks were due to the town. He would like to epochally mention Mrs Fairclough and I alias Fairclough, Mrs John Reynolds, Mr Pearson, Mr Payton, Mr Johnson, Mr Bond, Mr Morris, Mr and Mrs Mr Atkin proceeded to refer to the loss sustained by the society by the death of Mrs Sharp, who was one of the subscribers. In explaining why there was a difference between the item in the cash account and the secretaries' report with rehrence to the net proceeds of the dance at Parr H4ll, Mr Atkin explained that the £5O mentioned included the subscriptions received whereas the subscriptions and proceeds were stated separately in the balance sheet.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he was sure they would all agree in the vote of thanks which in course of time would be proposed for the kind assistance which had been received from almost everyone in Warrington. The Secretary reported that help was Rot confined to one or two mem- bers of the society, but to the whole of the town.</p>
        <p>, . Mr. L. W. REYNOLDS submitted the balance sheet and stated tnat a few mere subscriptions had come in since the balance sheet was printed. The loss on the season's work was £2B 64 an 4 the amount due to the treasurer was now £136 Os 7d.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Frank Hickman, the EION TREASURER stated that there was no loss on the Froishara special train. There might have been on the Lyarn trains, but t tild been under consideration whether it would be dvis sble to cantinue running them.</p>
        <p>a Mr. CI:WILES WHITS : Th 0293 9711') atteril the =cart not pay for the special trains themselves ? &amp;Air. REYNOLDS t No.</p>
        <p>M. WHITE : Then the people of Lymm have an undue advantage. Mr. REYNOLDS I don't see how they could come otherwise. We have visitors to the concerts from Latchford and rll-1 wall. rhe train on the average con- veys from 60 to 70 passengers. Mr. WHITS: That is a matter for the Railway Com- pany. Mr REYNILDS pointed out thtt now there were the electric trams to Latchford there Wa3 less reason for running the special train. The committee had never been satisfied with the results obtained by running a special trtin. In an;wer to further Tiestions, Mr. REYNOLDa stated that the society lost more on the "Messiah" concert than 11 DM any other concert...</p>
        <p>Me. IsTawconine considered that the atrocious weathe r en the occasion of the concert was responsible for the loss oa the season's work.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR called attention to the organ trust account and asked why the Corparatioa hal not paid any rent for the organ for nearly three years. Mr. REYNOLDS stated that the agreevaent was that the Corporation shoufl pay £2O per annum but no definite period was stated in the agreement. The Corporati3n did not reoeive that amount for the uie of the organ and they did not feel justified in paying, such a sum to the society. A deputation waited on the Corporation but no decision was arrived at. _</p>
        <p>_.„. The MAYOR suggested that the secretary should write to the Town Clerk on the matter and this was agreed to.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. C. PRICE moyel that the report of the secre- taries and the balance sheet be adopted. Mr. FR&amp;NE HICICtIAN. i s.c3ncling the motion, said he did not think there was any item on the expenditure side of the balance aheet that ought to be cut down. He did not sea how they could lessen the expenditure of the society so far as the vocalists were concerned. On the contrary he would he inclined to raise the standard. The society did not exist for the purpose of making money, and there should be no deterioration in the pro- gramme. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. MR. PARR RE-APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>Mr CHARLES WHITE, ill proposing the re-election of Mr Parr as president of the society for the ensuing year , said he was sure the resolution was one which would meet with the unanimous approval of the meeting. (Applause. )During the past few months Mr Parr had given much of his time to the service of the town, and he was rarely absent from any of the meetings he had to attend The Musical Society was greatly indebted to him, because if there had been no Parr Heti they would not have been able 0 hold the cancerts—(hear, hear)—there was no other p'aes in Warrington where they could possibly have been held. There was another matter which, although slight in itself, should be referred to and that was that the society was especially indebted to Mr Parr for attending every concert and bringing his family with him. (Sear, hear.) If all other heal people of position and influence would azt in a similar manner and display the same earnestness,they would not have had such a balance sheet as that which had been presented that night. (Bear, hear, aad applause.) The motion was carried with acclamation. Mr PARR said he felt it to be a great honour to have been again elected to the responsible position of presi- dent of the society. With regard to the suggestion that had been mode by Mr White with reference to his atten - dance at the concerts, he could assure that gentleman that the boot was maths wrong leg. He had to thank the ladies and gentlemen for providing such a good con- cert, and he could assure the meeting that it was a great pleasure to him and to his family to attend the conzerts on every possible occasion. If during the ensuing year he could be of any service to the society he would accept the kind nomination which bad been made that evening with great pleasure, fie hoped they would have a better financial rep )rt to present when they held their meeting net year. They must all, as far as they could, put their shoulders b the wheel. Everything was good except in the matter of pounds, shillings and pence, and they must endeavour to put the society in a better financial position. (Sear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF OFFICERS AND COMMITTEE. An the vice-preeidents were re-elected. It was stated that some of them did not subicribe Co the society, and it was sugge3ted that their atteution be called to this fact by the secretaries. Mr LEE moved the appointment of the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr PAYTON, in 99.000 ding, rerntrked that it was through no lack of eff irt on the part of the committee that there was an adverse balance. _ _</p>
        <p>The proposition was unanimously carried. The com- mittee consists of Messrs H Bond, D Fanshaw, J New- combs, P C Price, W Scotson, R H Silcock, W H Wall- ington, H Whlfield, J T Knowles, and G Johnson. Oa the motion of Mr JOHNSON, seconded by Mr F HICKMAN. the joint secretaries (Messrs W E Atkin and F J Lord) were ag sin eleete d. It was stated that they had worked admirably in the interests of the society and to the satisfaction of everyone concerned. Mr Atkin. thanked Mr Lord for his able assistance, but the latter contended that the brunt of the work was capably ' carried out by his " senior partner." Mr REYNOLDS was re-appointed hon. treasurer, on the propositions of Mr ErIPKISS, seconded by Mr NEWCOMBE. Mr REYNOLDS observed that he regretted his efforts had not been successful. He could only say he had done all in his power to get as much money as possible. He could not do more, but be hoped that they would re- trieve their position next season. Mr ATKIN alluded to the resignation by Mr G F F White of the position of hon. librarian. Mr White fou it impossible 10 devote the time required for the wor</p>
        <p>Tea under Seal. In order to guarantee a tea that is of the same fine quality when delivered to the consumer as when it is gathered in the Tea Gardens, a tea must be shipped from the Gardens in sealed chests, and, like</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>and a hearty vote of thanks was due to him for his past services. On to proposition of Mr REYNOLDS, econded by Mr LOED. Mr G Johnson (the assistant librarian) was appointed to the office. Mr Newcombe was elected registrar of attendancs.s and auditor.</p>
        <p>THE DEFICIT. SUGGES [IONS FOR PAYING OFF VIE DEBT.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSSLEY (the conductor), in the course of a few remarks at the close of the meeting, said he welcomed the curtailing of the season by a month by doing away with the Lenten cone ut, which bad nr•ver paid. With regard to the wo,k of the cornhig season nothing had been decided. He suggested as a means of helping to clear off tbe debt that they should have a light opera for about three nights. There was, however, a possibil- ity of a loss on this, and :hi only ne ans by which they could provide against it would he by having a guarantee fund. It would cost about £lOO for the three nights. He would be glad to receive any suggestions. He moved a vote of thanks to those who made the arrange- ments for the deuce, which was so successful.</p>
        <p>Mr. Newcomna-s-eLndeJ the vote a thanks.</p>
        <p>The MAY,2 said they felt mast grateful to Mr Crossley for the zeal efficienly and energy he had displayed in the interests of the society. There were not assay Who could ssy they had only ben abY.nt once tro ra duty during the year. He thought a small committee 'nigh t be appointed to consider Mr Crossley's suggestion..</p>
        <p>Mr flicamaN remark ,d that there was no need for a debt to be han4ing over thmThand if some definlte pro- posal ;vas browzbu before the chorus something would b 3 dono.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHARLES War uggested that a banar !night be organis,d wold tha object of extinguishing the debt'. El. thought there would be very little trouble in raising £5OO.</p>
        <p>The &amp;Italia's servic.a in the chair were acknowledged on the proposition of Mr. W. H. Wallington and the pro- ceedings terminated.</p>
