      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A_ TRAP .ACCIDENT AT</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A_ TRAP .ACCIDENT AT</p>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Councillor Rogers obtains 1100 Guineas and</p>
        <p>Costs.</p>
        <p>An action for damages for personal injuries arising out of a trap accident near vVarriogton, last January, was heard before the Lord Chief Justice in the Crown Court, Mancn;ester Assizes, 03 WedlleSdaV no,rning. Mr and Mna 11,3Z,t9 of the General Wolfe 11,:el,Warrington, were the plaintra, and Miss Kiernan and others, pro- prietors of the Pattea Arma Hotel, Warrington, the defendant,. Mr Ll.,z-loa te ir3i.ated plaiotiffs, and Mr Sutton and Mr F E Smith the defendants. Plaintiff wag driving from Winwick t, Warrington in a pony trap, be said, at the rate of six or seven miles an hour. The defendants' 'bus came along behind him,and in attempting to pass struck the hub of the fore wheel, I so d jarred the back wheel. The trap swerved and the pony b ilted, and eventually collided with a telegraph post, pitching Rogers and his wife our. He was under the doctor's care for six weeks, the trap was seriously damaged, and the pony had received certain injuries. Mr Langdon said plaintiffs had been upset about the accident. "They were very much upset" humorously retorted his lordship. Several witnesses were called, including Dr Reginald Smith, who said he had treated the plaintiff for a broken rib on th,‘ right side, and three or four ribs Were bruised. Mrs Rogers was suffering severely from snook.</p>
        <p>'the case sv,ts e7ntual!y RAttlei for 100 guinlas and costs, verdict for the reaintiff.</p>
