      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISI RICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISI RICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Monthly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held on Wednesday afternoon,Mr T Halton presiding. There were also present :—Dr Murray, Me.iers Brooke, Rough ton, Knowles, Backhouse, Forst:law, with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk), Dr Sephton (medical officer) F Kerfoot (inspector of nu slnce.),B S Newcombe (surveyor), and 13 H Burna2e (assistant surveyor).</p>
        <p>CROFT WATER SUPPLY.-•••A. SATISFACTORY ARRANGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that since the last Council meeting be bad avail written the Town Clerk of Warrington upon the subject of the deficiency of the water supply and the drying up of the wells in the township of Croft,reported by Dr Sepbtoo. He had also bad a visit frms the Rev H I Wadeson, Rector of Croft, wh) placed the matter so seriously before him that Mr Wad6son and the Assistant-clerk at nem, sought an interview with the Town Clerk and W ater Engineer. Sections of the Cor- poration Act and the position of affairs having been placed before these geatlemen, the Town Clerk at once admitted the liability of the Corporation to provids for present requirements (wAthout prejudice to future act,ion) and promised' attention with the least possible delay. The Assistant clerk had since written for confirmation of these arrangements and had received the fallowing letter :—Town Clerk's Office, Warrington, June 30th, 1902, Dear Sir,—The Water Committee have agreed to the terms provisionally arranged between us for the supply of water to the town- ship of Croft. The Water Engineer will put the matter in hand at once. Yours faithfully, J LYON WHITTLE. Continuing, Mr Sutton said that no doubt the Corpora- tion had been placed in difEculties is connection with the alteration of their systems of water supply at Win- wick, and they could only hope that no further com- plaint would arise respecting the supply of water by the Corporation in substitution of the water lost by the wells in consequence of the operations at Winwick.</p>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE manlier. Dr Sephton repined that during the past month there had been 14 births and 32 deathq. The deith rate was equal to 32 6 per 1,000 against 23, the average of the last three months. Twenty-three of the deaths ocaurred in the County Asylum at Wtnwiek and the death rate, ex- clusive of these. would be 8.6 per 1,000. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ASYLTIM SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>THE ASYLTIM SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>The lospector reported that complaint bad been ma de by Mrs Dumbell, Winwick, of the polluted state of Mill Brook. On his visit the brook was fairly clean, but in wet weather the sewage from the itsylum ran off the laud into the ditches, making them unfit for the use of cattle.</p>
        <p>After sone discussion the InspecZ:or was instructed to again inspect the brook ani alsolto intervie he doctor sine Asylum. THE ERODE AT BURTONVirOOD.</p>
        <p>The following appeared in the Inspector's report The brook at Buttonwood is again polluted, and in RS present State is a great nuisance. A large filtering bed Is being put down at the brewery, and I expect the work will be completed to-day. The INSPECTOR said a good efilivat was now coming fri.m the brewery, and he was of the opinion that the pollution was owing to the brook " not having time to make itself clear,"</p>
        <p>The Crum/sun remarked that Mr Forshaw had done everything he possibly could to abate the nuisance. The INSPECTOR was instructed to present a further report at the next meeting, COUNT Y COUNCIL GRANTS. The CLERK sub rnitted an agreement with the Lancashire County Council for the payment by them to the Council of the sum of £1,596 towards maintpnance, &amp;c., of main roads, and a special gtant of £1 006 as follows :—New kerbing and channelling Winwick-read, between ()la m Brook and Middle-lane, £100; re-construction Winwick- road between Long-lane and Sandy-lane, £4OO ; extra macadam, Winwick-road, £3OO ; new kerbing and channelling and half-cost fligging up to 6 feet wide from Sankey Bridges to Efoughtes Works entrance on condition Banal District Council will complete the flag- ging of the remainder of the width, £lO6 ; kerbing and channelling and forming footpath where widened, Liver- pool-road, £lOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>IMPROVEMENTS.</head>
        <p>IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The recommendations of the committee for improve- ments were submitted. Tee Council approved them all witn the exception of one suggested fur Poulton, the consideration of which was, at the request of Mr Mather who was unable to attend, adjourned until the next meeting.</p>
        <p>rhere was no other business of public) interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MAZAWATTEE TEA</head>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE TEA</p>
        <p>be delivered to purchasers in sealed tin-foil packets. No particle of quality is lost in transit or handling. With ordinary teas that are Imperfectly packed or not packed at all, there is a loss of a considerable part ef their original value by exposure to atmospheric influences. Mazawattee Tea has all its first fragrance conserved under seal.</p>
        <p>LORD KITCHRITER'S TRIUMP:</p>
        <p>Never has an Englishman received a greater ovation from his brother Britons than fell to the lot of Viscount Kitchener last Saturday, when he got home from the front after his particularly arduous, but eminently successful spell of service in South Africa. London rose to greet him to a man, and the pent-up enthusiasm of the last few weeks broke out over the whole town. If fact, Saturday was kept very much as a holiday in the capital of the Empire; and in the evening many of the illuminations to which London has grown quite accustomed during the day-time, were lit up in honour of the great General's return. One of the most noticeable features of the whole proceedings was the conspicuous modesty of the hero of the hour; and far from advertising himself, it may be said that— he endeavoured to keep almost unfairly in the back- ground. With his usual promptitude and punctuality, at 9 a.m. Lord Kitchener landed at Southampton Docks from the Orotara, which had been anchored off Netley on the Friday night, only he and his staff being allowed to leave the ship, which was flying the yellow flag to indicate a case of small-pox on board. The band played "The Conquering Hero" as the General was met by the Mayor and Corpora- tion, who escorted him to the Hartley Institute, where he was to be enrolled as a freeman of Southampton, which boasts a great list of names on its roll. En route the inhabitants gave him a foretaste of his London reception, and in the Hall a speech was insisted upon by his vociferous admirers. This was soldierly and short in the extreme, Lord Kitchener speaking simply as the mouthpiece of the rank and file of the Army, and eschewing absolutely the personal element of the situation. All honour done to him he plainly showed was only accepted by him as honour done to Tommy Atkins, whose fortunate repre- sentative he happened to be—a modest attitude which is not characteristic of all our generals by any means. Thence Lord Kitchener drove to the South-Western station, which was reached at 10.45. At Basingstoke a halt was made for the purpose of hitching on a Great Wsstern engine, as at this function the train left</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY.</p>
        <p>As a postscript to the Euripidean adage the unexpected always happens, it might be added that the expected sometimes happens in a very unexpected fashion. At least, this is true of Lord Salisbury's resignation which has been rumoured off and on for over a year now and has been expected—journalistically, at all events—at any time; but the news in Monday's papers came like a thunderclap, so little was it anticipated before the Coronation, fixed to take place between the Bth and the 12th of next month. Naturally Lord Salisbury, on the principle of nobles.ge oblige, could not go out of office while the war in South Africa lasted; and then, at its conclusion, the Coronation appeared so imminent that the Marquis decided to wait until after the great event. Then came the now historic postponement: and though it is confidently hoped that his Majesty will be crowned within a month, the Premier did not care to continue to steer the ship of state even for that short period. However, it is pleasant to be able to announce that the resignation is in no way due to special ill-health. as Lord Salisbury is considerably better than he was a few months ago, but merely to the natural desire of an elderly man for rest after a very long spell of hard work. The war has unquestionably been a great strain on Lord Salisbury, and the loss of his wife, the faith. ful companion of more than forty years. was an even greater blow ; and since then his one desire has been to get out of harness. But before this no suitable opportunity has offered, and it is signi- ficant of the urgency of the noble Marquis's desire that not only did he not care to retain his great position until after next month's historic ceremony, but placed his resignation in his Majesty's hands on Friday at the first interview granted by the King after his operation. The following day the King sent for Mr. Balfour, setting at rest all the many rumours as to Lord Salisbury's successor in the Premiership. Almost every prominent member of the Cabinet has been mentioned in this conneetion, and undoubtedly the Duke of Devon- shire, if not Mr. Chamberlain also, had clms, which, however, he preferred to waive in Mr. Balfour's favour, as he did not wish to be forced to attend Parliament every day. However, there is no prospect of any immediate split in the Unionist Cabinet, but a certain amount of recon- struction will necessarily take place. For instance, at the meeting of the party on Monday it was an- nounced that Sir Michael Hicks-Beach, the Chancellor of the Exchequer, would not continue in office longer than was necessary for Mr. Balfour's convenience, not from any feeling of dissatisfaction at being passed over, but because he too was anxious to retire from public life, which is hardly to be wondered at after she three trying Budgets he has had to father since the outbreak of the war. On the other hand, he spoke most warmly of the new Premier and their long connection, and promised his continued support in an unofficial capacity. The Marquess of</p>
        <p>DRIVE THROUGH LONDON. the L•oui,ll-Westkru tine tor ate other great rail- road in order to arrive at Paddington, and here an address was presented. The Great Western terminus was reached punctually at 12.45 where the scene was very gay and bright. The whole of the arrivvl side of the station was gaily decorated, and on the platform a most distinguished and la 'Want group awaited the arrival of the train- 11opay and the leading members of the head- quarters staff being particularly conspicuous. The Prince of Wales, representing the King, was the first to greet Lord Kitchener, and then his Royal Highness drove off to be at St. James's Palace to receive the guest of the day. During the wait Sir John Aird presented an address on behalf of Paddington, and many greetings were exchanged. Then began the triumphal drive through the Park by Buckingham Palace and the Mall to St. James's, Sir John French and Sir lan Hamilton accompanying Lord Kitchener. All along the route the crowds were very great, and the cheering terrific, Lord Kitchener saluting constantly. At Buckingham Palace the Queen, with the Princess of Wales and Prince Eddy, were on a balcony waving their handkerchiefs, an honour which the General acknowledged with marked salute and a smile. At St. James's Palace the luncheon passed off most pleasantly, the Prince of Wales proposing Lord Kitchener's health in a very neat little speech, and his lordship responding with soldierly brevity. Afterwards he was granted the supreme honour of a special interview with the King—almost the first since the operation—and was invested with the Order of Merit. Lord Kitchener afterwards drove to Mr. Ralli's house in Belgrave Square, which is his usual head quarters in town • and early on Sunday ,morning ran down to Hatfield to spend the day with Lord Salisbury. London, however, did not settle down so quietly, and kept up its festivity all through the evening; but its pleasure in greeting the idol of the hour was obviously spontaneous and very genuine—not merely the excuse for a day off and a night out. It is doubtful indeed whether any function, with the exception of the Jubilees, has ever given the town more pleasure than the welcoming of Lord Kitchener home.</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR. Salisbury has undoubtedly left a greater mark on the history of the last half-century than any living man, as not only has he held the first post in the state for a longer period than any other statesman of the age, but he has always shown himself a keen administrator in foreign affairs. 'Born on February 3rd, 1830, a younger son of the second Marquis of Salisbury, the Right Honourable Robert Arthur Talbot Gascoigne Cecil, as a younger son, had no great prospects in life after his Eton and Oxford career, during which his ability had been noted. For a while he went out to Australia, but returned in 1853, and entered politics that same year by being elected member for Stamford in the Conservative interest. This seat he retained until he succeeded to the title in 1868, his elder brother, Viscount Cranbourne, having died in 1865, an event which made a greu difference to his v- ;ton in life. Previously, iW</p>
        <p>SIR MICHAEL HICKS-BEACH, WO IS RESIGNING THE CHANCELLORSHIP OF THE EXCEEQUEE.</p>
        <p>1857, Lord Robert Cecil had married the daughter of Baron Alderson, the famous judge, and for years they added considerabl/ to a somewhat exiguous income by jounialism,Lerd Salisburyhim- self contributing regularly to the Saturday Review on foreign subjects. t. The year after his brother's death he became Secretary of State for India in Disraeli's administration, holding the office for a year, and resuming it again from 18744875. •</p>
        <p>her</p>
        <p>1876 Lord Salisbury was Special Ambassador al the conference on Eastern Affairs at Constantinople and two years later attended the Congreesoflterlinl as second Plenipotentiary for Great Britain, bring- ing back with him the celebrated peace with honour." In that same year, 1878, he became Secretary Foreign Affairs until 1880, and having succeeded to the leadership of the Conservative Party at the death of Lord Beaconsfield, took office as Premier in 1885, keeping the Secretaryship for Foreign Affairs in his own hands. In 1886 ha again took office as Premier and First Lord of the Treasury, assuming the position of Foreign Secretary a year later and holding office till 1892. Out of office till 1895, upon returning Lord Salisbury held the same important posts ; but upon being again returned with a majority at the general election last year, he passed the Secretaryship of Foreign Affairs on to Lord Lansdowne. fading the nominal post of Lord Privy Seal, and directing the Cabinet. Altogether, Lord Salisbury has been Premier for five thousand days, passing Mr. Gladstone's record eighteen months ago. Mr. Balfour has served as his uncle's lieutenant in the Lower House for many years, and has filled many offices during his twenty-eight years of Parliamen- tary life. Born in 1848, Mr. Balfour entered the House of Commons as M.P. for Hertford in 1874, and, after acting as private secretary to Lord Salisbury, became President of the Local Govern- ment Board in 1885. He has also been Secretary for Scotland, Vice •President of the Board of Education (Scotland), Chief Secretary for Ireland, Foreign Secretary (for a brief period in Lord Salisbury's absence), Leader of the House of Commons since Mr. W. H. Smith's death. He is now in his prime and has had wonderful Parliamentary experience for his age, and few men have ever entered the office of Premier under more favourable auspices. He is extremely popular with every section of the House, even the Irish, and he will continue to control it, as he will not, for the present at any rate, go to the Upper House. He is a man of considerable natural ability, but is not noted for great application, but this his new position will enforce. Everything, however, points to his being a most suitable and successful Prime Minister, and he carries with him the goodwill of his opponents as well as his supporters into his new sphere.</p>
        <p>The Coronation Bazaar held at the end of last week at the Botanical Gardens, Regent's Park, was the most phenomenal success of the kind ever organised, and the result will be a very big cheque for the Children's Hospital in Great Ormonde Street, in aid of which it was got up. The work connected with the bazaar was very considerablei but was most competently and thoroughly carried out by the committee, and the lady-helpers, all prominent women in Society, excelled themselves in the good cause. The</p>
        <p>principal stall- holders were Mrs.</p>
        <p>Arthur Paget, who presided</p>
        <p>over a- wonderful</p>
        <p>tent of . jewels,</p>
        <p>the Duchess of</p>
        <p>Sutherland, Prinoess Henry a _ Pleas, __ the</p>
        <p>Duchess of Marl-</p>
        <p>borough, Lady Tweedmouth,</p>
        <p>and countless others, all of whom worked</p>
        <p>unremittingly and fairly coined</p>
        <p>money. Madame Melba organised MRS. KRTHITR PAGED.</p>
        <p>a great concert, at which Signora</p>
        <p>Caruso and Renaud and the Misses Palliser sang and Mlle. Heritesova played the violin, while at another Kubelik and Madame Albani appeared under the management of Mrs. Walter Palmer. Her Majesty came in person to open the bazaar, accompanied by many members of the royal family, which made the opening day pardon/arty brilliant. She was received by the Committee and escorted all round the bazaar, making several pur- chases on her tour. One of the prettiest events of the day was the presentation of a souvenir of the bazaar to the Queen by little Baroness Clifton, who is only three years of age, and was " helping " at her mother's stall. Altogether, the great Coronation Bazaar was an even greater success than was antici- pated by the most sanguine. One of the most interesting topics of the hour is the question of the Marquess of Hopetoun's suo- cessor at the Antipodes, and the issue has now been fined down to three possible candidates, of who= One is regarded as almost a certainty, unless he should refuse the post, which has certainly been offered to him. Lord Brassey and Lord Carrington have been on everybody's tongue since Lord Hopetonn's resignation of the Governor-Generatsbip of Federated Australia, especially the latter, whe was nicknamed "Popular Charlie" during a previous Governorship in Australia, and thoroughly won the hearts of the Colonials; but neither it seems are so likely ultimately to fill this highly important positionin the Empire .as Colonel 31cCalmont, M.P. The last named is ..st llousehold word throughout the country, andra</p>
        <p>LORD BRASSEY. LORD CARRINGTON. very popular sportsman owing to his great vic- tories on the turf, especially with Isinglass. who won the triple crown" a few years back, but though his popularity is undoubted. and he has other qualifications, his appointment must be looked upon as an experiment inmany ways. Colonel " Harry " McCalmont has been decidedly one of fortune's darlings, as, when a young and not too wealthy an officer in the Guards he came into an unexpected fortune of several millionsfrom an uncle. who. had been expected to leave his money to another branch of the family. Soon after- wards Colonel McCalmont won the Derby al- most at the outset of his career on the turf, and netted more money with Isinglass than has ever been won by one horse before. Then he went into politics and won the seat at Newmarket. for the Conservatives, and, when the war broke out and militia regiments were asked to volunteer, he went to the front in command of the 4th Warwickshires, and has only recently returned. If he accepts the Governor-Generalship of Australia, it will be his first big appointment, but there is every indication that he will till it well and make a popular and able administrator—certainly he has youth and energy on his side. The position will, ipso facto, carry a peerage with it. The latest attraction at the Empire is Mlle. Odette Dulac, the latest importation from Paris, where she has made a great hit and is looked upon as a second Yvette Gilbert. In fact, she is the most up-to-date craze of the volatile French, and the management of the Empire have shown. great enterprise in securing her services, Up-to-</p>
        <p>date, however, as are her songs in Paris, they</p>
        <p>seem a iittl too Continents, for our morestaii</p>
        <p>capital, and _fell</p>
        <p>Grundy's ap proval, so much</p>
        <p>so that Mlle. Odette Dulac has found herself</p>
        <p>compelled to cur tail her repertoire</p>
        <p>by e exclusion of one or two of</p>
        <p>COL. MC CAL3IONT.</p>
        <p>favourite</p>
        <p>songs, including the famous ".Te anis bete." rage of Paris. However, the substitutes are excellent, and she has made a decided impression over here too and is very well received nightly. Mlle. Odette Dulac is pleased with her success. but not so pleased at the limitations placed on lyrics is ,Londoht</p>
