      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>JULY 18th. 1902</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY'S resig- Lord Salisbury and his Successor. nation hardly comes as a surprise, though it is none the less an event of the first political importance. The veteran had long baen in failing health, his grip of affairs had perceptibly slackened, and his speecheg, once so alert, so luminous with their rather bitter wit, seemed to fall langui.lly from a wearied physique and an overstrained' brain. He is followed in his retirement by the respect of all parties, not unmixed with a somewhat distant affection. He was one of the great figures of the more dignified ago which produced a Gladstone and a Disraeli; and while he leaves behind him statesmen of undoubted intellect, not one of them is built on the same great lines. He had his qualities and his defects. He is the last of the Tories. He never concealed his distrust of the people, his contempt for Parlia- ment, and his half-tolerant, half-cynical disbelief in the importance of any sort of legislation. He presided over Governments which tinkered at labour and temperance reform. He accepted the changes which were forced upon him, not so much because he thought they might do good, as be- cause he believed that no Act of Parliament ever produced much effect in any direction whatever. His was not a sympathetic nature, and he seemed to care as little for liberty as for social ameliora-</p>
        <p>tion. Bat, on the other hand, he stood splendidly' aloof from popular passion. He never truckled to a majority or tickled the ears of a mob. If he had the prejudices of an aristocrat, he was abundantly endowed with the virtues of a noble caste—inde- pendence, dignity, and self -respect. The late war was not of his making. It came when old age had slackened his powers of control, and against it one may set off important services to the cause of peace. Against Lord SaLissaav's successor there can be no personal objection. Mr. BaLroua, has a gift of exciting regird, almost affection. which amounts to something akin to genius. It is, in- deed, his one claim to leadership. Ia many ways he is more enlightened than his uncle—more sympathetic in his attitude to social questions,and more tolerant and kindly in his handling of Ire- land. But it seems a doubtful experiment to place at the head of affairs a man whose chief characteristic is constitutional indolence. His leadership of the House of Commons has been, on the whole, a rather .grotesque failure. With all his slackness, he is not respectful of the rights of is opponents, and be makes no attempt whatever to master detail. But no bet ter choice was open. The DUBE of DEVONSHIRE exaggerates the same faults and is disqualified by his seat in the Lords. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S accession would have plunged us into endless difficulties abroad. His loyalty to Mr. BALFOUR, by the way,: deserves a passing word of appreciation ; one could wish that certain brilliant persons on our own side were equally faithful to their elected leader. For the rest, the resignation of Sir MICHAEL Hicks-BExen is an event only second in importance to the retirement of Lord SALISBURY himself. It may be an affair of a purely personal significance, but, on the other hand, one cannot but view with dismay the dis- appearance of a man who was, with all his involun- tary inconsistencies, a staunch pillar of Free Trade and sound finance. With him goes the only Con- servative force which stood between Mr. CRAM- BERLAIN and his dream of a Customs-Union. The old Tory party has all but ceased to exist. The new Unionism is committed to a more dangerous because a more interesting blend of Imperialism and Protection.</p>
        <p>POLITICIANS are already The Balfour beginning to watch for Some ochewanegpeepeirn Administration. signs policy of the Government. 6 cotmlie- mentaries on Mr. BALvouß's accession to the Premiership have been unable to get away from ths phrase "An Amnrath to Amurath succeeds." They insist that the old-fashioned Toryism which distinguishes Lord SALISBURY, in his attitude, for example, to questions of domestic legislation, will survive Mr. BALvorit. But Mr. BALIOTTIL is much more nearly in touch with popular sentiment, and there is a belief gro wing up that he will be more disposed to consider it. Within the las:, conple of days a Bill to give County Conn' cils power for promoting private Bills has been introduced very quietly by a responsible Minister. It has been pigeon-holed because of Lord SALISBURY'S distrust of the County Councils as new-fangled institutions. Perhaps the move- ment may be taken as a sign of the moderning of old Conservative antipathies. On Wednesday, too, Mr. BALYOUR foreshadowed some concessions in the Education Bill. They do not remove the conscientious objection which Nonconformists have to rate-aid for dogmatic religious teaching, but they show that Mr. BALroirs. to some extent</p>
        <p>perceives the force of their objections, and wishes r at least to reduce their sting. Liberals will be glad of any action that tends to reduce the bitterness of religious controversy, and make the Bill less perni- cious. The exact nature of the modifications which the new PRIME MINISTER in his unfettered judg- ment ,will make in the Bill remains to be de- clared. But he is favourable to Parish Council representation, which will give parents in each locality a voice in the management of voluntary schools, arid that is something. It seems amazing that the people who are in,ist nearly concerned in the education given in the schools should have had to clamour through their Par- liamentary representatives for this right, but at one time they appeared to give little pros- pect of obtaining it. Gradually the scales are falling from the eyas of the Government, and they are realising how impudent is this attempt to run the parish schools at the expense of the nation, and leaving them in the control of the parson. Mr. BALvouu has only begun to understand the education problem since the Parliamentary de- bates on the Bill ; and we suspect that as he realises what it means his sympathy with the ex- treme clerical view diminishes. He is not a bigot, and has not much relish for sacerdotalism. if Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CIIAMBERLAIN had their way clericalism would receive short shrift. On other questions than education the Balfour administra- tion may prove enlightened and progressive. We are most afraid of it on its economic side.</p>
        <p>IT is a good sign at the Protection of Child Life.. beginning of the twen- tieth century that the In- ternational Congress in the iaterest of the children is exciting so much attention. The series of meet- ings at the Mansion House began this week, land the delegates from all parts of the world seem to havelbeen practically unanimous on the first reso- lution. This was to enforce a minimum age, below which no child should be allowed to attend an elementary school. The tendency of all ad- vanced educationalists is to delay the commence- ment of instruction by book learning. The first quality to be strengthened in the budding mind is the faculty of observation. The most erudite of mankLid marvel at its freshness and vitality when It is encouraged to develop spontaneously. The primary idea of education, as the word implies, should be "to draw out," but,unhappily,for many '1 generations the word has beco no associated with ' odious and constrained tasks, and prejudice against the acquirement of knowledge has taken the place of the natural thirst. The time must come in the life of everyone when the spurs must be applied, but the mistake is in trying to drive the foal. Many educationalists of to-day affirm that children should not be taught to read and write until they are eight years old and, indeed, a highly-suggestive paper was given some little time since at a meeting of the British Association on the comparative intelligence of native and European children showing the advan- tage of the former. Of course, as age advances the benefits of our systems and hereditary qualities tell in favour of the highly-civilised nations. There is no suggestion in this, however, that the infant soil should be left barren of Kindergarten teach- ing. A more liberal cultivation of the eye and ear will bring a full reward; and the childish imagina- tion may be quickened to study without any forcing or hard-grinding if tactful plans are adopted. Of course, so far as it effects the poor the question is intimately related to the housing problem. Many young children are sent to school prematurely because their parents want to get them out of the way in the daytime, and the pre- mature discipline and herding together in school buildings must be objectionable. The Gdrmans,al- - they have an unfortunate way of dolling the youth of their land in the "teens "by over-reading and over-examination, are reasonable towards the ' young. They pay more attention to playgrounds, to zoological gardens, and to athletic exercise than we do as affecting the small children. A good deal was said at the meeting by Mr JAMES CarcatToar about the dwarfing and enfeeblement caused by under-feeding,but iris not to be seriously urged that the State can discharge the responsibility of parents in that regard. It iA affirmed that one reason why the children of pauper aliens grow up ' healthier than those of the Britishers in similar `. circumstances in the East End of London is that the alien mothers suckle their young. We cannot expect an International Parliament at the Mansion House to discuss the merits and de- merits of artificial foods for infants, but it was only right and proper that the importance of main- taining the domestic interest should be inculcated. There is a danger of decadence in the decline of maternal concern and in the soul-deadening con- dition of life among the poor in our large towns.</p>
        <p>The first of the Twsntieth Century Free Church Funds is also the largest. When Wes- leyan Methodism essayed the task of raising one million guineas there were not a few who thought they were aiming at "shooting the stars." Yet they have almost realised that stupendous sum. Before Conference meets the amount in hand will be well over £900,000, and the total amount of promises reaches £928,803. If to the former sum be added the £52,500 raised in Ireland and the £20,000 in the foreign mission districts, then it may be regarded that the aim has been realised. It appears that at the Mill Conference it was understood that the contribltions from the two latter sources would be included, but latterly only the amounts given by British Methodism are regarded as contributed to the fund. The interest on the sum remitted to the central office already amounts to nearly £35,000,but against this there are the working expenses estimated at £50,000. Liverpool is one of the largest coatributing dis- tricts, it having sent to the central office over £60,00 and promised £70,000 altogether. Man- chester district has remitted £51,000.</p>
        <p>The scanty news which arrives from South Africa is not without its serious note of warning. Some of the financial arrangements for the repatriation of the Boers are being grossly mis- managed. By the treaty of peace we agreed to give the Boers £3,000,000 as" a free grant "from the" British Government" in order to repair the damage of the war and rebuild the burnt farms. It has now been decided, presumably hy Lord Milner, that this sum shall be reckoned as a dent incurred by the two new Colonies, so that the men who profit by it as parties to the settlement will in due course be required to repay it, with interest, as taxpayers of the two Colonies. /4o wonder the Boers stigmatise this proceeding as "a breach of faith." It is, if the facts are rightly reported, a mean and dishonourable evasion. This species of stupidity will react very seriously on our own interests. At present we are on our trial in South Africa—every official act will be judged as a clue to our intentions and as an omen for the A few mean and harsh decisions at this jfuuutncrteu're would suffice to set the Boers scheming how best to win back some day and in some form or other the independence they have lost.</p>
        <p>Of all the incidental pageants for which the Coronation that was to have been gave occasion,</p>
        <p>quite the most gorgeous was the reception of the , Indian Princes at the India Office. The court- yard had been turned into an Oriental palace. Thousands were lavished on the decorations. 311-othing more splendid could have been devised to do honour to the Indian erinces who formed the centre of the picture. It was hospitality on a scale of which even this wealthy country need not be ashamed. Yet gorgeous, lavish, wonderful as the show was, it cost us nothing. India pays for the entertainment of her own delegates,the guests whom the King invited. The pillars and the gilt and the antique furniture, the flowers, the wines, and the tapestries, are all of them to be .found by the taxpayers of our wretched depend- ency, now entering on its fourth consecu- tive year of famine. A small matter, you may say, but it is typical enough of the spirit which governs all our dealings with our poor de- pendency. When in the pursuit of our policy of expansion we wage a war to enlarge our frontiers, it is India that pays. In time of peace we maintain at India's expense a garrison so much in excess of her needs that we can spare from it not only 5,000 men for the African campaign, but an even largernumber for China. And with it all, when in her direst need the Kaiser and the American public subscribed to the famine fund, not a soli- tary guinea was contributed from the Imperial Exchequer. Year by year, in pensions to civil servants and interest on loans, we drain some thirty millions ef tribute frem the famishing country. And all the while we comfort ourselves with the pleasing fiction that we are bearing " the white man's burden," and governing India for her own good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY JFLY 18. 1902</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse, on Wednesday morning. Dr. Patter- son presided and there were also present .—Mrs Clay, R3O R 51,tti0.,w3, Dr. Murray, Messrs. S D fine% T H Ashcroft, A J Jolley, A Brookee, A Jolley, T Halton„l Backhouse, W Houghton, R Forshaw, J Payton, U PT- ing, with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk), F Gate (workhouse muster). A. Wright (superintoadent Padgate School). TREATS TO CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The SUPERINTENDENT of the Padgate Industrial Schools reported that a special treat, as directed by the Guar- dians, was given to the children on July 3rd. The Cheshire Lines Engineers gave their annual treat to the boys cu July 12, Each boy received a priz3 or present, and" a great quantity of things were sent for the gir.e." Mr. A. J. doLLET alluded to the kindness of Mr Connor who had offered to give the children a treat in continua- tion of the extra trear, arranged for when the Corona- tion festivities were being organized. It would, he said, take place on the following Friday. Toe children would leave Padgate Schools at one o'clock, drive throu,l3 Warrington, to Cherry-lane, Lymm, and afterwards pro- ceed to a farm at High Legh where arrangements had been made by a relative of the Superiotendent's to provide tea. A 'bus would leave Mr. Connor's at two o'clock for the convenience of those Guardians who wished to join the children. The CHAIR3IAN said the Board were vary therletil to Mr. Connor for his kindness. The thanks of the Guar- dians would b?.. conveyed to him by letter. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>OVERPAID SUPERANNUATION MONEY. The MASTER reported to the !louse Committee that on loohing over the eup,raonuation account he fouad that the sum of ris 81 had been overpaid by W Brobbin. It ws decided co refund the money. Mr A BROOKES moved the confirmation of the minutes which were approved.</p>
        <p>THE TREATMEN I' OF SANE EPILEPrICS. The CLERIC reported that Oil committee meeting in connection with the Paor Law Conference, hell at Man- chester; on Tuesday, July Bth was attended by the Cnairman, Mr Brookes (senior vice-chairman) and the Assistant-Clerk. The annual conference would be held on the 26tn and 27th September under the presidency of the Right !Inn Sir John T Hibbert. Mr A J JOLLEY said he also attended the committee meeting when the Bull 3ct of the treatment of epileptics was discussed. The committee had the benefit of the presence of Mr Jenner-Fust, who took a warm interest in the proaeedings,and he (Mr Jolley) thought there was good reason to hope that after the deliberations of the committee, and the consideration of the matter at the forthcoming conference, some permanent scheme for the treatment of sane epileptics might be evolved and put in a form to be submitted to the Guardians for criticism or acceptance as the case might be.</p>
        <p>A MEMBER ABSENT FOR SIX mosras. TEO CLERK called attention to the fact that Mr Pater Cunliffe, one of the Guirdians for flaydock township, had not attended any Board meeting for more than six months. By the Local Government Ac, 1894, Sec. 46, Sub sec, (6), it was enacted that "If a member of a Council, or of a district, other than a borough, or of a Board of Guardians, is absent from meetings of the Council or Board for more than six months consecu- tively, except in case of illness or for some reason approved by the Council or Board, his office shall on the 1 expiration of tho,o months become vacant." Mr Sutton ' added that Mr Cunliffe had had a copy of the agenda convening the present meeting, in which the fact of his six months' sb3ence was recorded, and he must, therefrre, be aware that bia case was under consideration that morning. It was decided that the Clerk should draw Mr Cunliffe's attention to his long absence from the Board meetings before taking any further action in the matter, which will tie discussed at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>THE RESIGNATION OF VACCINATION OFFICERS. The Local Government Board wrote acknowledging the receipt of the Clerk's letter of the 19:h ult with reference to the proceedings of Messrs B Hobson and G Belford as vaccination officers in the Warrington Union. As regarded the first paragraph of the letter. the Board directed the assistant to enquire whether Mr Hobtion had sent in his resignation as vaccination officer. Aa re- garded Mr Holford, the Board had decided in view of all the oircumstances. mentioned in the letter to give him another opportunity of redeeming his promises of amendment tend( accordingly they accented his ex- planation on the present occasion. The Board trusted the Guardians would in future, pursuant to article 28 of the Vaccination Order 1898, take steps to ascertain from time to time that the vaccination officers are carrying out their duties satisfactorily. Mr J G Powotam. of Great Sankey wrote applying for the position vacated by Mr Hobson. The CLErat said the application was prematore as the Board had not advertised for applications. The Clerk proceeded to read a communication from the clerk to the Buttonwood Parish Council asking the Board to petition the Registrar-General for the establishment of a separate sub-registration office for Burton wood, The Clerk, in referring to this suggestion, said it was a very difficult matter to induce the Registrar-Gsneral to multi- ply districts. There must be very strong reasons ad- vi nced before the Rogistrsr-General would consent to the division of a reg'stration area. Mr. FORSII&amp;W said that when the matter was before the Board on a previous occasioo, the Clerk advised him to wait until Mr Hobson resigned. Be thought the time had now arrived when steps should be taken to get Buttonwood made into a sub-registration district. It j was an important district which was likely to be very much developed in the near future. The population was rapidly increasing, and the assessable value of the dis- trict was now £20,000. It was very inconvenient fer the people of Burtonwood to walk tour or five milesto Penketh for registration purposes. There was no railway accommodation, and the people were obliged to go to Penketh before nine o'clock in the morning or six o'clock at night. There was a strong feeling in the parish about the matter and Colonel Pilkington had seen the Registrar General on the subject but nothing came of it. If the Guardians would give the inhabitants their support in the matter be thought some progress might be made. If Bartonwood could not have a registration district of its own, it would he more convenient to be included in the Earlestown dis- trict. He wenn therefore move that application be made to the Registrar-general to form a separate regis- tration district for Buttonwood or failing that the inclusion of Buttonwood in the Earlestown district. The CmairtoreN said be could quite hes r out the remarks of, Mr Forsha.w with reference to the inconveni- ence of the present arrangement with reference to registration. The people of Buttonwood were greatly inconvenienced and if it was impossible to have a separate registration district it would be advisable I. have the registrar living in the Buttonwood district. Mr ASUCROFT seconded the proposition, and said an alteration was greatly needed. Mr A 3 JOLLEY said he did not want to oppose the wishes of the inhabitants of Buttonwood, but it was almost impossible to get a division of districts where the population was not very large, The Board tried to get the inclusion of Latchford in the Warrington district but failed to do so, A great deal coo pressure bad been brought to bear on the Registrar General with reference to the separation of Buttonwood as a sub-district and nothing had come of it. It Was extremely doubtful whether any good would be done by the discussion whichload just taken place. The last time he(6lr Jolley) saw Colonel Pilkington on the matter,Colonel Pilkington seemed to think it was quite hopvless to obtain a repar- ate sub-district for Buttonwood. Councillor Jolley moved an amendment tkat the Board proceed to adver- tise for candidates to succeed Mr Hobson as registrar for Cuordley, Penketh, Great Sankey, and Buttonwood. and also as public vaccinator for the district, a sondition</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>STRANGE FATALITY FATALITY AT HALTON.</head>
        <p>STRANGE FATALITY FATALITY AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, Mr R Dabson, coroner, opened an inquest up n the body of Jane Grice, aged 28 years, wife of Thomas John Gibe, farm labourer, Halton-lodge Cottages, Runcorn, who died on Thursday night. In reply to the Coroner, a gentleman who gave his name as Samuel Heron Johnson, of 6, Whitechapel, Liverpool, said he appeared as an act of courtesy to the Coroner and jury. He was not a qualified chemist, but a.whole- eile eruggise The husband of the deceased said his wife thought she was pregnant, and had said she would get some stuff, same as a neighbour had had. On Wed- nesday she gave him a letter to post for Liverpool. He placed 16 penny stamps inside. He did not know the address. His wife said she was sending for some stuff, but he did net know what it war; for. Be did not know whether slv! got the simff or no'. At 5.30 on Thursday evening he left his wife bright and cheerful, but at a quarter-past six be found her lying down in the yard, foaming at the mouth, and apparently in a fit. She died in his arms. He afterwards fou,d a small bottle over the garden hedge in a potato ficki, and he gave it to the police. He had four children, the eldest being five years and the yeungeet four months. Dr Carruthers said the cause of death was not apparent from the post- mortem examieation. and he had handed the stomach and its contents to be chemically examined. The deceased was in the commencement of pregnancy. The inquest was adjourned for a fortnight for the county analyet's report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRIC I COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRIC I COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly tre,ting of this authority was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Present:—Mr J Knowles (in the chair), Messrs T Wright, C Itlyiance,A H Talbot, E Clarke, T Baxter, T Lewis. 8 Woodward, G Pilling, P Gleave, J Spruce, Admiral Jones-Parry, J Mounfield, J B Chadwick, and T Antwiss with Messrs G F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant cbrk), Dr Adams(medical officerl, J Swinton and Wle (survey ors)and J Far- rington (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RESIONkTION,</head>
        <p>A RESIONkTION,</p>
        <p>Mr James Taylor, member tor Helity, wrote esigning his position as a Councillor owing to his inability to attend any of the Council meetings. On the proposition of Mr Wood ward, notice of motion Wa3 given that the seat be declared vacant. THE RIVERS POLLUTION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Cheshire County Council wrote on July sth in reference to rivers pollution :—Referring to my letter of the 15 h of May last and yours of the follow- jog day I shall be obliged if you will kindly let me know what further steps, if any, have been taken by your District Council to stop the pollution complained of by the County Medical Officer of Health so that I may report the same to the Rivers Pollution Committee which meets on the 17th inst. The Clerk said that in reply he had informed the County Council that notices had loam served for the abatement of the pollution of the brook in Kingsley and that he would also ()train a report from the inspector as to the effect the notices had already had in toe district and would report before their meeting on the 17th. Further that the medical officer had submitted a report to the Council about the pollution ef Halton Brook which would receive the consideration of the committee. He also pointed out what had been done in connection with the streams around Helsby. The inspector in a lengthy report on the subject said that out of 35 notices posted he had only received one reply. The difficulty with the individuals concerned seemed to beast the method they should adopt to pre- vent any alleged pollution. On the proposition of Mr Woodward, It was resolved that the communication of the County Council on the subject of Kingsley Brook be referred to the Parish Council of Kingsley for their consideration.</p>
        <p>THE HEIGHT OF WALTON ARCHES, Mr Joseph Ward, clerk to the Walton Inferior Parish Council, wrote under date 7.h July callmg attention to the height of Walton Arches. The road was being filled which diminished the height so that farmers with a load could not get under as formerly. Mr. Swinton, sorveyor said that there was no doubt that the height from the road had diminished at that particular point. It belonged to the Ship Canal Company and a lot of bull ling bad been geinz on arould the locality. Ele would try to get the contractors together and have the matter put right.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of 'Guardians was held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse. Present Mr A R Ncrman (in the chair),Messrs J Douglas, T Lea. T Reynolds, T Wood, the memnPrs of the Run- corn Distrct Council, with Messrs G F Atleton (clerk), W E Hough;(assistant clerk) J Cunnah (master). T Cook, W DePrez and T Riley (relieving officers.) THE QUARTERLY VACCINATION ACCOUNT. Mr Woodward, Ckairrnan ot the Finance Committee, reported that the outpayments were as follows : Runcorn £BO ; Frodsham. £25 ; Budworth, £9. The fees for the vaccination tor the last quarter showed the following payments, Dr McDougall, £34 123 TI ; Dr Carruthers, £7 12e 91 ; Dr Burton, £35 le 21 ; Dr Bower. £l9 4s Bd. The fees for the vaccination dicers showed Mr Cook, £9 153 51 ; Mr Riley, £4 153 91; Mr DePre z. £5 164 101. Tnis was a net total for the r quarter of £ll9 183 (murmurs of disapprobation). Mr Wright e That is about three times as much as it used to be. Mr Woodward, continuing, said the balanc3 in . hand from last meeting was £2,039 6s 101 ; receipts, £2BO 23 Id ; cheques drawn tut day zuzi 63; balance, £1,771 is lid. The report WAS adopted. A TRIP FOE THE INMATE!. The Master reported that on the 3rd inst. the boys, girls, and old women went to Norton Priory on the in- vitation of Sir Richard and Lady Brooke. On reaching the Priory an excellent tea was partaken of and after- wards the time was pleasantly spent in games and sports. The old women were each presented with a Coronation cup and saucer, and each child received a cup. Previous to leaving, buns were distributed, and the children at home were kindly remembered by' her c ladyship. A. conveyance was provided for tho old \women and children and the cost defrayed by Lady Brooke. The Chairman remarked that the report was a most satisfactory one.</p>
        <p>VOTES or THANES. The House Committee resolved that a vote of thanks be accorded to Sir Richard and Lady Brooke for the treat to the old women and chillren at Norton Priory on the 3rd instant ; that a vote of thanks be also conveyed to Mr Spruce, of Preston-o'clf-Hill, for flowers for the flower service ; that Mrs Hayes, girls and industrial trainer, be allowed fourteen days leave of absence ; thst the invitation from Mrs Hoiden of Antrobus for the Friendly Society Girls to visit Antrobas Vicarage on the I.Tch lost be accepted with thanks. Passed on the motion of Messrs Reynolds and Wood.</p>
        <p>asx for CUNNINGHAIL S HIGH-CLASS MINIMAL WATHRS, —Advt</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY .4. CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Business at the Guardians and the Rural Coun ci was of a very routine ni.ture on Tuesday. The only item which called for special mention was the large amount paid for vaccination. The exact sum will be seen in another column. Meantime the Board can possess itself in patience in the hope that when the present vaccination bill has run its course in some two years the Board's expenditure will be considerably reduced.:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A DISHONEsT YV IDNES BOY. At Liverpool (loaner Sessions on Tuesday, Mary Gaughan, aged auout forty five and Joseph. Snaps (14), were charged with having at Widnes, on the 12th July, stolen a purse containing £5 10s, three postal orders for Li 14s 61, anti 29 postage stamps, the property of William Thomas Yates, of W Idris ~ ruad.—The jury found the female prisoner not utility, and tee boy Snape guilty. Gaughan was uiecharged, the Chairman remarking that there was no stain on her character. Snape,who is absent on ' leave from the Navy, was put back till Wednes- day. . WIDNES TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. The annual exhibition ef scuool work and distri- bution of certificates to the pupils of the Higher Grade School and School of Science, Widnes, took place at the Institute on Wednesday afternoon. Toe Mayor presided, tied Mrs F H Ciossage presented the certificates. Mr Walton, 8.A., principal, read an excellent report. Speecoes were made by Mr II Wade Deacon, J.P., chairman of toe Widnes School Board, Mr D Lewis, and Mr F Neil. MERSEY MISSION TO SHAHEEN. The twenty-seventt, anniversary services in con- nection with the Runcorn Branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen were held on -,sunday. The morning preacher was the Rev ES Oliver, .M.A.,and in toe evening the pulpit was occupi3d by Mr J Stokes, licensed lay reader. A children's service was held in the afternoon, when a sermon was preached by Mr J T Collier, licensed toy reader, Special hymns and anthems including the anthem 'Praise ye the Mighty God,"were sung by the choir and children, assisted at the evening service by Mr Tonks' orchestral baud and MeSSIS Oui,hwaite,sen. and jun. BLACKPOOL SUSINIHR;CAMP. commencing June. at Kirkham's Fstm.—Beakfas flintier, Tea and Supper every aav for a wesa and a good c„uop be 3 for 15s ; week-end Es 6d.—Apply H W uniMN, Secretary, 153, Islington, Liverpool.—Advt. .NATIONAI, CHOOL SUCCESSES. An examination was held at the Technical Institute on Saturday, July 5,,b, for four new Local Science and Art Scholarships, tenable for three years at that school. These sctolarships are worth £36 each, and are the most valuable ones which have been offered to elementary school pupils in this district. There were twenty-one competitors from the various schools in Runcorn. The success ful competitors are George Ralph Clare, Fred Guy Taylor, Arthur Rathbone, and Thomas Howard, all of whom are scholars at the National Boys' school. This result reflects great credit OD the head master, Mr AS :Andsay, &amp;C.F. We congratulate the boys heartily on their good fortune, and hope it is but a precursor of tneir future success. GOOD TEMPLARY AT RUNCOHN. The Runcorn Forward Lodge 2444 held its weekly session on Monday evening last being opened in the usual form by the CT. The minutes of the last session being read and adopted, another new member was proposed and initiated. rile pro- gramme for the t•veaing being a social, the follow- ing items were rendered, chorus "Light in the Darkness ;" part song, "Ring the Boils" Sister Patrick; song, " Friar of Orders Grey," Sister Anders; reading, •' Buttons." Sister Roscoe; song, "Land of my Fathers," Brother Aughes ; address, Brother Lindon of St. Helens; duet," Life for a look," Brother Hughes and Sayle. Programme for next week " Sankey's Hymns" and Prayer. Tile session was closed at nine o'clock by the C.T. FUNERAL, OF THE LATE MRS BUCK. The remains were interred in the Runcorn Come- ! tery on Monday afternoon of the late Mrs Buck, widow of the late Mr Alfred Buck, chemist and druggist, 77, Bridge-street, Runcorn. The deceased lady, who died on Thursday afternoon, at the age of 77, was an eld and respected resident of Runcorn, and belonged to a family well known and highly esteemed throughout the district. She had been an invalid for a number of years, and had been at- tended by Dr Carruthers. Among those who attended the funeral were Mr Harry Buck (son), Dr Carruthers, Mr G Summerfield, Mr Annett, and Mr Andrews. The officiating clergyman was the Key Canon Wood. Wreaths and other floral tributes were sent by Mr Harry Buck, Miss C Buck,and Miss Wh teman.</p>
        <p>ELLESMERE-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. Anniversary services in connection with the Ellesmere-street Sunday School were held in the Methodist Free Church on Sunday, when sermons were preached morning and evening by the Rev II B Coventry, of Birkenhead. There was special music at both services. In the morning the anthem was "I will sing of Thy power" (Sullivan) and in the evening "Praise the Lord " (Elvey). A musical service was held in the afternoon. Mr James Brandreth, of Helsby, presided. Special hymns and a children's anthem were rendered. Solos and recitations were given by the Misses Kitty Rutter, Curzon and Moores, Dora Gandy, Paul, Minnie Stubbs and Ethel Lightfoot, Masters Albert Barker, James Moores and John Parker and Mr T CIUZ3a. Collections were taken at the services in aid of the school funds, and amounted to .£25 6s. RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS AT DELAMERE. The "F" and, G " (Runcorn) companies of the second Earl of Chester's Volunteers took part with the other companies of the battalion in a sham fight ' in Delamere Forest, on Saturday afternoon. The men, to the number of 110, assembled at head- quarters at two o'clock, Captain Ashton in com- mand, and preceded by the band marched to the station and entrained for Delamere via Ilelsby. At Delamere they were joined by the Chester and Sanford companies, and after the battalion was formed up, Colonel Smith, Commanding Officer, presented medal clasps to the ti.en of the first active service company. Sergeant Jeacock was the only man from Runcorn who received a clasp. Five others were unable to be present, and their clasps were handed to Captain Ashton to be presented privately. At the conclusion of this interesting ceremony the sham fight was proceeded with. The outlying companies were the defending force and the headquarters the attacking force. The fight lasted two hours, the outlying companies having retired on the Cheshire Yeomanry camping ground when "cease fire" was sounded. Refreshments were afterwards served out to the men. The Run- corn Volunteers returned at seven o'clock and were home about nine o'clock. The battalion goes into camp on Salisbury Plain for a fortnight's training this week-end. 30,000 rBeeLtoINZ PbayOLLlhßavAlsiadoxtlitoerird-sstifehett Manchester. The most successful Eye Specialist in England. Na has wred after Hospitals. Doctors, and Oculists have failed' Book post free on mentioning this Daper.—Advt.</p>
        <p>GRA WFORD'S CREAM.</p>
        <p>(:) WA C,KIE S</p>
        <p>AND CREANF</p>
        <p>SID CASE AT RUNCORN• At the Runcorn Police Court on Friday, before Messrs E Imison (in the chair) and S Taylor, a young married woman named Ada Handler, living in Ashridge-street,was charged with having stolen a pair of boots, valued at 2s 11id, the property of Messrs Henry Tyler and Sons, Church-street, on July Srd.—Defendant was also charged with steal- ing a sheet valued at 1.8 led the property of Mary Glutton on the 10th July.—The evidence showed that the defendant stole the boots from Messrs Tyler's shop and subsequently pledged them at Mr Lightfoot's, pawnbroker, saying that the boot were too large for her.— In the second case it appeared that Mrs Clutton, prosecutrix, became acquainted with the defendant through being the means ot restoring the defend- ant's boy, who was lost on the sth July, to her. The defendant afterwards applied for lodgings and was shown a spare room. Subsequently the sheet was Imissed.which was fourei in her possession when de- fendant was apprehended for stealing the boots. IThe defendant said elle was very sorry for what she had done. She was short of money and did not know where to get food. The husbaud of the defendant, a hard-working, respectable man, said his wile did not take drink but had got into careless ways.—Defendant was bound over in sureties of .£2 to come up for judgment if called upon. WELSHERS SENT TO GAOL AT WIDNES. Before the Mayor and Alderman Neil, at Widnes Police Court, on Monday, Lewis boloman, of Man- chester, a bookmaker, was charged with obtaining 2e from Thomas Wynne by means of a trick on Friday night, on the occasion of the Widnes Athletic Sports.—Thomas Wynne said be was at the sports, and made a bet with prisoner's "pale' Soloman booking the bet. Witness backed Mercer, tee winner of the mile flat race, for 21, and wnen the race was over Soloman had gone. —Thomas Jacks, Ditton. corroborated the pre- vious witness. Prisoner was also charged with 'obtaining 2s from Edward Hogan at the same time and rdace, Hogan backed Wailing in the 120 yards sprint at evens, and had 4s to draw. Upon going i for his money Selman had gone.—Orlando Kempsey, of Liverpool, and Lewis Soloman were charged together with obtaining 2e from Thomas Jacks. Jacks laid is to liempsey's Is 6d on Mercer in the mile. Mercer won, tied Jacks received 2e, with the promise of 6..1 after the race. Prisoners both denied the charge, Kempsey stating on oath that he had never seen Soloman in his life. Kemp- sey, however, was sent to gaol for one month, and Soloman for one month on each charge, three months in all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HALTON LADIES' CLUB.</head>
        <p>HALTON LADIES' CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the f...'astle Hotel,Halton was celebrated the 86th annivrrsary of the Halton LiLlies' Club. At the present time the club is SR a most prosperous con_ dition financially and numerically. It boasts a member ship of almost three hundred, and is, iu tact, one of the largest and oldest institutions of its kind in the country. It was originally lounded by Lady Brooke's family and in its early career was financially assisted by the farmers of the district until it reached its present healthy condition. The financial statement for last year shows that £lO5 33 was paid in sick money and £4O in funeral money. The balance in baud at the beginning of the year was £3l 9s 41., and the members subscriptions amounted to £14603 63. The total funds of the society at the close of the year amounted to' £1,595 123 101 The management fund account showed subscriptions from Lady Brooke, Mr Charles Reynolds, Lady Brunner, Mrs Charles Reynolds, Mrs Walker, and Mrs Wray. On Monday the weather was all but perfect. After the village had been paraded by the club members, number leg 220, headed by the Volunteer band,in the cust miary form, and Divine Service attended in the Halton Church where a short service was conducted by the Vicar of Timperley, in the absence of the Vicar, the members walked to the bowling green of the Castle Hotel, where tea was provided in a large marquee. Lady Brooke I unfortunately, was unable to be present, and the Vicar, Mrs Wray, and Mr and Mrs C Reynold, were also conspicoue by their atsence. The processionists were accompanied by the Revs S. Wilkinson and C Wray (Aston), Dr and Mrs Carruthers, and Messrs J H Gamon,T Wright.and P R Wright. After the tea, which was excetlealy served by Mr 3 Walker, the landlord of the hotel, Mr Wright said they might congratulate themselves upon having a very beautiful day for the 86th anniversary. 'they re- gretted very much the absence of Lady Brooke,who took the very greatest interest in the club. Had she been able she would certainly have been present. Be had received the following letter from her : " Lady Brooke I regrets very mucii that it is unlikely she will be able to be present at the annual meeting of the Halton Female Friendly Society on Monday, the 14th, as she only re- turns from London on that day. She wishes Mr Wright to convey her regrets to the narmbers, and tier bests wishes for their enjoyment." Mr and Mr Charles Reynolds, both honorary members, who took a great . .</p>
        <p>interest in the club, were also unable to be presen.. Mr Reynolds bad sent the following I.tter " Herewith is Mrs Reynolds' 103 and my two guineas for the manage- ment fund of the Ladies' Club. Many thanks, but there is no chance of our being over at the yearly day." Lady Brunner was also an honorary member. He was interested to read ia the account of the</p>
        <p>laying of the foundation stone of the Runcorn Cottage Hospital that Sir John Brunner said that the first time he visited Runcorn was in response roan invitation from the Halton Ladies' Club. He wrote to Sir John Brunner, and receivedehe following reply from London: "Sir John Brunner is in receipt of your letter of the Btb inst. and much regrets that their engagements in town prohibit him and Lady Brunner from accepting your very cordial invitation to take tea at the Halton Ladies' Club on the occasion of the annual meeting on Monday." He (Mr. Wright) hoped they would have Sir John and Lady Brunner with them some day. Ee had to express regret that Mr and Mrs Wray were not able to be present. They were all very pleased, how- ever, to have Dr and Mrs Carruthers and the R9vg. S Wilkinson and C Wray with them. (Applause.) Mr Gamlen, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Rev S Wilkinson, made an interesting fieancial statement. Be alluded to the fact that last year things were very black in connection with some of tee funds of the club. They tad three, as they hoped, good investments with the Haiten Burial Board, the Great Central Railway Co., and the War Fund. The bulk of their money was in the Great Central Railway Co. For a number of years they received 4, q and 5 per cent, except in 1894,when the great coal strike was on. That year they got nothing,ard it was not generally known that Mr Rey- , nolds paid the interest, a very considerable amount, on that occakion. For two or [Urea recent years the money h4d cone very badly owing to depression throughout , the country, but he was /*aged to say that during the ilast twelve months their money had improved by £2OO land they all hoped that happy condition of things wraild continue. (Applause), The Rev S Wilkinson and the Res C Wray also spoke brit fly, and three cheers wire given for the High Stewardess, Mrs Carruthers. Dr Carruthers returned thanks for his wife. It was mentioned during the proceedings that one of the guests, Mrs.. Elizabeth Both, bad beea a member of the Ladies' Club 57 years, and bad not missed one anniversary celebration. This year Lady Broke is the patrone•s, Mrs Carru- thers the high stewardess, Messrs. J H Gamon and Thos. Wright the trustees, and Mr Percy R Wright the secre- tary. Dancing was continued te a late hour the Hill being visited by a large number of people</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>AGAINST CO-OPERATION.</head>
        <p>AGAINST CO-OPERATION.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Shopkeepers Organise a Boycot</p>
        <p>One of the most remarkable of industrial fights is maturing in the town of St. Helens, and will bear fruit In active hostilities during the next few days. Listo te a battle for supremacy between tho St. Helens Cooperative Society, which is housed in the largest and most ornate buildings in ths borough, and has a membership of about 8,000, and a huge combine, 1 embracing all the tradesmen. I For several years past the tradespeople have been suffering from the inroads made on their profits by the Co-operative Society, and as a eery great extension of the Co-operative Stores has just been completed with a view to capturing still further the general trade of the borough and its environments, one hundred and seventy representatiJe shopkeepers met privately on Monday evening, at the suggestien of the St. Helens Grocers' Association. wth the object of taking the situation into their serious coasideratioo. Trio result was that the aforementioned Combine was formed, and the plan of campaign is at the present mo- ment under the consideration of the legal adviser to the Grocers' Evsociation. If his approval be obtained the matter will be laid before a general meeting of traders. It is understood that the scheme is a great boycott of those people who have dealings with the Co-operative Socirte,and who for many, things have to go to pri- vate shop-keepers. Customers at the Cceoperative Stores are to be boycotted ; tradesmen who do any work for them, together with bankers, doctors, churches, and chapels—indeed, anybody and everybody who are in any way caught adiing to the prosperity of the Co-operative Stores. The Combine are even gong so far as to refuse to give work, or articles of apprenticeship to the sons and daughters of co-operators. lAlready a big list of vict'ma has been prepared, and • huge vigilance committee has been formed to watch aveiy part of the borough with the object of addiag to i this list. , Intense excitement prevails in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BRUI AL ASSAULT AT NEWTON-</head>
        <p>BRUI AL ASSAULT AT NEWTON-</p>
        <p>LE-WILLOWS.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Police Court, on Monday, before D.: Dowling and Mr J Unsworth, Michael Coyne, a bottle- maker, Gas•street, Newton, was charged with unlaw- fully wounding Joseph Hamblen and Wi'liana Birchall ha' striking them on the head with a brass rod on July 13th.—Evidence was given to the egect that prosecu- tors were standing talking together in Gas-street, when prisoner approached them from behind and struck them on the head with a brass rod, rendering them uncon- sciouir—Sergeant Barrow stated that from information be received he arrested prisoner at ten minutes past eleven o'clock at his lodgings. He charged him with wounding the men and be replied : •• That's wrong:" —Prisoner said he had hurt no one. Five or six men met him when be was going to his work and called him names.—The Clerk : And out of revenge you used this rod 7—Prisoner : No, I did not. I never spoke a word to them in my life.—The Clerk : You never spoke to there—Prisoner : No,and I never used the rod .You have taken the wrong mars altogether. I have struck no one in ruylrfe.—lnspe,tor Morgan asked that the prisoner should be remanded to the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday, in order to see hew the injured men went one. Be bad seen the doctor that morning and he said they were goir gon well so far.— Both prosecutors had their heads banclaged.—Prisoner was accordingly remanded. Prisoner was brought before the magistrates at War- rington on Wednesday, and the charges were reduced to those of common assaults. Evidence having been given, a fine of LI and costs with the alternative of a month's imprisonment was , imposed.</p>
        <p>PICNIC FROM NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the members of Newton Congregational Christian Endeavour Society, and the senior scholars of the school, to the number of about 40 left Newton at 1.30 in a char-a-bane and wagonettes for Lymm. On arrival after a most pleasant drive,tea was partaken of at Mrs 'Massey's. The evening was afterwards spent in rambles round the Dam and in other parts of the neighbourhood, which is of a purely rural character. Lymm was left behind at 8 o'clock, Newton being reached at 9.30. The arrangements were eficiently carried c'a t by Mr D Mel:lardy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Attempted Suicide at Go! borne.</p>
        <p>George Heaton, an elderly man oh no fixed address, was charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday with attempting to commit suicide by trying to drown himseit at Gol- borne on the 11th inst. Prisoner admitted the offence.—Stephen Spibey, Lewton-road, Golborne, said he was going to work at, 720 on the date in question, when a woman drew his attention to the tact that a man was in a pit. Witnessjuniped over some rails and saw prisoner in the water. lie got into the pit, which was not very deep, to try and save the man whose bead was under the water. By means of a rope witness end another man pulled him out.—Prisoner now stated that he had been very low-spirited and did not know what he was doing.—He agreed to go into the Workhouse, and the Bench committed him to that institution.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr A J Thorp and His Coronation Hymn._ Mr A J Thorp, of Padgate-road, Warrington,wh' recently received His Majesty's gracious acknow- ledgment for his Coronation hymn sent to His Majesty, has written with four copies of the hymn to His Royal Highness, the Prince of \Vales. Mr Thorp on Tuesday morning received the following gracious acknowledgement :—York House, St James's Palace, S. W• The private secretary to the Prince of Wales is directed to thank Mr Arthur James Thorp for the copies of his Coronation hymn which be has been good enough to send for 11. R. H.'s acceptance, 14th July, 1902 —The Mayor also wrote from Ireland, as follows ;—Tullymore Park,Bryans- ford, Co. Down, 11th July, 1902. Dear Sir,-1 have received with much pleasure a copy of your Coro- nation Hymn and thank you much for your kind thought in sending it me. I much appreciate its high tone and sentiment and feel sure it will rank well with the many other loyal effusions this momentous occasion has brought forth. Believe me, yours faithfully, J Charlton Parr, (S(ayor). LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AWD SHOES. UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—A.dvt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Adv Local Success. _ _</p>
        <p>Wallace Gandy, a student attending the Pupil Teachers' Central Classes, has been successful in passing the recent London Matriculation Examina- tion, being placed in the First Class. He is we be- lieve, the first pupil teacher in the town to pass this examination during the period of apprentice- ship. He is a pupil teacher at People's College.</p>
        <p>SEVERAL SECOND-HAND BICYCLES for e*le cheap. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-et, Warrington.—Advt. The Death Rate.</p>
