      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LATE MR. J. L. TUNSTALL.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. J. L. TUNSTALL.</p>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>At noon on Monday. the grave closed over the remains of the late Mr J L Tunstall, of Heathfield, Grapponhall, whose death was recorded in our last issue. The inter- ment took place in the Warrington Cemetery in the presence of a large number of sorrowing relatives and friends. The Rev Richard Greenan, rector of Grappen- hall, conducted the service. The chief mourners present were Mrs J L Tunstall (widow), Mr Thomas Tunstall (brother), Mr H G Tunstall (brother), and Mrs Tunstall, Mr Wilmot Tunstall (brother), Mr John Slyman (Stret- ford). There were also present at the Cemetery. the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Alderman J Shaw Green, Councillor A Bennett, Councillor L Greening, the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle),the Borough Treasurer (Mr J Fairhurst), and the Deputy Town Clerk kSdr Stanley Wilson). The Warrington Board of Guardians to which body deceased was formerly assistant over- seer, were repressnted by the Chairman (Dr Patterson) and Mr G Pilling (chairman of the Assessireot Committee), Mr W Owen (valuer), and Mr Samuel Sutton (assistant clerk). 'I be Warring on Infirmary and Dispensary, of which institution the deceased was formerly hon. secretary for 16 years, was represented by Mr F Monks, Mr Charles White, Mr George Dakin, Mr John Fairclough, Mr C Jackson Holmes, Dr Fox, Dr Richmond, Dr Bowden, Dr Langdale (the latter two gentlemen being the deceased's medical advisers), and Dr Mort (chief surgeon). Mr J T Looker and Mr Thomas Locker were present on behalf of the firm of Messrs T Locker and Company,Limited,of which deceased was a director. Mr II Isherwood was present on behalf of the directors ef the " Observer "Company, and Mr Loftbouse for the Longford Wire Company. Among others present were : Mr J T Birtles, Mr Percy Silcock, Mr E J Hall (of the Warrington Society), Mr J Longland, Dr Peacocke, Mr 3 W Hayward, J.P., Mr Herbert Hatton, Mr John Harrison. Mr J C Young, Mr J Rymer Young. Mr W 9 Howard, Mr E J Dignum, of Canada; Mr F W Gilbert, Mr C D Parkinson, Messrs Charles Leigh, Thomas Pemberton, G F Freeman, C H Longshaw (office staff of Messrs Tunstall and Company), William D.inteth, W E Hatch and I' Richardson (workmen), and Richard Shackleton and Albert Hare (of the Warrington Bill Posting Company, Limited). A number of handsome floral tributes were forwarded by the following : Mrs J L Tunstall and children, Mr and Mrs T Tunstall, Mr and Mrs H G Tunstall, Mr and Mrs Wilmet Tunstall, Mr and Mrs Theo Tunstall, New York; Mr and Mrs Hardaker and family. Manningharn ; Mr and Mrs Bur- gess, Mr and Mrs H Burgess. Mr and Mrs J IR Young, Mrs Dutton, Mr and Mrs J F Dutton, Mr and Mrs W Dutton, Mr and Mrs J C Young, Professor and Mrs A H Young, Mr and Mrs C D Parkinson, Messrs John, Joe, and Jim Swinton, Captain and Mrs Deacon and Miss Swinton, Mr and Mrs B W Knowles, Bradford; Mr J Davis, Dr and Mrs W I Peecocke, the Misses Greening,the Misses Annie J Browne,Vida Lyon Whittle, May Greening and Dorothy Browne, Mrs and Miss Laverack, Mr and Mrs R T Fairclough. Mrs J Pierpoint, Mrs J E Davies, Mr and Mrs William Owen Mr and Mrs Arthur Browne, Mr and Mrs Thaunan Pemberton, Mr C Leigh, Messrs J and T Locker ; directors of the " Observer " Company, Limited, office staff, Bewsey Chambers ; and workmen and billposters, Queen-street workshop. Messrs J and W Dutton, Sankey-street, had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Result of Playing with Matches.</head>
        <p>The Result of Playing with Matches.</p>
        <p>Charles Sutton, the two-year-old child of Mr and Mrs Sutton, greengrocer, Fennel-street, was ad- Mitted to the Infirmary on Saturday, suffering from burns. It appears that the child Was put to bed at 4 p m., and later on was beard to scream. The mother rushed upstairs and was horrified to find the little one's clothes in flames. A waistcoat eon- taming a box of matches had been left on the bed and the child had abstracted the matches and struck one of them, which had ignited its clothes and the bed. The child succumbed to its injuries on Monday.—An inquiry into the circumstances of the case was held on Tuesday afternoon at the Coroner's Court, before Mr S Brighouse. The only witnesses called were the deceased's mother and Nurse Scott from the Infirmary, after which the jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>F G Thompson was the highest scorer for Northern,22 being the result of his innings. S K Fernie (11), J S Pilling (11), and Shutt (10) also made useful contri- butions.</p>
        <p>The second eleven sustained defeat at Northern 16 being the adverse majority. T B Job (32), G F Bentley (32), and G F Durandu (17) were the top scorers for the winners, whilst for the Warringtonians J Thompson contributed 29, A Duckworth 23, and W Hind (20).</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission eleven had little difficulty in disposing of Lymm Grammar School, compiling 116 to their opponents 67. J Dargon was in splendid form with the bat, knocking up 47, whilst J Cox compiled 31. For the Grammar School, Moulder (28) and Wood (13) were the only players to attain double figures.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers visited Arley, and defeated the eleven of that village by 22 runs. T Barnes (19) and R Brown (13) were the top scorers for the En- gineers, Smith being responsible for 21 for Arley.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's were beaten to the extent of 34 runs by rrington Wesleyans. E Chilton (13) and E Jones (12) were the only batsmen who reached double figures.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Peter Walker and Son's team met Widnes Crescent at the chemical town, and came out victor- ious by 50 runs. Their success was in no small measure due to the efforts of T Wright, who scored 43 runs and captured 4 wickets for 5 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington may claim a promising young cricketer, in the son of Mr. W. Bainbridge, of Froghall-lane. He is a schoolboy at the County, School, Bedford, where they have the benefit of the County ground for the school playground, one of the masters, being the cap- tain of the Bedford County team. Warmsby, the one time well-known Lancashire professional, ,s grounds- man, and Parbury, a schoolboy at the County School, is one of the bowlers for the County team. Young Bainbridge, the " Field " records, scored 16 against Wellingborough Grammar School, and bowled four out. The same week against Bedford he obtained 30 runs. Against the M.C.O he caught two out, and in the return match with Wellingborough Grammar School caught one and bowled one, so that he must be pretty useful all round. He wicket-keeps generally for the school team.</p>
        <p>Cycling. At midnight on Saturday a cyclist named James Carpenter, aged 26, was riding down Buttermarket- street, and when turning into Scotland road he ran into the barricade near the new tramway lines. He was thrown from his machine and severely shaken and bruised. He was removed to the Infirmary for treat- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Lynes, the Warrington professional, took a well- earned "benefit "on Saturday, and there was a large attendance of spectators at Arpley to witness the encounter with the Northern eleven. The Warring- tonians acquitted themselves admirably,and the visitors were fortunate in being able to make a draw. Taking first knock, Warrington compiled the substantial total of 201, and Northern had run up 71 for the loss of seven wickets when stumps were drawn. Had time permitted the playing of the match to a conclusion, there is every reason to believe that Warrington would have secured the victory.</p>
        <p>W H Podmore must be congratulated upon his highly creditable contribution to the Warrington aggregate. Batting with freedom he compiled 59 in splendid style and carried out his bat. J Gibbon was again in brilliant form, his innings being productive of 51. P B Hawtin failed to maintain his previous week's form, but his score of 26 was obtained by patient cricket and was very acceptable. CEI Bishop was responsible for 13 and G Flampson for 11.</p>
        <p>Lynes, who was heartily cheered bn going to the wickets, did not achieve much success as a batsman, being dismissed for 6. His bowling, however, was most effective, and accounted for five wickets at a cost of 32 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Angling.</head>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>The members of the Howley Hotel Angling Society held their opening match on Sunday last, the place selected being Vale Royal, 39 members taking part. The party proceeded by 'bus, Vale Royal being reached by 11 o'clock. The positions having been drawn for and the respective members having taken up their alloted positions time was called, and the match, which was of two hours duration, commenced. After the usual weighing in process was completed the following per- sons secured the seven prizes offered,their weights be- ing as follows :</p>
        <p>Ozs. Drams. Ist E Gilbert l3 ... 9 2nd T Robinson lO 8 3rd B Dobson 9 ... 12 4th A Norris sth J Robinson 5 ... 4 6th J Crossley 7th E Berry, jun</p>
        <p>Mr Gilbert's catch is the heaviest weight of fish caught at Vale Royal this season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WOMAN'S FEAT.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN'S FEAT.</p>
        <p>For s woman to walk eight miles with ease within a short time of being so week from anemia and indiges- tion that she could not walk across her house floor, may be regarded as a pedestrian feat of more than ordinary interest. Chas. F rde's ale Beans enabled Mrs Gertrude Morley, of Awswortb, near lik,stou, to do this. he says :—" I was so weak from anseraia, indigestion, and other allied ailments from which I bad suffered from girlhood, that I could not walk across the lbor without being fagged. Constipation and head- ache also troubled me sorely. For months I had beea in this condition and had vainly tried all sorts of medicine. My husband then bought me a Don of Bile seam and by the time I had taken them I was a great deal better. A little perseverance with them was all that was neceseart• to restore me ta health. lam now well and recently walked eight miles with ease." The above is by no means an isolated case of the vied benefit resulting from Bile Beans. They are the world's specific for amernia, indigestion, constipatimpiles,briad- ache, all female ailments, liver and kidney disorders, warm weather languor, and all diseases arising out of loss of energy, defective liver action and assimilation. Supplied by all cbernisti at one and three-half-peace or two and nine .per box. They are sold only in sealed boxes : refuse all offered loose.</p>
