      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>St.iPaul's Church.—G.F.S. Picnic.</head>
        <p>St.iPaul's Church.—G.F.S. Picnic.</p>
        <p>The members of the Girls' Friendly Society asso- ciated with St. Paul's Church had their annual picnic on Saturday, when Frodsham was visited. With the exception of a slight shower of rain at three o'clock, the weather throughout the afternoon and evening was of the most charming character, and greatly added to the pleasure of the visitors who numbered about 70, and were conveyed to Mrs Parker Hoose's Mersey Mount, in three 'buses sup- plied by Mr H F Greenough.Theseleft the Kendrick- street schools shortly before two o'clock, Frod- sham being reached in an hour. The party was accompanied by the Vicar (the Rev W Braceeamp) and Mrs Bracecamp, Miss Westwell, mission lady, and several lady members of the congregation who rendered valuable assistance in the preparation of the tea, which was an excellent one and thoroughly enjoyed. Thislimportant part of the day's proceed- ings over, the party were photographed on the field by Mr Coiske, after which a visit was paid to the celebrated caves, the ramble over the hills and through the picturesque lanes affording great en- joyment, especially to those by whom they were seen for the first time. At the caves a pretty and secluded spot was found on the summit of a bank, where a short service was held. Hymns were sung prayers offered, and an address given by the Vicar on he words "Go Forward Sisters," the initial letters forming the words " G.F.5.," by which the society is known. Some valuable advice was given to the members, and the service was rend ered addi- tionally impressive by the congenial and novel sur- roundings. Warrington was safely reached at ten o'clock.</p>
        <p>Sequel to a Collision With an Electric Car. At Manchester Assizes, on Monday, Messrs Crosfield and Sons, Limited, of Warring- ton, sought to recover damages from the Great Grimsby Tramway Company on the ground that by the negligence of a driver in the defend- ant's service a van was smashed and a horse badly injured. Mr Byrne (instructed by Mr F R Roberts) was for the plaintiffs, and explained that as the horse and van were being driven across the road- way an electric cer advanced at a good pace. The driver Sounded the gong repeatedly, but fir some reason or other the horse suddenly stopped and refused to move. The result was that the car came on and dashed into the van, overturning and badly smashing it, The horse was also injured, and one of the occupants of the van was thrown out. The hearing of the case was not concluded when the court adjourned.—The case was resumed on Tues- day, when evidence was given for the defendants by the driver of the car. He stated that the acci- dent could not possibly have been averted, and that he did all he could to stop the car when he saw that the horse had shied, and placed the van in danger.—The jury awarded plaintiffs £45 damages.</p>
