      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A SHOCKING HOME AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before Mr 6' Monks and other msgistrates, George and it lizabeth Peplow, 43. Tanners-lane, were charged with haying on the 4th July. and on other dates during the past six months, neglected their four children in a manner likely to eause them unneces- sary suffering and injury to health.—Mr BI owne, who pro- secuted on behalf of the N.S.P C. 0., said the defendants' child Annie. aged 13 months, died on Sunday week -two days after Inspector Hackett laid tbe information of the case -from tuberculosis, but the doctors could not say that death was attributable to neglect en the part of the defendant-. Each defendant blamed the other. The woman sold her husband was of drunken habits, and ill-used her,and the man accused the woman of drunkenness and said she neglected him. The man was a foreman shunter on the London and Berth Western Railway and earned 32s 6d per week. His wife bought and sold old clothes in the market, end earned 10s or 12s per week, and there was a son earning 12s. Last Thursday week, P.C. Sankey was called to defend- ants' house, in consequence of a fight taking p'ace between the defendants. He found both defendants drunk and there were other people in the house drinking. The house was in a filthy condition and the constable reported the matter to Inspector Hackett, who visited the house on July 4th. The woman was then suffering from the effects of drink. The I three elder ehildren were fairly well clothed,but their clothing I was filthy dirty, and their bodies were verminous. The baby was in a terribly emaciated condition, and was marked with vermin bites. The bedroom whites the defendants occupied was worse than a pig-stve,ane the one in which the children slept was too filthy to descries. Dr Peacocks had seen the house and had declared it to te unfit for habitation. The woman made a statement to Inseector Hackett, in which she laid that the house and children were badly neglected. because her lausband turned her out at night. She had had to sleop in the wash-house tor the past three months. Her husband told her that her death warrant was signed " He never gave her more than 20s per week. In reply, the male defendant said he gave his wife from 27s to 30s per week,and she got drunk two or three times a week. She wouldj not keep the children or the home cleaa,and the baby was neglected very much. He never compelled her to sleep in the wash-house —Evidence was then given— Mrs Hardman, a neighbour, said she was in the htbit of getting up in the morning between two and three o'clock, and she had never heard Mrs Peplow in the wash-house. If she had been there witness would have known.—The male defendant denied that there had been any neglect.—The Chairman said the Bench hed decided to adjourn the ease for a fortnight. In the meantime the defendants would be under close obsereation, and if there was no improvement they would be committed to gaol for six months: ATTACK ON A CYCLIST AT WINWICK. - -</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions, on iiredaesday, a young man named Arthur Hartley. 8, Leonard-street, Stockton Heath. was charged with committing damage to a bicycle telonging to the Warrington Corporation to the extent of £2 10s.—Defendant pleaded guilty —Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy town clerk, who prosecuted, said the com- plainant was Frederick William Lloyd. employed by the Cor- poration as a water inspector. The defendant was a labourer employed at the Winwick Asylum, and at the time of the offence had lodgings in Winwick, although his permanent address was Stockton Heath. On the 4th inst. news reached the complainant that there tad been a breakdown at the Winwick pumping station. He at once proceeded there after securing the necessary workmen, and found if was necessary to turn off certain valves at Clam- brook bridge. For that purpose be in company with a witness named Hardman. a plateplayer. went on their bicycles. When opposite the Vicarage at Winwick Lloyd saw two men a distance of about forty yards off. They walked off the footpath into the road, and one of them was heard to say" Here come two cyclists ; knock them off." As they got near—the cyclists gave the men plenty of room and got as near the Asylum wall as possible—defendant de- liberately rushed at Lloyd and knocked him off his machine Into the wall. The machine was considerably damaged. but Lloyd fortunately was not hurt. It was not thought necessary to summon the defendant for assault, although the case might have been Embus. Considering the import- ant work in which complainant was engaged, be asked the Bench to make an order for payment of the full amount of the damage. These offences were not uncemmon In the country.—Defendant said he did not remember anything about the affair. It was quite an accldent.—The Bench ordered defendant to pay the amount of the damage with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. —Before Messrs J W Hayward (in the chair) and H Thornton. -</p>
        <p>FruDly—Before. the Dep—utir4l-sioT(in the chair). 001 m.</p>
        <p>„ cillor Roberts, Messrs Hepherd and Thornton. BOY SENT To A HOME.—Jarnes Barnes (10), 1, Jolley's Court, was summoned for being found wandering without being under proper control. —Mr W Jones (chief school at- tendance inspector) said the boy was on the streets day after day. 'rise father said he could do nothing with him, and be (Mr Jones) asked for the boy to be sent to the Liverpool Boys Refuge.—The Chairman asked if it was not monstrous that the boy should be sent to a school and maintained at the public expense. because the defendant pleaded non-liability. —Mr Jones said it was considered better for the boy that he should be sent away. The father coal i be ordered to con- tribute towards his maintenance—Ultimately the ,nagistrates ordered the boy to be sent to the Refuge the father to con- tribute 2s 6d per week towards his _maintenance.</p>
        <p>MINOR CAO.RB —Harry ROICOB (13), 105,-17o;ter-street, and Samuel Lythgoes (13), 158, Battersby-lane, were fined in the cost. (6s) for damaging a quantity of hay, the property of James Newton, Orford-lane to tae extent of is.—Charles Henry Newton, 33, Wlnwick-street. was fined 5s and costs for assaulting Annie Patten, 19, Heath-lane. on July sth. —A similar penalty was imposed on Patrick Shaughnessy, 23, Hopwood-street, for assaulting his wife Isabella on the 6th inst.—Harriet Sherrat, 24, New-road, was ordered te pay the costa (3s 6d) for making use of abusive language towards Edward Henry Knowles, 14, New-road, in New-road on the 11th inst.</p>
        <p>MeNDAY.—Before Messrs F Monks (In the chair). T Birettall, J W Hayward, H Thornton, and 8 Wright. . _ _</p>
        <p>A WARNING TO BOYS —Bight boys, named John Gieave. Walter Johnston, Charles Antall, Arthur Arstall, Robert Bradshaw, Alfred Starkey and William Weir, of Wash-lane, and Rodger Burrows, Sandhill-terrace, were charged with doing wilful damage to a ladder, the property of the Man- chester Ship Canal Company. All but Starkey and Weir pleaded guilty--Mr Newmann (tiffancheeter).who prosecuted on behalf of the Ship Canal Company, said that as this case was only brought as a warning, he would withdraw the charge against Starkey and Weir, and proceed against the other defendants. It was the desire of the company to put a stop to the:damage which was being constantly done to the company's property at Latchford. Defendants were seen on the railway line on the canal bank, and they were playing with a ladder which broke. The amount of the damage was Mr —Defendants were each fined Es 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDRESDAY —Before Messrs John White (in the chair) and S R Armitage. DRUNK AND REFUSING To Q in.—Mary Connor, Fern- worth-road, Penk.th, was summoned for being drunk and disorderly at Penketh on July 2nd. and also for being drunk on the premises of Frederick Johnson, "Crown Hotel," and refusing to quit when requested to do so.—P.O. Houghton stated that at 3,10 p.m. on the date named he was called to the " Crown Hotel " by the landlady, and found the defendant there in a drunken condition. The landlady told her to go home and she refused, and witness had to take her home. She was very disorderly on the highway Defendant called her daughter who stated that she was sober at 3 o'clock.—The magistrates imposed a fine of be In each Case</p>
        <p>TRESPASSING IN SEAIIOII OF GAME—Us. Shepherd was charged with trespassing on land at Winwick in search of gate on July 11th, and also with using a net for the purpose of taking game.—James Rowson was charged with being an accessory to both offences.—P,o Metcalf gave evidence, and defendants were fined 10s and costs In each case. ASLEFP WHILST IN CHARGE OF A I:ORM—Joseph McCarthy ..as lined 10s for being asleep whilst in charge of a lorry at ltixton on July 2nd. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Memo J.Oekles.ton (in the ehair) and J</p>
        <p>Prlthrd.</p>
        <p>OBSCENE LANOITACIC•—ThomS.II Hißidge was fined 10i including costs orbs default 7 days for the above offence.- Thomas Kadley was fined the same sum for a similar offence A MIXED UP BOTHER.-A young woman named Sarah Price, Surrey-street, was summoned by Annie Yates for. assaulting her on the Bth July.-The evidence showed that on the date in question there had been some bother between the women over a bro)ch and tie which had been lost. The defendant accused the complainant of having the brooch in her possession and after some words the defendant went to the complainant and struck her ore the chest, rendering her unconscious.- The defendant. who cross-summoned the complainant denied striking her, and asserted that it was Mrs Yates who struck her and made her faint- -Bvldence wee given at length ay many witnesses, which showed that there was a general fight between the women and their female relateves, tke magis- trates deciding that the parties be each bound over in their own surety of £5, each to pay their own costs. its 6d. A WANDERING SON AND 1175 MOTHER -Annie Lovatt. Norfolk-street. was summoned for having assaulted Sarah Ann Withington, Grosvenor-street, on July sth.-The evidence showed that the ,on of the defendant had been turned out by his mother, and that he lodged with the coin. plainant. On the day in question the defendant went to the house where her son lodged and demanded to see him. She then struck rdrs Withington in the face-Defendant denied the charge, but after evidence had been given at length, the Bench deelded that the defendant be bound over in the sum of £2 to keep the peace for six months, and pay the costs 12s 6d, or in default three weeks.-Defendan t elec- ted to go to prison. A PAUPER LUNATIC'S MAINTENANCE.-Mr W Hough applied on behalf of the Runcorn Guardians for an o rder in ' the case of William Walker, a pauper fanatic, sent from the Dutton Workhouse on June 14th to the Upton Lunatic Asylum. The Guardians, he said, had incurred charges amounting to £4 13s id, and the man's maintenance had been fixed by the Visiting Committee of the Asylum at 7s 7d per '-reek. The order asked for was to seize so much of the man's property as would be necessary to meet th e charges. The order was granted. THE ANNUAL BREWSTER SESSIONS.•-Tbe magistrates fixed August 25th as the date for the holding of the annual licensing sessions. LICENSING APPLICITION.-Thomas Carter applied for permission to sell until the next transfer day at an off- lionised beerhonse 15, _Stanley-ltreat, Runcorn. The police _ .</p>
        <p>• • raised no objection and the application was granted.</p>
      </div>
