      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>38 MILLIONS OF LEM OAS</head>
        <p>38 MILLIONS OF LEM OAS</p>
        <p>PARRS BANKILIMITED. Report of the Directors.</p>
        <p>The directors 'of Parr's Bank, Limited, in sub- mitting to the shareholders the balance sheet of the Bank at 30th Tune, 1902, certified by the auditors, report that the position and prospects of the busi- ness continue to be in every way satisfactory. The amalgamation of Parea's Leicestershire Banking Company Limited with this company has been successfully carried out. The subscribed capital has been increased to £8,542,500, the paid-up Capital to £1.708,500 and the reserve fund to .£1,708,500 In conformity with the authority given by special resolution passed on the 27th May and confirmed on the 12th June Mr Hussey Packe, who was Chairman of Pares's Leicestershire Banking Company Limited, has been elected a director of this bank. The Directors have to record, with much regret, the decease of their esteemed colleague,Ar Hugh Cameron Ross. To fill this vacancy,and the one created by the death of Mr Emil Heinemann last year,the directors have elected the Hon Arthur Stanley. M.P., and ion Sir Charles William Fremantle, IC.O 8., to seats on the board. The shareholders will be asked to confirm these three appointments. Mr John Dun retired at the end of January from the position of general mana- ger, whiPh he had held for thirty-six years. In recognition of his long and unique services to the bank, a sum of £lO,OOO was, as announced and approved at the shareholders meeting on January 23rd, voted to him by the directors, Mr Dun con- tinues, as a member of the Board, to give the bank the benefit of his great business experience. Mr R W Whalley, formerly assistant general mana- ger has been appointed general manager in suc- cession to Mr Dun.</p>
        <p>The balance brought forward at 31st December, 1901, was £97,909 18s 10d. From this there has been deducted the sum of £lO,OOO voted to Mr John Dun on his retirement from the general manage- ment. The balance brought for ward by Pares's Leicestershire Banking Company Limited on 31st December, 1901, was £11,205 33 lid, making the balance brought forward £99,113 2s 91. Including this sum, the net balance of prefits,after Providing for all bad and doubtful debts, and rebat- ing the current bills at 5 per cent is £287.694 15s 2d. From this sum the directors recommend a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum for the half- year ended 30th June, 1902, payable, free of income tax, on let August £162 307 103, the transfer to bank premises account, £20,000, leaving balance to be carried to next account of £103,387 5s 2d. A sub- branch of the bank has been opened at lielsby, Cheshire. An extraordinary general meeting of the company will be held at the bank premises, Winwick-street, Warrington, on Thursday, July 24th, for the pur- pose of declaring a dividend to be payable on the lit August, confirming the election of directors and conducting the ordinary business of th, company.</p>
        <p>A Woolston Drowning Mystery. Mr Brighouse held an inquest at the Horse and Jockey Inn, Woolston, on Monday night. on the body of a man unknown, which was found in the Mersey at Woolston Weir on Saturday. The evidence of the weir keeper (Charles Rose) was to to the effect that he saw the body floating, and after it had gone over the wash he secured grapp- ling irons, and with the assistance of Police Con- stable Spence got it to the bank, and removed it to the Horse and Jockey Inn, The jury returned an open verdict. The following description has been circulated by the police :—Beight, s.ft. 9at. ; age about fifty years; brown hair turning grey, hair very thin on top of the forehead brown moustache and brown eyes; dressed in bue serge trousers, red and white cotton shirt, khaki stockings, and lace-up boots with toe cap. The body appears to have been in the water about a fortnight or three weeks.</p>
        <p>iip—pie-ton Rifle Club—Opening of the Ladies'</p>
        <p>Bran Ch.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoen—tie—ladies' branch of the above club, which was successfully inaugurated a few weeks ago, was opened at four o'clock in the presence of from 20 to 30 ladies and one or two gentlemen. The weather outside the Quarry, in which the meeting of the club takes place,was very hot ; inside it was all one could desire, the trees and secluded position fully protecting one from the hot rays of the son. Mai,or Roome, who has greatly interested himself in the formation of the club,was in attendance, and addressed a few words to the ladies present, showing how necessary it was that at times, for the purposes of protection,they should be expert in the use of firearms. Some valuable ad- vice wasgiven, and later on he showed the ladies how to use the rifle. Some sf the ladies shot remarkably well, and there were several "bull's eyes." Owing to the ;lateness of the inauguration of the club the committee have de- cided to charge half the annual subscription only for this year.</p>
        <p>Also use EIFFEL TOWER LEMON JELLIES.</p>
        <p>BRITAIN :-</p>
        <p>S. A. PALMER,</p>
        <p>REPUTED QUIRTS, 4e 6D; REPUTED PINTS. 28 9D. HEAD] OFFICES AD WAREHOUSE FOR OREAT</p>
        <p>MOUNT PLACE, BROOK-STREET (oPPosITE JOHN NOBLE LTD.).</p>
