        <head>WARRINGTO N TRAMWAY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTO N TRAMWAY</p>
        <p>MATTERS.</p>
        <p>What "Bill Brown" Thinks.</p>
        <p>--- A Stockton Heath Opinion of the Interview iiith Alderman Roberts in Last Week's Examiner."</p>
        <p>[BY AN ODD FELLOW.]</p>
        <p>Oa opening my last week's " Examiner " I was struck with the immense amount of talk as reported in the Council proceedings and the special liaterview as to Tram waystaffecting Stockton Heath in particular and the town adjoining it generally, 414 being very jealous of the fair character of my native village I decided to interview (that seeming to be the proper thing) " Bill Brown" on the sub- bet and to give you the benefit of that worthy's °Pinion. need not trouble you with ti life picture of " but content myself by saying he is a Heathen "of the deepest dye a sage of no mean Order, and somewhat of a prophet (he actually pro. Phesied that Mayors of County Boroughs would not be knighted when the struggle fqr that position Was going on) aod the following, so far as 1 remem- ber our conversation, are his remarks, which I trust will receive the careful perusal of your intelli- gent readers. Having found Bill in his garden destroying insects very liberally (he being an active member of the Village Garden Society) I at once approached him with the question, " Well Bill have you seen Your Examiner' to-day." " I have," says Bill after disposing with his foot of a healthy looking snail, rather viciously. V 4 ell and what do you think of the interview ?—The Parish Council and inhabitants generally are I fear in tor it, are'nt they ?" "In for it be blowed ! We need not fear such riace, we'll have the trams right enough before °rig, that is when the North-Western Railway care •do the bit of a job at the crossing, which will 'llot be yet awhile, until the doposited money has rned enough interest to pay them for doing the crk without touching the lump sum," said Bill. "But Bill, this will only then allow the trams to Up to Stafford-road." ; Well," said Bill, " you don't want to make me '',ieve that trams to Stafford-road and no father &amp;Li Pay, because if you do,you are very much mis • ‘en. Stockton Heath people, at least a many of errl, will walk the test of the Ca