milar purposes in other districts especi- /1 in Stockton Heath which is full of So many ' ',na town of light and leading, and Corporation "dials. Elln't ask me to believe they will be con- much longer. If the Corporation don't take in ekton Heath, some other company will before &amp;I'S, and it is too good a thing for the Corporation li:tat some other people get hold of and then 1.7.1% to be bought out. No, no, you wait, never 'lnd the Of ‘1311t," I added, "look at the cost the Chairman t.ll t„ramways mentions, for street widening from 'vving Bridge to the Mulberry Tree Inn;, tall(-1Ost," said Bill," it's downright impudence, I akv„i,t, for the tram people to want to come into "'der district for the purpose of profit and then rurable at having to pay for widening a road let Yhich they want to trade. They have spent a street buying in Warrington in connection We" the trams,without a word, only the owners of 4e,?.,_rdiperty were different from those in Stockton Irpr,"•°• Let the property belong to one or two I and the cost of widening would be dropped hot cinder and the absolute necessity fo Ide„ ''llsi•i,.:49—trams or no trams—would be urged upon ultnediately. However as it's getting late and I goonat to fasten up the garden gate, I will bid you the' night, but don't you be bothered by what 1,0„8e chaps say. You'll have your trams—keep eYes open !" nd promising to discuss the matter again RJ'ntime. Bill disappeared in search of his garden ttLe Pad-lock —mumbling as he went,that Stockton th""ith was not quite as safe as it used to be before e Rood—from town.</p>
