        <p>Heath Scheme. _ ,We are informed that Messrs Preece and Cardew have llorted upon the Cost ot the construction of the pro. 5d light railway from the tramway terminua at L'Afford.road to Victoria-square, Stockton Heath. The iTitn. ate of the cost is more than double the cost of a t 111ar length 0f tramway within the borough. H the cl;1 No,t;Doration are compelled .rnptoc4o7 ouit n Out con will idivtionlnvse ki•ith the Order 1 to tbt(Penditure of at least £ll,OOO and thin would have t incurred without y additional fare being Tne Town Clerk : any additional rerlOrt to the corn- NttßPd• tho " Points out that in his opinion 90 per cent of Ni)11, People who now makeue from Stafford- of the 'buses 11 kisvicl use the cars on the tramway 141 to Rylands-street and vice and would pay a penny fare. pie estimated tolt lly . rr Nak, of constructing this light ali way would 4 4," the Corporation ,411C,IPa borough—single s ingle construct track, witht o miles _e of Y within the 6,;°Prbe would for equipment complete. The money coou,t"-!. boto",,E'llt for the benefit of the ratepayers and as the m *otil", with the revenue from Stockton Heath line be a source of considerable profit. There is little liehttlkhility of the corporation proceeding with the b tailway under the conditions imposed y the ert illtsith reference to the difficulty at Wildersoool Cross- 414"v"ssure in being brought to bear upon the London Ikt"Otth Wp t n Railway Company to complete the at the earliest possible date. e-on a Tram Car at 'Green-Street Warrington Police Court on Friday. before Mr d and other magistrates. John Inman, 29, Sankey. was summoned for entering a</p>
        <p>-LORD ROSEBERY ' striking speeches. Holdroyd's Gravel Pills cure kid 4 '3 i•tvot, L umba go, Sciatica, Backache biseases, etc. A guaranteed cure. Try a box all Chemists. Post free 12 stamps. Ifoldroyd I Rat Cleckhea on York (3).</p>
        <p>I tram car; whilst in a state of intoxication on Saturday last. He pleaded nct guilty.—Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy-town clerk, prosecuted, and said the case was a very important one, as it effected both the public and the tram servants. On Saturday nieht a car left Rylands-street for Sankey Bridges at 10.40. At Green street it stopped to take on passengers, amongst whom was the defendant. Seeing that defendant was under the influence of drink, the conductor refused him admittance. Defendant then forced hisj way on the car, seized the conductor by the coat collar and pushed him under the staircase. The passengers wcra alarmed and the driver,seeing what had occurred, went to the assistance of the conductor. Defendant still refused to leave the car, and after a delay of eight minutes the driver was obliged to take the car on its journey. Shortly after passing the , " Coach and Horses " Inn, tho assistance of Constable ' Woods was obtained. When defendant forced his way on the car he used very obscene and threatening language to the conductor,and be con inued this for practicallyithe whole of the journey until the help of the constable was obtained. The case vit as about as bad a one of its kind as it possibly could be,and he asked the Bench to inflict the full penalty of 40s.—The driver, conductor, and P C Woods gave evidence bearing out this statement. —De- fendant admitted being under the influence of drink but said be believed he was acting within his rights in riding on the car, provided that he conducted himself with decency towards the passengers and conductor. He denied insulting or roughly treating the conductor, and contended that the driver and conductor usedunnecessary force towards him. He Mao pointed out that the car eft Rylands-street at 10.40 and it had been stated in evidence that Constable Woods' assistance was obtained about 10 50. If there was a delay of eight minutes at Green-street it left only two minutes in which to travel from Rylanffsistroet to the "Coach and Horses." H the conductor had used a bit of discretion there would not have been any excitement or delay—As this was the first case of its kind which had been brought before the Court the stench decided to fine the defendant 103 and costs (19 s in all).</p>
