      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>.-:.,:,..'.!:.:• ..i.'1.;.:',..,'.-:i'F'.;;;. -;:!...-,..:._.4,4...;-:!</head>
        <p>.-:.,:,..'.!:.:• ..i.'1.;.:',..,'.-:i'F'.;;;. -;:!...-,..:._.4,4...;-:!</p>
        <p>THE .WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Temporary Plant at Work.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the water getting low in the wells at Winwick and the great risk of a scarcity of supply,the Water Committee decided a fortnight ago to make temporary arrangements for providing a supply of water from the new wells in Delp'i-lane.ln order was immediately despatched to Messrs James Simpson and Company. Limited, engineers, of Grosvenor-road, London, 8 W, and the firm roset the occasion in commendable fashion. The order was given on Wednesday last week and by Sunday the pumps and machinery were despatched from London by special train on Friday one of the pumps was got to work and it is now pumping half a million gallons per day from the new source of supply in Delph-lane to the reservoir at Winwick. Another pump of similar capacity will be at work in the course of a few days and these two pumps will give the Corporation an additional supply of one million gallons per day. This additional quantity will be ample to meet the present requirements of the town. Messrs Simpson and Company are laying down the permanent plant at Delph-lane but this work will not be completed until October. When the plant is ready, the temporary pumps which have been laid down this week will be removed. The Winwick exten- sion scheme when completed will provide an addi- tional supply of three million gallons per day, In addition to the extension at Winwizlr, the Cor- poration are erecting a new service reservoir in connection with the Appleton supply, and are also re-constructing the filters. The work is being carried out by Dlesirs Bennie and Thompson.</p>
      </div>
