      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>letters to the 1.-713ttor.</head>
        <p>letters to the 1.-713ttor.</p>
        <p>Warrington Library.—ls it well Managed?</p>
        <p>Sir,—The calling of public attention to the state of affairs it, the Library has come none too soon, and if the bulk of the Warrington public had only known how far we are behind other towns in this respect, the matter would have been gone into long ago. It is complained that some of the books are dirty, and in some cassia too had to be desoribed by so mild an adjective. No doubt this is very true, but it is also true to some extent of every Free Public Library, and while it should unquestionably be remedied by replace copies of the offending volumes, there are ether evils which cry for attention first. The whole Library in Warrington is so far behind the times that a dirty book here and there is of comparatively no importance. Who ever beard of a public library without a catalogue P That is tbe ridiculous position we are placed in. Four years ago I was told at the Library that there was no complete catalogue, but that one was being prepared. I decided to wait for it, and am waiting yet. The old catalogues kept for borrowers' reference at the ILibrary itself have been getting steadily dirtVr ' and more dilapidated, and at last have disappeared from eight, either eaten up by microbes or wisely withheld as too bad even for Warrington's easy-going Toe only way to obtain a book now is to worry the assistants until they can unearth a book one has not had before. There is apparently no way of ascertaining what becks! the Library contains. The two small show- cases on view are excellent so far as they go, but they cannot hold many books, and poor as our Library is, it is, to a great extent, wasted through this limitation. One is constrained to ask whose fault it is we cannot have a catalogue ? I know of a neighbouring town where 14,000 volumes were dealt with and a complete catalogue was prepared and printed in six months. In Warrington we have to wait nearly as many years ! If the Librarian has fair treatment and is competent, such a delay is unreasonable—one would say impossible. Why has it occurred here ? lam not in a position to speak as to the competency of the gentleman who serves the Warrington people as chief Librarian, but for his own credit's sake I think he should make a move—and a big one, and that forthwith. If his hands are too full, he is not having fair treatment,and the Committee should putt he ma ter righ%Fle is responsible for the Library,the Museum, and the Technical Institute, and these would fully occupy any two ordinary men. When one man is called on to superintend the lot, he does it in the only possible way,viz.,by neglecting one or the other,and unfortunately it seems to be the Library that suffers in our case. If the Committee have not been struck with this obvious fact, it must be because in their enthusiasm</p>
        <p>for the Museum and Technical Institute, they are blind to the interests and claims of the Library. The Museum is unquestionably a fine one. I think it is too fine— unnecessarily fine for a town of this size, and from a scientific standpoint it is almost entirely neglected by I the publie. Where then is the utility of a careful arrangement of the geological section, for example ? How many students use it ? It is practically useless to any but students, and by compatison, the Litrary is used by probably fifty or hundred for every one that finds the Geological Section of value or interest. The thing is good and admirable in itself, but its sweetness is " wasted on the desert air," and if the idea is to encourage the study of geology, the expense in room, money and attention is out of all proportion. The I money annnally devoted to Museum and Library is not 1 excessive, but when the former is satisfied (and it I evidently has the first call), the poor Library is starved almost out of decent existence. It is cramped in miser- ably small quarters ; it has no catalogue ; it has no accommodation for ladies and for youths ; and there is no quiet corner for students—no " Reference " depart- ment, except by intruding on the privacy of the Chief Librarian, and pursuing one's investigations under the awe-inspiring scrutiny of so many-sided a man.</p>
        <p>The result of all this is naturally enough the spring- ing up of private circulating libraries on every hand. If you want a modern book,the Warrington Free Public Library is the last place you would go to for it. You are compelled to buy a copy for yourself or pay so much per week cr per year at a private circulating library. You have to pay through the rates for a public library which is practically useless to you,and if you really want a book you must ray again to a private individual or company that is np-to-date. Is the Librarian satisfied with such a position ? Are the committee proud of their handiwork ? Is the present state of affairs the best that can be got from their collective intelligence and time ? If so, the whole is inferior to some of its Darts, for there are men amongst them who, if they professed to run a public library, as individuals would be ashamed to own the ancient conglomeration of volumes that is offered to the Warrington people as a Free Public Library. Things are so bad that I dare not say much on the subject of branch libraries. These are for other and more favoured towns where the rates are lower than ours. We spend more and get less for ie. Still in a town covering an area as great as that of Warrington, branch libaries should at least be on the carpet. Then there is that £7OO exampls of Henry Woods—l, like many others who enjoy good pictures, think the money could have been better spent on kindred objects, and the Library is one. It would have sufficed to make a wholesale reform in the Library, and still have left a good-margin for pictures. Some towns might spend £7OO on a picture without incurring blame, hut we are so far in arrest, and with our heavy current expenees, are so hard up, that such a purchase seems a cruel ex- travagar ce. It certainly shows a lack of balance or sense of proportion in the gentlemen concerned. We might appeal to Mr Carnegie, but as a shrewd business man, he would regard the case as not deserving, the resources we have being squandered on objects of com- paratively small importance, while the Library, which the whole town should be able to look to for a good supply of books in decent condition, is starved and apparently neglected and mismanaged. I sincerely trust that this question will be followed up by others. Everybody (outside the committee) is aware of and dissatisfied with the existing condition of things, but it is too much trouble to make an effort to remedy it. "Laissez faire " has a firmly established position in our town, but surely there are enough lovers of books amongst us to make a force sufficient to bring about reform. We have some enthu- siasts for education in our midst ; will they take part in the attempt ? or do they not consider the library a help to education ?</p>
        <p>The fact that we are so far behind some of our neigh- bours should be sufficient to make the committee, and the librarian ex..rt themselves for very shame's sake,but hitherto it does nct appear to have influenced them, and the prers affords the only way of trying to drive the truth home. The importance of the subject is my only reason for trespassing on your space, at such a length. Yours truly,—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ALFRED HULME.</head>
        <p>ALFRED HULME.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Shop-Breaking Case. At Liverpool County Quarter Sessions, on Tues- day, Thomas Troy (30), tailor, an old offender, for having at Warrington, on the 26th June,broken and entered the shop of Thomas Domviile, Priestley- street, and stolen thirty gold rings, value £2O, was sentenced to twelve months' bard labour.</p>
        <p>Extensive Damage at a Warrington Fire. About four o'clock on Tuesday morning, Thomas Griffiths, a watchman in the employ of Messrs IBonnie and Thompson, tramway contractors, heard the cracking of glass in the top storey of the premises of Messrs Chorley, marine store dealers, Scotland-road. It was very foggy at the time, and Griffiths Rave information to P.O Edwards. Hav- ing discovered that a fire had broken out, the officer sent information to the Fire Station,and the brigade at once turned out with the reel cart, ladders, and fire escape, followed immediately after by the steam fire engine " Captain," which had to be drawn by men, no horses being obtainable. Haif- a-dozen jets were quickly got to work from the street hydrants, which furnished a good supply of water, mainly from the Appleton mains. The fire originated in a warehouse at the rear of the premises, which adjoined a number of cottages facing Town Hill. These were in danger of being ignited, but luckily that was pre- vented by the prompt action of the firemen, who were under the direction of Superintendent end Deputy-Superintendent Turner. The inhabitants of the cottages were in a state of great excitement, and only the assurances of the firemen that there was no danger prevented them removing their belongings. After a short spell two powerful jets were got to work from the steamer, and these en- abled the brigade to keep the fire from spreading from the warebouse,the roof of which had fallen in. After two hours' steady progress the fire was Rot under, but by this time the store, which was packed with almost every kind of material, had been practically gutted. The damage, about £6OO, is Covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
