      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>'EXAMINER FRIDAY JULY 18: LATEST NEWS. ITHE MEN OF THE HOUR. THE KING. Character Sketches by T. P. O'Connor. Date of the Coronation. MR. A. 7-BALFOUR.</head>
        <p>THE 'EXAMINER FRIDAY JULY 18: LATEST NEWS. ITHE MEN OF THE HOUR. THE KING. Character Sketches by T. P. O'Connor. Date of the Coronation. MR. A. 7-BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>Whatever the outside feeling (writes Mr T P O'Connor in this week's " M.A.P."), the succession of Mr Balfour to the greatest office in the Empire is universally popu- lar inside the House of Commons. Mr Balfour is undoubtedly the best-liked personality in the assembly. Even the very faults which are urged against him by his severer critics, are there part of his personality. He is said to be slack and indifferent and too yielding by some among his own party ; but this his political opponents interpret into a certain large tolerance of temper and judgment, which makes him easy to deal with. It is amusing sometimes to see Mr Balfour cheered by the Liberals, while below the gangway on his own side of the House Lord Hugh Cecil sitr pale and angry, with his long thin hands twitching in nervous excitement, because his leader and relative has made some concession to the arch-enemies in the Nonconform- ist_ camp.. .</p>
        <p>Mr Braltour is, curiously enough, mere popular:on the Irish benches than in any part of the House ; the reason this should be regarded as ; peculiar, is that he and they had perhaps the fiercest fight of the geoeration in the old battling days, and he did not spare them nor they him. But those were days when he was more combative than he is now in his more sedate middle age ; and most of his opponents on the Irish benches have either dis- appeared from Irish politics or, like himself, have left behind the first morning of their political youth. In any case, Mr Balfour is always unusually considerate to the Irish members, and they are always very considerate So hind.</p>
        <p>ao trained himself in the languages and in the spirit of the East that probably be could have gone among many of the tribes and passed as one of the Arabs. And his victory was as much the result of his power of learning through native sources the movements and purposes of the enemy as of his vigilant eye over food and trans- port. And similarly when be got into that dreadfully ' nasty and difficult tangle with Major Marchand at Fashoda, it was his wondrous tact that at once asserttd the supremacy of England, and spared the French officer humiliations that might have made war inevit- able. That stroke of sending Marchand some food and wine, and offering him all the hospitality of the English can p, was a masterpiece ; only Kitchener would have hail the ready wit to do it. It was exactly the same quality that enabled Kitchener to bring the Boer leaders finally over to peace. What a fine specimen that was of true diplomatic skill when Kitchener slapped Da Wet on the back with " Don't sulk, De Wet" ; it was perhaps some saying like this that broke finally the Boer hesitations and made peace. And these same qualities were to be seen in the arrange- ment of that thanksgiving service at Pretoria. There was no march past, there was no hymn of triumph among those sung ; nothing was done which miyht, by a show of insolent victory, have outraged the feeling of the gallant enemy that had been conquered, all of which showed the true statesman and the inspired diplomat under the general's tunic. Kitchener is undoubtedly English by blood. 3nd yet it was something that he was born in Kerry, and that he spent many of his early years there, Berry is a county , where the peasants are all gentlemen and ladies in bear- ' ing, in manner, in thought, and in feeling. Soft as I their ow n skies--gentle, friendly, courteous—they prob- ably taught to the tall and lanky striplicag who roved over their mountains something of the flexibility which has proved the turning-point in eventful moments in his history and in that of his country. But again do not figure Kitchener as superhuman. I saw him listen to a debate on himself in the House of Commons; and his emotions of pleasure, of amusement, of profound interest, were written on his face in lines as transparent as the lines of Mr. Gladstone's expressive countenance. Kitchener is very great ; but he is also very human.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>An Unofflcial Announcement.</head>
        <p>An Unofflcial Announcement.</p>
        <p>A news agency states author itatively that Saturday, August 9, has been officially fixed, though not yet officially announced, as the date of the Coronation. The time to- the commencement of the ceremony will be eleven o'clock am. The decision was on Thuraday privately made known to one or two of the officers in charge of the arrangements, so that they might have their preparations well in band. _ .</p>
        <p>In regard to the service, it has so far been decided to omit only the Litany, and it is possible that :he sermon will also be omitted. There will be no alteration in the musical portion of the service. This morning the mem- bers of the band and choir will receive notices from Sir Frederick Bridge, director of the music at the Corona- tion, caking them to attend at the Abbey on Friday, August 8, when the only rehearsal is to be conducted. The choristers, as has been stated, have been chosen from various churches in London and till country.</p>
        <p>THE OABINEI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Resignation of Lord Cadogan.</head>
        <p>Resignation of Lord Cadogan.</p>
        <p>Sir M. Hicks-Beac to Retire at the</p>
        <p>End of the Session.</p>
        <p>DUBLIN, THURSDAY NIGHT.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced from Dublin Castle to-night that Lord Cadogan has resigned the office of Lord Lieu- tenant of Ireland, an,l that his resignation has been accepted by his Majesty the King. The Central News is ens bled to state that, at the request of Mr Balkur, Sir Michael Elicks-Beach has agreed to retain the Ch ancellorstlip of the Exchequer until the end of the present sitting of Parliament. Sir Michael intends to spend the next winter abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MR. AUGUSTINE BIRRELL ON LORD SALISBURY.</head>
        <p>MR. AUGUSTINE BIRRELL ON LORD SALISBURY.</p>
        <p>Plain Speaking.</p>
        <p>Mr Augustine Birrell, addressing a meeting of North Bristol Liberals, said for the past twenty years Lord Salisbury had ruled the House of Lords with a rod of iron. He had attempted nothing to settle the Irish question, but had made himself responsible for a mea- sure of self-government in local matters for Ireland. Yet he had declared that local self-government for Ireland was a more dangerous thing even than Mr Gladstone's Home Rule Bill. That was Lord Salisbury's one contri- bution to the settlement of the Irish question, and that left the question exactly where they found it. Lord Salisbury never had trust in or affection for the people; he was opposed to household suffrage, and he bitterly opposed the repeal of the paper taxes- If Lord Salisbury had had his way Oxford and Uambridge would still be closed to Nonconformists. For social reforms he never had any great regard, and he viewed them with a mix- ture of suspicion and dislike. Lord Salisbury was never a friend of the temperance cause, he never favoured shorter hours of labour, and he never grappled with the question of the housing of the poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NEWTON-IN-MAKERFIELD VICTORIA MEMORIAL COTTAGE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON-IN-MAKERFIELD VICTORIA MEMORIAL COTTAGE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>A three days' open-air floral bazaar in connection with the above was commenced on the Mesnes Football field yesterday. A large committee htls be,-n at work for some time, and they must be highly gratified by the result of the first day's pro- ceedings. This is the first open-air bazaar that has been held in the district, so teat the affair savoured very mitch of an experiment. Great interest was taken in the event by the public, and as yesterday turned out brilliantly fine, a large crowd assembled, the football field presenting a gay appearance. Hoge marquees and tents were erected on the field and the way in which these were set out and decorated reflected great credit on the management. Yesterday the proceedings were opened by Dr Valentine, the Rev Tlt Matthews occupying the chair, the bazaar tent being crowded to its utmost capacity. The chairman spoke in very eulogistic terms of the cause for which the Bazaar was being held,and after Dr Valentine had formally declared the proceedings open, the stall- holders were quickly kept very busily engaged Entertainments comprising Pierotts, and perform- ances by the Makerfield Amateur Dramatic Society were held at intervals and were largely patronised. During the afternoon a procession made up by the Thursday Football Club paraded the town and to the field, and at night the Earlestiown Prize Band and the Reformatory School Band enlivened the proceedings by playing for dancing. The baiaar will be continued to-day (Friday) and to-morrow (Saturday). Fuller particulars will be given in our next issue.</p>
        <p>Proposed Memorial to a Warrington Hero Mr T Hughes, of 70, Argyle-street, St Helens announces that a memorial is to be erected to Mr Charles Houghton, who lost his life in gallantly trying to save a woman in the Mersey at Warring- ton a short time ago. Peculiar Accident to a Boy._ - -</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a boy named Alfred Brittain living at 27, Fothergill-street, cff Marshouse-lane, was admitted to the Infirmary suffering from a broken ieg. He was playing on a sand heap, when he fell into a deep bole the boys had made while playing, and sustained the injury named. H M. ASHTON'S LIFN TONTO IS THE ICING OF TONICS —B3, SANICEY-STREET, WELlTingtoll.—(Advt.) Victoria College of Music.—Warrington Centre.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of Candidates who were suc- cessful in gaining Professional Diplomas and local certificates at the examination held July Bth at the Masonic Hall. The examiner for practical music was Dr J H Lewis, D C L, and in theoretical music Dr G Prior, FRCO,F V C M. Teachers names are given in parenthesis. Higher examinations (all pianoforte unless otherwise stated).—Associate : Gertrude Blair of Glazebrook (Mr C H Dawson, ALC M, LV C M), Mrs Annie Davies (Mr G F lAinsley FV C M). Intermediate section: Albert Woodcock, •Earlestown, 97 marks, honours; Florence Newall 93 marks, honours; T Robinson Grant 85 marks, honours; Hannah Thornily, 70 marks (Mr Dawson) Florencs Wkitield, 75 marks (Miss L Green, AL C M). Junior Section: Ruth Lightfoot 92 marks,honours ; Annie Kilduff 85 marks, honours; Edward Light— foot, 75 marks; Henry Short 74 Marks, (Miss L Green) John Peers 80 marks, honours, (Mr Daw- son) ; Lily E Williamson, violin. 75 marks (Miss B Bouchier, AIW M) ; Edward Jones, Earlestown, organ playing, 91 marks, honours (Mr Dawson). Primary grade: Florry Moores (Penketh),92 marks, honours; Elizabeth Ormsby, 92 marks; Francis John Donovan 85 marks, honours; John Whittle,; 78 marks; Harold Plinston, 75 marks (Mr Dawson) Miss Mary Mills 90 marks,bonours(Miss L Caldwell); May Whitfield SO marks, honours (Miss Green) . Fred Final!, Eariestown, 87 marks, honours (Mr' Albert Woodcock); Elsie Johnson 8S marks, honours (Miss Maude Jennings) ; Percy cook 84 marks, honours (Miss Polly Plinston) ; Eva Everall 50 marks, honours (Miss Ida Hewitt. Preparatory Grade: Preston Jones, 80 marks. honours (Mr Albert Woodcock). Theoretical examination.—Junior honours spat:ion : Florence ewall, 100 marks; Albert Woodcock (Harlestown) 95 marks; T Robinson-Grant (Earles- town) 90 marks; Ed Jones, 85 marks (Mr Dawson). Junior pass section: A Woodcock, 100 marks (Earlestown) ; T Robinson-Grant (Earlestown) 90 marks ; Edward Jones, 85 marks, honours (Earles- town) (Mr Cll Dawson). Primary grsde : Albert Hunt, 90 marks (Mr Dawson) Henry Short, AS marks; Annie Kilduff,Bs marks; Florence Whitfield, "T 5 marks; Ruth Lightfoot, TO marks; Edward Lightfoot,6s marks; (Miss Lilv Gr,en). All candi- dates gaining honours are privileged to wear the college sash which • is specially designed for the 'Coronation year.</p>
        <p>SEDIOUS ACCIDENT TO AN ASHTON MAN AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a serious accident Wel a collier named Williams, of Polton-road, Ashton-in- Makerfield, He was riding his bicycle at a fairly rapid rats, and in endeavouring to turn the sharp corner off Park-road and Willow-road, Newton, dashed on the footpath and was thrown with great violence against the brick wall, The unfortunate man, who was picked up unconscious, bad a serious would on his head. lie remained inzensible for ten minutes. Dr Darold Watkins was speedily on the spot, and having dressed the man's injuries sent him to Ashton in a cab, The bicycle was a complete wreck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HIS QUALITIES FOR LEADERSHIP.</head>
        <p>HIS QUALITIES FOR LEADERSHIP.</p>
        <p>Me Balfour ban undoubtedly very great qualities— greater, perhaps, than are generally understood. His leadership of the House of Commons bail not the strenu- ousness that Mr Gladstone ever gave it, and that per- haps one of hist colleagues would try to give to it ; but, on the other band, his suavity of temper, his unfailing courtesy, and his readiness for conciliation, smooth away a vast amount of difficulties, and he is able cei- tainly to do things which no other member in the house would accomplish. Above all things. he is an able and resourceful adroit debater. No man has anything like hie boundless ingenuity in defence ; his quickness to seize a point; his skill in tearing to pleCes the argument of an opponent. He succeeds ta his great office at a time of some difficulty. The Education Bill, which he has the duty of steering through the House of Commons, is a very contentious measure, raising many passions, and it is being fiercely fought line by line. Its fate is, therefore, a little uncertain, and its late involves to a certain ex- tent the fate and prestige of Mr Balfour. And the big mrjority behind him is heterogeneous as well as large and lacks a certain cohesion. But, on the other hand, the fact that the Premiership is in the House of Com- mons will give to the Government a consistent end nem- tinuous direction, which it badly wantsd woe° under Lord Salisbury—who, in adaition to the disadvantage of sitting in the House of Lords, was remote fre,m most mundane affairs. There was a painful evidence of drift—espe,lly I itely in the Government—owing to Lord Salisbury's positioa and peculiarities ; this will now be removed, and s o a greater vigour may be found in Ministerial directions—all of wnich seiil be to the advantage of Mr Balfour. A MAN HARD TO READ.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour is a maa hard ito read ; his general air is one of supreme indifference to everything in the world, and certainly he has never shown any particular grati- fication at his own splendid fortunes. Perhaps he was revealing his real feelings when he uttered the following words, Mr. Balfour once wound up an address by putting on the fairy wishing cap. "It I could wish for some eaittily gift •4 to be given by a fairly to an infant whom I loved, my first wish would be that under no circumstances should the child be under any temptation to become la politician. That would be my negative request. But I should ask the fairy to give the chila an omnivorous, universal, and insatiable curiosity to know everything that can be known—that he should look at learning and the acqlU- sition of knowledge as the great delight of his life ; tor, believe me,it is one of those pleasures which lasts longer than any other. It is an appetite which is not dulled by satiety, which is independent of changes and circum- stances, or of the love or dislike of your fellow-men. It is one, therefore, which every wise man shoald cultivate for himself."</p>
        <p>These are words, I know, the sincerity of which wi I be queried ; and yet I believe them to be sincere. The work of leader of the House of Commons, which Mr Balfour has now performed for seven years,is terribly laborious ; bow any man's health stands it is a marvel. Lassitude is often the portion of the working M.P.—how much more so must it lbe the portion of the man who has always to be about the building ; can never take a whole day of!; must always be on the qui vive ? Mr Ballour'd physique—like his character—has many unexpected things in it ; and one of these is a certain freshress and vigour which it displays at moments when one might expect the con- trary. Even when other men are fagged at the end of the sesson, his fine brown eyes look bright and alert ; be has always naaintained,too, the lightness of.bis figure —one of his horrors is that of getting fat—and, in short in spite of everything, be gives one the impression of the man who bas not in vain cultivated some of the arts of the athlete. But in spite of everything, I daresay he is often heartily and sincerely sick of the trade of Parliamentarian, and he often has said as much to his intimates. Probably ha his innermost heart he thinks he would have been better employed ins laboratory or a library than in the House of Commons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>REAL KITCHENER.</head>
        <p>THE REAL KITCHENER.</p>
        <p>Now that Lord Kitchener 1 has once more taken his place as the foremast soldier-figure in England (writes Mr T P O'Connor in this week's MA.?.") people are creating all kinds of imaginary Kiteheners—very different from the real man. This is always done ; popular heroes become legendary always and every- where, one of the first things to be said about him, of course, is that he is quite different .from the ordinary man ; free from all his weaknesses ; a machine, a being without emotion ; indifferent to applause ; loathing public notice ; above all things, a woman hater. This, however, is all nonsense. Lord Kitchener likes applause ; is keenly ambitious ; loves bearing himself discussed ; and is by no means indifferent to the smiles of the fair. He differs from other men in placing am- bition as the first of man's passions, and is strong enough never to allow anything else to take the supreme place in his life. His freedom from female ties is largely due to the fact that he spent so many of his years practically in the desert—far away from their in- fluence, and the very common and human fault of shy- ness probably accounts, when he got back to civilised life, for his failure ever to ask any of the many women in love with him to marry him. A good many people I daresay, will be surprised, if not shocked, to hear that so great a man as Kitchener can know anything of shyness ; and yet it is so. The other day, at Pretoria, when he was bolding the great and final review of his troops before they and he dis- persed to the far corners of the earth, one of his duties was to fix on to the dresses of the nurses the badges which they had earned by their brave conduct during the war. Close observers saw that Kitchener was shy, awkward, did the work sadly, and that even the deep red-brick of his complexion became visibly redder as he was doing this unaccustomed work. In fact, the great general blushed—the fit of shyness came over him, and he was unable to control it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>HIS EYE FOR DRAMATIC EFFECT.</head>
        <p>HIS EYE FOR DRAMATIC EFFECT.</p>
        <p>Similarly with the proposed hatred of Kitchener for public applause and popular demonstration. As is pointed out by even his admirers, there is no man who has shown on so many occusions a splendid insight into ' dramatic situation. There has never been one of his 1 great events wbicti has not been so stage-managed as to produce the greatest possible dramatic effect. When he was advancing on Omdurman for instance, he cut the wire between Xgypt and home for several days—they were the last day) that still intervened between him and the crowning victory. This mysterious and sinister veil, which all at once was drawn between England and her frees, heightened the mystery,the suspense,the passionate interest in the last hours of the great struggle and when Kitchener permitted the veil to be lifted again it was to :display to a people almost maddened with suspense the bloody field on which lay crushed for ever the broken and scattered forces of the Mandi. And again when this victory was completed and when Kitchener stood in the middle of the town where Gordon had died, surely it was a very high and keen sense of the dramatic which impelled Kitchener to hold a funeral service to Gordon's memory. Here, indeed, in sober solemn unexceptionable fashion, was the final triumph ' of civilization over barbarism heralded sod commemor- ated to all the world. The thanksgiving service at Pretoria,again,was stage- managed with wonderful skill ; and, what is more, with wonderful good taste and good feeling. lam no judge of Kitchener as a general; and I believe that it is not as a general in the usual sense of the word that he is ' esteemed most highly by those who know him best. In- deed, it is said that on the battlefield he is not at his best, but at his worst, and that the iron self-will he abows elsewhere disappears there ; and at Paardeberg be was responsible for one of the least successful and most bloody of the frontal attacks of the whole war. It is in the vast preparations on the march ; in the careful and ceaseless adaptation of means to ends ; in the spirit of incessant activity which his own energy conveys as 1 an infection ; in his stern command over men ; it is in the2e things that the greatness of Kitchener as a soldier lies, and not in the mysteries of strategy or tactics. AS A DIPLOMATIST, - -</p>
        <p>But there is one thing which Kitchen" er undoubtedly in, and that is a great and inspired diplomatist. He must have tremendous powers of adaptation and observation. Before he became the great Egyptian General, he had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS. ---- McClew—Bate.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. ---- McClew—Bate.</p>
        <p>The marriage took place at St Albaza's Roman Catholic Church, Warrington, on Thursday afternoon of Miss Ada McClew, youngest daughter of Mr and Mrs James McClew, llorsemarket-street, and Mr W Bate, son of Mr and Mrs Peter Bate, 23, 1131leston-street. The event aroused:considerable interest and a large congregation , assembled. The officiating priest was the Rev ,Father Gibbons: The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in a dress of cream brocaded silk, trimmed with chiffon and orange blossom. She wore a white hat trimmed with chiffon and ostrich plumes, and carried a lovely shower bouquet of lilies, the gift of ,the bridegroom. She was attended by two bridesmaids—Miss M Gittens and Miss Winnie Brooks. Mias Gittens wore a dress of ple helio- trope, trimmed with eream lace, and a white bat trimmed with black velvet. Miss Brooks was attired in a pale blue dress, trimmed with black velvet, and a white bat. Both bridesmaids carried bouquets of marguerites and wore gold brooches, the gilts of the bridegroom. Mr J M Bate discharged the duties of best man. Mr J B Caldwell ably presided at the organ and played several appropriate piices, including Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." At the conclusion of the ceremony the bridal party pro- ceeded to Mr Birtles's Studio Sankey-street,to be photo- graphed, and the wedding breakfast was afterwaros served at the Winmarleigh Cafe. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for Blackpool, where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travelling nress was of Royal blue trimmed with cream, with hat to match.. The following is a list of the presents ;</p>
        <p>Bride to bridegroom, gold links awl studs Bridegroom to bride, gold bangle Mr McClew, cheque Mrs McClew, drawing room carpet &amp;c, Mr P Bate sen., couch Mrs Bate, eider down quilt to Mrs Spencer, tea service, toilet ware, ornaments &amp;c, Mr Spencer, cooking utensils Mr and Mrs P Bate jun., parlour brasses Mr J M Bate, wringing machine Mr B Eats dinner service</p>
        <p>Miss M Gittens. cutlery</p>
        <p>Misses 13 and I' Gitten's, silver spoons Miss Byrne, table cloth Mr and Mrs T C Bro ,ks, heartbrug Miss Brooks,door mat</p>
        <p>Mr and Mrs W Brooks. kitchen furniture</p>
        <p>Mr JohnHeeketh drawing-room clock</p>
        <p>Mr Wicks kitchen fire Irons</p>
        <p>Employee; of Mr John Hesketh, kitchen clock Messrs J. and Arthur Plcton, case of silver cutlery Mr and Mrs George Morrell. set of silver carvers St Alban's Church choir, silver cruet Mr and Mrs F Short, cheese dish Mr Roylance, coal boa MrA lgoylence, placgues Mrs Broughton, copper kettle Mrs Flood, brass ornaments, am. Rev Father Whittle, photograph Mrs Leslie, brass stool Mr and Mrs J. Hill, wicker chair Mr J. Broa lbent and Miss Turner, walnut table Mrs Mercer, bed spread Mrs Lester and Miss Broughton, fish dish and glass</p>
        <p>jug Mr W Knight, set of brushes Mr Carter, glass jugs Mr Breward, pickle jar Mr W H J Jenkinsibreakfast service Messrs Hignett, skin rug 3.</p>
        <p>Marriage of Miss Speakman at Frodsham. The marriage of Mies Bertha Speakman, younger daughter of the late Mr Philip Speakman, J.P., C.A., of Fox Hill' Nethertoo, Frodsham, and Liverpool, and the Rev A JJamesen, son of the late Mr J Jameson, of Hey- wood Hall, Laneaebire, and of Arnatimaite, Cumber- hnd, was so'emnised at Overton Church. Frodsham,on Thursday. The tunctiun was a very fashionable one. The guests numbered over one hundred. The bride's family is exeeedingly well known in Cheshire and Lancashire, and the bridegroom, who spent several years in Runcorn, as a curate-in-charge of Weston and St Michael's Churches, r. cently resigned the living of St Augustine's Church, Stockport,which he had held for rune years. Tie bride, who was given away by her b other, MreEaton Speakman, was attired in a dress of white silk, covered with d'Alencon laoe, cod wore a white tulle veil, and a spray of orange blossora. She_ carried an exquisite bouquet of white roses and lilies. The bridesmaids were Miss Arnold, Miss Jameson, Miss , D Jameson, Miss K Douatil, and Miss G Speakman. They were attended by Master Philip Donstil. Mr E M Jameson was best man. The ceremony was performed by the Rev fl B Btogg, vicar assisted by Rev Canon Maitlaod Wood, vicar of Runcorn, end the Rev G E Warlow, viear of Stoekington. Nuneaton. A reception was subsequently held at Fox Hill. During the after- noon, Mr and Mrs Jameson departed for Scotland .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TRE OLD BURYING GROUND AT BT. HELENS. During Tuesday night four coffins were removed from the open space in Bridge-street, S. Helens, and re- interred at the cemetery. As previously reported, the workmen employed by Mr Fred BrOWD, the contractor of the new hotel which is to be built on the south side of Bridge-street, dug up several old coffins in excavat- ing for the walls at the rear of the building. It appears that the ground in which the coffins were found formed the site of the vestry of the old Wesleyan Chapel. When the vestry and the chapel were demolished some years ago it was evidently forgotton that the vestry had been built on part of the old graveyard attached to the chapel and no arrangements were made for the removal of the remains under the flooring.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>RAILWAY MISHAP AT ST. lIPLENS.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY MISHAP AT ST. lIPLENS.</p>
        <p>A somewhat alarming incident happened on the London and North-Western Railway Company's Raven- head branch at St. Helens on Wednesday afternoon. The Ravenhead branch is a most important goods line, which laps Pilkington's extensive glassworks, several chemical works, glass buttle works, and collieries. It appears that a train of empty coal waggons was going along past the end of Canal-street at the point where the canal has been drawn of! and partly filled up, when it was run into by the engine of a goods train I coming down the incline from Messrs. Nuttall's bottle- works. The result was that four waggons were knocked through a wall into the canal basin. The wall was carried away for a distance of abnut thirty yards. The waggons were allowed to remain where they fell until evening, when a breakdown gang !rom Peasley Crose, under Mr. Davies. !Mad the unbroken oaes on to the line again, and patched up me that had its wheels knocked off.</p>
        <p>At thelEarlestowncPolice Station pn Saturday morning before Mr J W Emmett,John Hall, a tramp, was charged with being drunk n Earlestown on Friday, and in default ot paying a fine of 23 6d and costs was sent to gaol for 7 days. At Newton on Monday, before Dr Dowling and Mr J Unsworth, James Donohue of Haydock, who appeared with I his head well ornamented with sticking plaster, was charged with being drunk in Earlestown on Saturday night. 1' C Mellor stated that the prisoner was drunk and creating a disturbance. He was unable to give an account of himself and he took him to the Police Station. Inspector Morgan said the prisoner had been out of the neighbourhlod for four years on account of a summons taken out against him for an old offence. A fine of 5s and Costs was inflicted. John W Hannon for being drunk at Farlestown on Saturday night was fined 2s 64 and costs.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon a fire broke out in a signal-boz on the Midland Railway at Swinton, and spread ao quickly that the signalman had to jump from the upper window to escape. Special arrangements had to be quickly made to reinstate traffic on the main line. It is supposed that sparks from a passing engine caused the petroleum stores underneath the signal-boa to ignite.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPIELT Or course, some achieve greatness, but very few succeed in convincing all their acquaintances that it isn't pure chance. A MAN never fully realises the joys of home until he sits at his own table and criticises his wife's cooking. "WHAT do you think of this new trick of photo- graphing sound waves?" "It ought to aid in securing speaking likenesses." THE YOUNG SWEET THING: "I wonder how it is there is no good English equivalent for fiance?" The Savage Bachelor: "How about idiot '?" VIOLA: "I've just finished a unique sofa-pillow. It's stuffed with old love letters. Cody: "In- deed ! How very nice and soft it must be." "THAT hard-boiled egg gave me a headache." "You shouldn't eat hard-boiled eggs." "I didn't eat it. A fellow hit me with it behind the ear."</p>
        <p>TEACHER (suspiciously) : "Who wrote your com- position, Johnny ? " ,Johnny : "My father." Teacher : " What, all of it? ' Johnny: " No'm ; I helped him." COUNTRYMAN (to street musician) : "What was that you were just playing ? " "That was The Death of Nelson.'" "Ay, man, ye have given him an awfu' death." _</p>
        <p>HE KNENV. —Teacher : "If you are polite and kind to young comrades, what will be the result ?" Bully Jones : "They'll know they can lick you." THE ARTISTIC TOLISLE.—" Oh, May, how did you get your hair arranged so beautifully ?" "I did it up carefully and then played two games of basket-ball."</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL SuBuRBANITEs.—Towne : "Do you make your cook pay for what she breaks ?" Sub- bibs (in amazement) : "Make her pay? I should say not! Why, every month, besides paying her salary, we reward her liberally for what she didn't break !"</p>
        <p>"LOOK here, ain't ye got any better sense than to be smokin' while we're handlin' these kegs o' powder? Don't ye know there was an explosion last week that blowed up a dozen men?" "Faith, that cud never happen here." "Why not?" " Bekase there's only two of us workin' here." PENURIOUS PHILANTHROPIST (handing halfpenny to small boy who has been holding his horse for about an hour) : "There, my lad there's some- thing for you." Smail Boy: "Can I keep the change, mister?" MR. NUWED (gloomily): "My salary has been cut down ten per cent." Mrs. Nuwed (cheerily) : "Oh, don't worry about that, dear! Silk, Ribbons and Co. are advertising perfectly lovely things at a reduction of twenty per cent." A scuoor, inspector in the North of Ireland was once examining a geography class, and asked the question: "What is a lake?" He was much amused when a little fellow, evidently a true gem of the Emerald Isle, answered: "It's a hole in a can, sir." " TIIAT young Softleigh asked me for your hand to-day." "And what did you say to him, papa, dear ? ' "I told him your mother needed both in the washing-tub, but I compromised with him by giving him my foot." CLERGYMAN preaching in country church for the first time, delighted to find the congregation very ttentive, and told the verger so after the service. " Lor' bless you, sir, we was all looking for you to disappear." "Why, what do you mean?" "Well, sir, you see, the pulpit is rotten, and hasn't been preached in this year or more."</p>
        <p>GOOD MAN: "Do you know what becomes of little boys that use bad words when they are play- ing marbles?" Bad boy: " Yes ! They grows up an' plays golf." "Josnrums is so thoughtful." "In what way ? " "She has had the rustle of her silk petticoat taken by phonograph, for fear she will forget it when it is out of fashion." " HORACE," she said, chidingly, "why don't you tall me you love me ?" Seizing a scrap of paper, the young man wrote on it " How can I, darling, unless you remove your arm from round my neck!"</p>
        <p>ENCiOURAG.EALENT.—Young Author: "What did you think of that story of mina? " Mrs. Miffkins : "It was the best one you have ever written—at least 500 words shorter than any of the others, I should say." JOHNNY "Do they have elephants in Asia ? " Papa "Oh, yes." Johnny: "Do they hays circuses in Asia?" Papa " Isf•o-o, I think not." Johnny: "Well, what's the use of having ele- phants if they don't have circuses ?" "WHICH would you rather do, fight or apolo- gise ? " "I'd rather meet him with pistols," answered the Parisian journalist. "If Igo near enough to him to apologise, we might suddenly lose our tempers and do some damage." GENTL&amp;MAN (to house agent) : "The great dis- advantage is that the house is so damp. House Agent: Disadvantage, sir? Advantage, I call it. In case of fire it wouldn't be so likely to burn."</p>
        <p>PRUDENT Boys.—" They say that a great many little boys hook green apples," said Johnny's uncle. "I hope you do not engage in such things." "No, indeed, replied Johnny. "All us boys has made a bargain that we'll wait and give the apples a chance to get ripe." "DIEM chops was good, mum," said the tramp. "But I allus likes to finish a meal with sweets. Yer ain't got any cakes, have yer ?" "I have cakes of soap." "Thank yer, mum. But I never touch 'em." "JANE. did you break the water-bottle?" "Yes, m'm ; but,-fortunately, I broke it into only three pieces." " Fortunately ! How's that fortu- nate ? "Oh, m'm, when a thing breaks into many pieces it is hard to pick them all up !"</p>
        <p>_ "WHEN I am telling a man a story I stop short if I see a peculiar gleam in his eye." "Does it mean that he has heard it before?" "No, it means that he isn't listening because he is thinking up the one he intends to tell me." MR. JINICS (to landlady) : "What kind of a duck did you say this was, Mrs. Dinkly ?" Landlady: "I didn't say. I simply ordered a duck from the butcher's." Mr. Jinks (struggling with a second joint) : "I think he has sent you a decoy duck." Mx. PIILMAN (about to propose) : "Miss San- ford, I am going to say what I wanted to say an hour ago. Can you not guess from my eyes what it is ! ' Miss Santord ; "Do you mean "good night ; " You look sleepy."</p>
        <p>oaAtmSeTal.,lailEtt.hroNusghbohy had is mothaelrwiaiayds . declinedurged di to t upon n him as a strengthening diet. Suddenly he sur- prised her one morning by eating a liberal plateful and calling for " more. ' When she asked for an explanation he replied, "I am bound to eat oat- meal till I get strong enough to whip Georgy Scott." MR. HOUSEHUN'rER (to house agent) : "Mrs. Househunter will be in to-day and I want you to tell her that the house we've been looking at is taken." " Agent : " Why—er—but it isn't." Mt. Househunter : " Well, it will be, for I'm taking it now. My wife can't make up her mind, but she'd want it as soon as she thinks she can't have it." ORNAMENTAL, HUT NOT USE', EL. " They say that old Gotrox is barely able to write his name.' "Now, that shows the injustice of things. Hers I could write my name to a cheque with the greatest ease, but what's the use ? " THE SAME. —Teacher : "The sentence My father had money' is in the past tense. Now, Mary, what tense would you be speaking in if you said My father has money ?' " Little Mary: "Oh, that would be pretense." HER DILEMMA.—The Daughter: "George is beginning to stay late in town, and he claims that it is owing to business." Her Mother : " Well?" The Daughter: "V -well, I don't know whether I should begin to economise or to be extra extrava- gant and dress myself in more bewitching evening gowns." STRATEGIC. —Mrs. Scrooge: "I am writing to ask the Browns to meet the Jonescs here at dinner, and to the Joneses to meet the Browns. We owe them both an invitation, you know." Mr. Scrooge: "But I've heard they've just quarrelled and don't speak." Mrs. Scrooge: "I know that. They'll refuse, and we needn't have a dinner party at all." INcRtmiNAsoRY EVIDENCE.—" Johnny, said dui little fellow's mamma, "I want to give you a piecs of cake, but I can't find the key to the pantry.' "That's all right, mamma," replied bright ROA Johnny. "I know how to get in without a key.' "That's all I want to know," she said, as siss reached for the allwer. There was a large amount of important business on the agenda for Tuesday night's meeting of the Colwyn Bay District Council, but the meeting was brought to an abrupt close by a dramatic incident. Dr Breaks brought ups ratepayers' petition, and declined to stop speaking when called upon by the chairmao. Thereupon the chairman, Mr Thomas Parry, resigned his position and his seat on the Council, handing to the Clerk the statu- tory fine of £5 and walking out of the room. The shortest people in Earope are the Laplanders. The beght of the men averages 4ft llins, ana the Leight of the women 2 ins. less.</p>
      </div>
