      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-07-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRESENTATION OF A HORSE AMBULANCE TO NEWTON-INMAKERFIELD.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF A HORSE AMBULANCE TO NEWTON-IN- MAKERFIELD.</p>
        <p>The horse ambulance, the provision of which the police of the Newton-in-Makerfield district, with the assistance of the tradesmen,undertook a short time ago, was formally handed over to the Newton-in-Idakerfietd Urban District Council on Monday afternoon. The rapid manner in which the fund for this purpose was accumulated was a matter of genuine surprise to every- body. I was only two or three weeks after the police had embarked on the project that they announced that the fund was closed. The employers of labour and the inhabitants generally had been liberal contributors to thelfund,whichireceived substantial augmentation by the proceeds of the Police and Tradesmen's Football Match. Among the police who had been most energetic in pro- moting the fund were Superintendent Cocker Inspector Morgan, and Sergeant Lings, while among ;he trades- men, Messrs T Siddeiey, J Fitzgerald, P Naylor and others had rendered very valuable assistance. It was originally intended to have presented the ambulance on Coronation Day, but unfortunately a fire broke out at the works and the vehicle which was nearly ready for delivery, was partially destroyed. A delay of a fortnight was thereby caused. There were present at the ceremony on Monday after- noon, Colonel aloorson, chief constable of the county Colonel J D Murray, of Wigan; the Rev T R Matthews (vicar of Earlestown), the Rev J C Matthews, Superin- tendent Cocker, Inspector Morgan, Councillors W Collingwood (chairman), Dr Patterson, Dr Latbano, Simpson, N Siddeley, E T Evans, and J Fitzgerald, Mr Cole (clerk to the Council), Dr Handyside, Messrs 13 5 Longton, V illiam Beatty and others. The ambulance was wheeled on the open space adjoining the Town Hall, and a large crowd bad gathered to witness the presentation. Before the proceedings commenced Us Chief Constable, the Council and other gentlemen present were photographed by Mr Unsworth, The Chief Constable made the presentation, and Mr Collingwood (chairman of the Council)acknowledged the gift:</p>
        <p>The police then gave a demonstration of the uses of the ambulance carriage, aftor which it was wheeled on the market square and inspected by a large number of people. The carriage was constructed by Messrs Wilson and Stcckall, of Bury, and is made to carry two injured persons in a recumbent position at one time. The fol- lowing is the description .—The great feature which has commanded so much success is the easy provision for ingress and egress, being hung so low on a cranked mail patent axle (of exceptional quality and durability) that an injured person can be placed in position with the greatest possible ease ; and owing to a particular mode of adaptation of the springs on patent tubber hall-bear- ings any jolting or uncomfortable motion is reduced to a minimum,lthe wheels being rubber tyred. It contains two stretchers, (1) which is slung and runs in on a folding frame, (2) the bottom stretcher is furnished with 12-in. rubber-tyred wheels, elliptic springs and axle (these are easily detachable), and can be used to run a patient along the floor of any works or mill, and can be carried up and down stairs ; it runs in the body on grooves on the bottom, after being placed on a portable tramway which is fitted to the back of the body ; this neatly folds up, and is carried with the stretcher, and is always ready. This stretclur is also fitted with an ingenious arrangement for raising the patient when descending a steep incline. Both stretchers are covered with stout sail canvas and movatle rubbers sheeting, also two pure rubber pillows are supplied, two outside and one inside lamps, brake, shafts, plate glass embossed side windows, body varnished self- coloured wood, under works and wheels painted and picked out tocchoice, and very highly finished. In addi- tion the interior is fitted with an automatic box contain- ing different widths of bandages, elints of all descrip- tions, 30. Extra ventilation is provided by a fan. The rubber tyres are of special quality, costing about £25. A brass plate bears the following inscription This ambulance carriage WBS presented to the urban District Council by the police of Newton-in-Makerfield, provided from the funds raised by a football match, together with subscriptions from the employers of labour and the general public, June, 1902.—J. M. COCKER, superinten- dent."</p>
        <p>The windows were specially designed by Superin- tendant Cocker, two showing the arms of the district and the other two the Red Cross with the words " St. John Ambulance, Coronation, June, 1902."</p>
        <p>CO-OPIERATITE SOCIETY, EARLESTOWN. On Saturday afternoon the Co-operative Society entertained the children of the members to a field day and gala on the ground of the Earlestown Foot- ball club. Something like 2000, children were present, and the day being delightfully tine, they enjoyed themselves to the top of their bent. A large marquee was erected on the field and from this the children were supplied with refreshments free. A large number of the parents were also present and entered into the spirit of the thing as heartily as the children. A Punch and Judy show afforded plenty of amusement, and a ventrilcquial entertain- ment proved specially attractive to all. Sports such as at races, skipping rope race, shuttlecock com- petition, sack race, hoop race, egg and spoon race, tVel , were also provided for the boys and girls and were carried through with much spirit, the entries for the various events being very numerous. The Haydock brass band discoursed music during the afternoon, and in the evening played for dancing. The whole of the arrangements were carried out by the committee.</p>
