        <p>Lawn Tennis.—Lancashire v. Yorkshire. • These counties met on the prettily-situated ground of the Cheetharn Hill Lawn Tennis Club Manchester, on Saturday, in fine weather. The courts were in excellent condition, and a fair num- ber of spectators followed the game with much interest. Neither team was quite at its full strength. Lancashire were without C E Hunter and L Mason, and Ycrkshire without C W Wade WI E Fletcher. Lancashire won by 7 matches to 4 (1 drawn), 21 sets to 14. and 186 games to 159. Warrington Volunteer Encampment. To-day (Saturday) the Warrington Volunteers will go into brigade camp on Salisbury Plain for a fortnight, The Mounted Infantry and Cyclist Com- panies, the machine gun detachment and a detach- ment from other companies, will fall in at nine a.m., under the officer commanding the battalion, to proceed by an early special train, which will con- vey officers' chargers and; baggage, and men's kits. Th• officers of the Mounted Infantry and Cyclist companies will go by this train. The remainder of the battalion will fall in at the Drill Hall at 630 ' p:m. under the command of Major J D Buckton, to entrain at 7 p.m.—At 11 o'clock on Tuesday night the advance party paraded at the Drill Hall in marching order. under the command of Captain B Fairclough, and entrained for Salisbury Plain shortly afterwards. The party consisted of Quarter- master and Hon. Lieutenant A F G Potter, Quarter- master-Sergeant J Willcocks, one sergeant, and 211 rank and file, and a transport detachment of one sergeant and nine men. TRY ALLEN'S FOOT-EASE A powder to be shaken Into the shoes. Vour feet feel swollen, nervous, and damp, and get tired eerily. If you flaVe aching feet, In Allen's Foot.Eas It tests the leet isnd Makes new or tight boots easy. Cures hot, swollen, sweating feet, blisters and callous spots. Relieves corns and bunions of all pain, and gives rent and romto-t'Fry ft Of all Chemists and Stores. Prlce is. Ild. Acce't ashil,'sti 1011 port 'fee f